WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.946
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.320
MODERATOR: I believe too
few serious scholars are

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.740
studying the social
impact of computers,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.010
the way they affect us
as individuals in groups

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:27.690
and in society at large,
as we learn, as we work,

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.210
and even as we play.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.500
Now, that's strange to me.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.530
Because here we have the
most powerful tool mankind

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.190
has ever known.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:40.200
And we've hardly begun to
analyze how it is affecting us,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.350
let alone how it is
shaping us and how

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.350
we can shape it so
that we get what

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.670
we want in the way of affects.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.620
Lee Sproull is one of
those few individuals who

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:55.440
has devoted her entire
professional life

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.960
to this all-important area.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:02.850
After 15 years as a professor
of social sciences at CMU,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.620
she's now a professor
of Management

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.540
at Boston University.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.750
And she's published seven
books and many research papers

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:10.920
in this area.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.660
And it's a special
pleasure for me

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.040
to have someone address this key
topic, someone who has devoted

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.280
her entire life to it.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:19.920
Thank you, Lee.

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.272
SPROULL: I feel so old.

00:01:27.272 --> 00:01:28.688
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.760
I'm not a computer
science pioneer.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.660
I hope that was at least
implicit in what Andy said.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.235
Although some of my best
friends certainly are.

00:01:41.235 --> 00:01:43.660
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.210
For the past 25
years, I have been

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:49.540
pleased and privileged
to be an observer

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:54.970
of the community making
parts of Bush's vision real.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.650
When Doug was talking yesterday
about how human systems

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.550
and computer tools need
to evolve together,

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:08.620
I was actually reminded of
one of my early experiences

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:15.190
as a user of this
technology in 1977.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.440
I'm trained as a sociologist.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:20.410
My doctoral dissertation
had absolutely nothing

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.120
to do with computing.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.460
But I typed it on an Alto
computer at Xerox PARC.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.920
I printed the final
document on EARS.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.820
And I think there may be a
few people in this room who

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.230
know about EARS, one of the
earliest laser printers.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.560
I collected the signatures
on the signature page

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.180
of my dissertation.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.790
I took my dissertation
to the registrar's office

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:49.750
at Stanford to be
certified, bound, blessed,

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:52.210
whatever it is that
happens to these things.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:55.480
Now, this was in the days when
the production requirements

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.560
for dissertations were
extremely stringent.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.810
The requirements
specified a certain kind

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:06.370
of rag paper, black carbon
ribbon, no erasures.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:10.870
So I handed in my thesis,
my laser-printed thesis.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.300
The dragon lady in
the registrar's office

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.600
flipped through the pages, ran
her fingers over the words,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.240
fixed me with a steely eye, and
said, you can't turn this in.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.944
It isn't the original.

00:03:24.944 --> 00:03:26.812
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.210
I was highly evolved.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:34.990
My computing tools were
highly evolved for that time.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.320
But the social system in
which I was doing my work

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.110
had a ways to go.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.000
Still true today.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.860
I'm delighted to be here to
celebrate the technology vision

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.950
that we are all
pleased to honor.

00:03:51.950 --> 00:03:53.950
Actually though, mostly
what I want to do

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:58.390
is talk about the human vision
or models of human beings

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:02.200
that are associated with
Bush's technology vision,

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:06.490
and also other visions that
we're hearing about today.

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.460
Every technology vision
that really captures

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:12.520
people's imagination
is accompanied

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:18.160
by a view of human beings using
the technology, who they are,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.700
what they do, how their
lives are changed,

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:25.330
improved, we hope,
by the technology.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:28.900
The human vision
that is associated

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.770
with the memex with
Bush's paper is a view

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.750
of a person working alone.

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:37.990
Could I have the
first slide, please?

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:42.170
Working alone, not doing
housework or manual labor,

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.560
but doing intellectual work.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.380
The technology, of
course, is envisioned

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:52.270
to support that solo work,
to make it more productive,

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.640
to make it even more creative.

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:59.500
Now, in Bush's paper, we have
evoked the view of the scholar,

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.770
seated at a wonderful desk,
interacting with the world

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:07.360
scientific literature, alone.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.580
Licklider, in his 1960 paper,
"Man, Machine, Symbiosis,"

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.370
evokes a view of the
scientist or an engineer

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:18.520
solving problems more
productively with a machine

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.760
doing the clerical and
mechanical activities.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.350
The scientist or engineer
is alone with the machine.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:31.060
Alan Kay evokes a view of a
person of any age, especially

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.000
a child, interacting
with a dynamic medium

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.220
for creative
thought, a Dynabook,

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.910
to work and play more
productively and creatively,

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.050
alone.

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:44.170
Now, of course, we have to note
that Doug Engelbart has always

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.750
been the exception
to this fairly

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:49.210
common view of the
human being associated

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:50.770
with the personal computer.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:55.000
As early as 1968, of course, he
was describing computer support

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.450
for people working together,
sharing files, working

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:00.820
in real-time meetings.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:04.190
This technology vision of
personal computing, of course,

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:08.180
is extraordinarily important,
extraordinarily worthwhile.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.080
There's a whole lot
more to be done,

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:11.630
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:15.820
But science, and
Bush, after all,

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:19.870
was speaking about the
scientific community.

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.300
Science and most
other productive work

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.580
in modern society is
inherently a social enterprise.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.080
Could I have the
next slide, please?

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:34.230
Lick was an empiricist, and
therefore dear to my heart.

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:38.790
He reported real data
in his 1960 paper.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:45.000
He reported, in fact, that
85% of his thinking time

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:49.080
was devoted to clerical
or mechanical activities--

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.670
searching, calculating,
plotting, transforming.

00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:56.670
He used his data
on human behavior--

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.700
data on himself, but still,
data on human behavior--

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.600
to justify his
technology vision.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.760
What he didn't
point out though, is

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:10.620
that while 85% of
his thinking time

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:13.710
was devoted to clerical
or mechanical activities,

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:19.260
probably only 15% of his
day was devoted to thinking.

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:22.620
The rest was probably
spent in meetings.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:26.910
You've been there?

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:29.770
Face-to-face interaction
with other human beings--

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:32.430
I don't mean only formal
meetings, conferences,

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:33.090
symposia.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:36.530
Hallway conversations
count, as well.

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:38.520
There have been many,
many time allocation

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.730
studies of people working in
a variety of fields, including

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:44.680
scientists-- bench scientists,
technical supervisors

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:48.570
and managers, people across
all of these studies, all

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:52.980
of this kind of work--
spend between 50% to 85%

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.990
of their day in meetings.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:58.050
And in addition to that,
they spend some fraction

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:03.240
of their alone time
recovering from meetings.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.150
I mean during
followup work, but I

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:08.370
guess there's other
kinds of recovery, too.

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:12.450
Let me give you just some
more evidence and reminders

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.520
of something that we
all know very well,

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:18.942
that science is a
social enterprise,

00:08:18.942 --> 00:08:24.870
one indicator of growth
in team size over time.

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:28.680
It took two people to
build the first airplane.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:37.169
It took 7,302 people to
build the Boeing 777.

00:08:37.169 --> 00:08:40.350
Author lists for
papers and journals

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.419
have certainly been
growing over the years.

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:45.450
In the past 40 years,
the mean number

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:49.230
of authors for papers published
in scientific journals

00:08:49.230 --> 00:08:52.410
across all disciplines
has doubled.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:56.070
The mean number of
authors per paper in 1955

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:02.040
was 1.8, in 1994 was 3.5.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:07.290
Last year, in '94, there
were more than 700 papers

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:12.600
published in scientific
journals that had more than 50,

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:17.730
5-0, authors listed
on the title page.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:21.540
The, I think, record
so far, last year,

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:27.110
the two papers announcing the
discovery of the top quark

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:34.200
last year, each listed over
400 authors on the title page.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.070
Now, even in academic
computer science--

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:41.520
computer science is not known
as a big science field--

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:46.930
the mean size of work
teams is increasing.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.880
If you look at the
proceedings of SIGGRAPH,

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.100
the computer
graphics conference,

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:58.680
the mean number of authors
per paper at SigGraph in 1975

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.290
was 1.7.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:06.330
In '94, it was 2.4.

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:12.570
If you look at the ACM
Computer Chess Championship,

00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.580
the mean number of
people associated

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:19.350
with the winning program
from '74 to '84 was two.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.020
The mean number associated
with the winning program

00:10:22.020 --> 00:10:26.340
from '85 to '94 was 3.8.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:30.750
These are fairly formal
indicators of the fact

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:34.680
that science is not
a solo enterprise.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:36.420
Here's a livelier one.

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:38.760
This is from Lewis
Thomas's description

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.070
of the marine
biological laboratory

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:44.100
at Woods Hole, a
human institution

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:50.010
that, as Lewis Thomas says, is
possessed of a life of its own,

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:53.760
with an extraordinary influence
on the growth and development

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.340
of biologic science.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.290
Lewis Thomas suggests
that, to learn more

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:00.450
about the marine
biological laboratory,

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:03.300
you might begin at Stony Beach.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:06.390
He says, "It's the
most minor of beaches,

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.610
hardly big enough
for a committee,

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.830
but close enough
to the laboratories

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:12.990
so that the investigators
can walk down

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:16.950
for a sandwich lunch with their
children on sunny weekdays.

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:21.480
On weekends in hot
midsummer, it is so crowded

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:23.580
that one must pick
one's way on tiptoe

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:25.560
to find a hunching place.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.710
But there is always a lot
of standing up anyway.

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:33.420
Biologists seem to prefer
standing on beaches talking

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:36.330
at each other,
gesturing to indicate

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:40.350
the way things are assembled,
bending down to draw diagrams

00:11:40.350 --> 00:11:41.640
in the sand.

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:44.550
By the end of the day,
the sand is criss-crossed

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.920
with a mesh of
ordinance, abscesses,

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:50.460
curves to account for
everything in nature.

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:53.310
You can hear the sound from
the beach at a distance

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:55.140
before you see the people.

00:11:55.140 --> 00:12:00.120
It is that most extraordinary
noise, half shout, half song,

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.820
made by confluent,
simultaneously

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:08.960
raised human voices explaining
things to each other."

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:13.700
Now that's what
science is about.

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:16.100
Personal computing,
fortunately, has

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:19.970
evolved to allow
interpersonal communication,

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:23.510
to allow people to explain
things to each other,

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:25.940
to talk with each
other using computers.

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:29.480
Could I have the
next slide, please.

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.460
You might just do a
little thought experiment.

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:34.370
What would the
world be like today

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:37.610
if there were 100 million
personal computers

00:12:37.610 --> 00:12:41.000
and no interactive
communication,

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.510
no ability to contact
people or create and sustain

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:48.380
human relationships with other
people through these machines?

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.320
Actually, I'm not sure we
would be having this meeting.

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:53.540
And I don't mean just,
we wouldn't have gotten

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:55.310
the logistics of it organized.

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:59.930
I'm not so sure we would
be here to celebrate

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:04.310
the way the vision has
come to be played out.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:06.950
Of course, we shouldn't
feel sorry for Bush

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:10.310
that he didn't predict
the power or importance

00:13:10.310 --> 00:13:13.490
of interpersonal
communication in the context

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:15.780
of his technology vision.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:18.020
He's in good company.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.350
Not only the history of
personal computing, but even

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:24.440
the history of
computer communications

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:27.800
features technologists
who underestimated

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:32.180
the lure and importance of
human communication using

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:34.070
this technology.

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:38.180
Everyone in this room is
familiar with the ARPANET

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:38.973
and email story.

00:13:38.973 --> 00:13:40.640
In fact, some of the
people in this room

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:46.100
were the heroes or villains
or something in that story.

00:13:46.100 --> 00:13:47.900
But the conventional
wisdom, anyway,

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:51.890
has it that the ARPANET was
conceived to link computers.

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:54.500
And of course, we all know
it turned out to link people,

00:13:54.500 --> 00:13:58.610
too, initially through
email, distribution lists,

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:01.340
and bulletin boards
populated by members

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:04.400
of the same intellectual
community, who

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:07.310
were housed in a small
number of universities,

00:14:07.310 --> 00:14:11.150
industrial labs, a
fairly homogeneous bunch

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:12.860
of folks, truth to tell.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:17.420
Although they might not be
pleased to hear me say that.

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:21.380
More recently, as internet
access has soared,

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:23.930
the human links have been
formed through mechanisms,

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:27.290
like newsgroups or
usenet groups, that

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:30.110
are populated by
people who may have

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:33.260
absolutely nothing
in common with one

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:38.180
another except for internet
access and a shared interest

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.490
in the topic of the
particular group.

00:14:43.010 --> 00:14:45.290
Commercial electronic
services providers,

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:48.290
like CompuServe
and Prodigy, also

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:51.680
didn't anticipate the importance
of the human communication

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:55.650
services, like
email, chat rooms.

00:14:55.650 --> 00:15:01.040
Now, scientists certainly don't
just process information alone

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:03.110
in their offices.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:06.410
Moreover, they don't
just communicate either,

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:07.670
generically.

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.910
They do these things
within social contexts.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:15.020
That is, through multiple
ongoing relationships

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:18.770
with other people
and organizations.

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:27.980
I must say, I have been struck
by the serious observation made

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:33.170
by all the speakers thus far
that people are important

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:35.540
and social issues are important.

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:39.950
And obviously, I'm glad to
hear the speakers say that.

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:47.930
But there's a sort of
funny gap between how

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:51.290
the previous speakers talk about
people-- by which they mean

00:15:51.290 --> 00:15:54.860
individual human beings
who speak into a microphone

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:57.080
or type with fingers
on keys or whatever--

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:02.840
and society, which is big
forces that are good or evil

00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:04.610
or something, but they're huge.

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:10.350
There's a very large middle
ground that's very important.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:13.010
And it's the middle ground where
most of the productive work

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:18.620
gets done in society, through
multiple ongoing relationships

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:21.980
with people in organizations.

00:16:21.980 --> 00:16:25.020
Could I have the next one?

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:32.910
Each relationship, every one of
them, is structured by roles.

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:35.010
Roles have formal
responsibilities

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:37.380
and obligations
associated with them,

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:41.050
norms, accepted
rules for behavior.

00:16:41.050 --> 00:16:46.230
Each relationship is shaped
through reciprocated action

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.690
of people working together.

00:16:48.690 --> 00:16:52.620
Each relationship is
sustained through trust--

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:55.680
trust, by the way, doesn't
have to mean liking--

00:16:55.680 --> 00:17:02.520
but sustained through trust,
obligation, and affiliation.

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:05.819
Now, people may complain
that they spend too much time

00:17:05.819 --> 00:17:09.089
in meetings, often because
meetings aren't always

00:17:09.089 --> 00:17:12.710
an efficient information
exchange mechanism.

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:15.660
But information exchange
isn't the only reason

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:18.450
for going to a meeting
or for participating

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:21.270
in an ongoing social context.

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.619
People like to talk
with other people.

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:28.140
People go to a workshop, even
when the papers are online.

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:33.390
Showing up signifies the
importance of the occasion

00:17:33.390 --> 00:17:36.870
and the importance
of the community.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:39.720
Let me give you an example
of a scientist doing

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:44.520
productive work through
multiple ongoing relationships.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:46.260
And by the way, I
apologize for the sort

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:49.380
of wishy-washy
relationship word.

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:52.840
I couldn't figure out a
nice, crisp alternative,

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:54.700
so we're going to
have to go with it.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.600
Think of a scientist at the
weekly meeting of his research

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:01.530
group who's critiquing the
research presentation of one

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:03.720
of his doctoral students.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:07.140
This scientist is
simultaneously helping

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:11.850
to create new scientific
knowledge, perhaps describing

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:15.510
additional analyses that the
student could do in order

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:20.460
to refute the claims
made by a competing lab,

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:22.320
in addition,
reminding the student

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:25.170
to verify the analytic
techniques he's just been

00:18:25.170 --> 00:18:28.830
describing with the new postdoc,
who has recently arrived

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:31.530
from a colleague's lab where
these techniques were first

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:32.940
developed.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:34.500
That's one thing
that the scientist

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:36.810
is doing, helping to create
new scientific knowledge.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:40.590
At the same time, the scientist
is training the next generation

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:44.550
of scientists through suggesting
the additional analyses,

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:48.300
through, perhaps, critiquing the
format of the student's slides,

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:49.980
through asking the
student to present

00:18:49.980 --> 00:18:54.310
his results in the professor's
graduate survey course,

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:56.160
helping to create new
scientific knowledge,

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:58.950
training the next
generation of scientists,

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.620
deciding which of
his colleagues labs

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.620
to recommend as a good postdoc
placement for the student

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:08.130
when he finishes his thesis,
deciding which journal

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:10.770
to submit the
student's research to,

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.650
and remembering that he still
owes the editor of that journal

00:19:13.650 --> 00:19:16.080
reviews on three papers.

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:18.060
Thinking about how
these results are

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:22.440
going to affect the next round
of studies, and deciding he's

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:27.000
going to have to apply for new
funds sooner than he had hoped,

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:30.360
realizing that he's
going to have to say yes

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:34.800
to the provost's request that
he chair the university task

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:37.740
force on graduate
education, if he's

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:39.870
going to have any hope
of getting the provost

00:19:39.870 --> 00:19:43.590
to approve his request
for additional lab space.

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:47.220
Now, the manifest
task, when we're

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:51.600
thinking about this scientist,
the scientist's manifest task

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:54.660
is to critique a
research project.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:59.820
But all of the other tasks
are equally necessary to being

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.190
a productive scientist.

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:05.730
They all entail information
processing, absolutely.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:09.990
But each of these
information processing tasks

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:13.310
is embedded in a
particular social context,

00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:18.420
a particular social
structure of reciprocal human

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:23.280
interdependence, a structure
that's sustained through trust,

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:25.830
obligation, and affiliation.

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:31.230
Furthermore, the contexts
the scientist is operating in

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:34.890
simultaneously operate
at different levels

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:38.250
of social aggregation.

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:41.040
Let me see if I can explain a
little bit what I mean by that.

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:42.634
Could I have the
next one, please.

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:47.610
The social enterprise
of science operates

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:49.650
at three levels of aggregation.

00:20:49.650 --> 00:20:54.570
At the institutional level,
we have the human conventions

00:20:54.570 --> 00:21:00.120
and organizations that
certify knowledge and allocate

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:01.560
resources.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:04.120
We have the Nobel
Prize committees.

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:06.690
We have the scientific
journals with their editors

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.370
and editorial boards.

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:11.940
We have the research
funding agencies.

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:15.870
At the middle range, we have
the distributed colleagues

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:21.090
and competitors working
in a single area.

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:25.230
These play themselves out in
conferences, summer studies,

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:29.040
postdoc exchanges,
pre-print mailing lists.

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:32.610
The third level of
aggregation is the close

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:36.870
in level, the day-to-day
interaction with colleagues

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:38.520
and students.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:40.440
This is the place,
this is the level

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:43.740
of aggregation, where
the apparatus is built,

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.960
where the measurements
are taken,

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:50.250
where results are initially
puzzled over and discussed,

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:53.670
and where the next
generation is socialized.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:57.030
This is also the place where,
as some people would say,

00:21:57.030 --> 00:22:00.000
if you don't have to get on
an airplane to go somewhere,

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.470
this is where you go,
the close in level.

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:09.150
The productive scientist
has to work simultaneously

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:11.790
at all three levels
of aggregation

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:15.840
and has to process information
and manage relationships

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.220
at all three levels.

00:22:18.220 --> 00:22:21.210
So what can we say about
what computer support

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:26.100
for interdependent
actors looks like today?

00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:28.980
What are the systems
and applications that

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:31.610
acknowledge interdependence?

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:32.110
Next slide.

00:22:35.390 --> 00:22:39.410
I think the big success stories
are electronic mail and file

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:41.180
transfer.

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:43.820
I don't know if there's
anyone in this room who

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:46.070
would think they're
glamorous or represent

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:48.260
research frontiers for today.

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:50.990
But they certainly,
in terms of numbers

00:22:50.990 --> 00:22:53.660
of people who use them to
get productive work done,

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:58.940
are the success stories,
unstructured general tools.

00:22:58.940 --> 00:23:03.140
People can and do use them
in any social context--

00:23:03.140 --> 00:23:06.110
well, probably not yet
to the Nobel committees,

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:10.610
but in most other
social contexts.

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:15.120
They require very little social
and cognitive overhead to use.

00:23:15.120 --> 00:23:19.790
But of course, they're just
the beginning of what we need.

00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:21.380
What are people
working on today?

00:23:24.470 --> 00:23:28.070
I feel a bit audacious
telling this audience

00:23:28.070 --> 00:23:30.840
about what computer
scientists are doing today.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:35.750
But I think some of
these kinds of work

00:23:35.750 --> 00:23:38.840
are not ones that would be
particularly visible or perhaps

00:23:38.840 --> 00:23:41.450
even particularly
interesting to you, so let

00:23:41.450 --> 00:23:43.430
me just say a very brief word.

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:46.940
At the other end of the
spectrum from unstructured email

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:52.670
and file transfer is
workflow, something

00:23:52.670 --> 00:23:55.940
that, unfortunately,
business organizations

00:23:55.940 --> 00:23:57.660
are quite enamored of today.

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.620
I don't want to dwell
on it, but it does

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:04.370
acknowledge interdependence.

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:07.910
It does things, like
attempts to automate things,

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:11.540
like administrative processes,
like travel requests

00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:14.030
or payroll or some
business processes,

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:16.520
like approving
loan applications.

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:22.790
It's sort of a transaction
processing thing.

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:26.480
The focus is on the flow
of transaction tasks

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:28.040
across processors.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:31.560
Some of the processors
may be human beings.

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:34.640
But the focus
isn't at all on all

00:24:34.640 --> 00:24:37.520
of an individual human
being's responsibilities,

00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.860
obligations, and tasks
as they're structured

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:43.500
in roles in an organization.

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:48.760
Another kind of work that a
bit of which is going on today,

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:54.680
there is something called
CSCW, which stands for computer

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:57.700
supported co-operative work.

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:02.150
And there is
groupware, allegedly

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:04.520
support for people doing
intellectual tasks.

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:11.720
CSCW, the acronym, was
created, I think, in 1986.

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:17.000
So this is certainly not an
old and venerable subfield.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:19.340
But at any event,
some of the examples

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:21.860
of things that
people are working on

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:28.220
include working with shared
files, shared design spaces,

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:33.260
shared document editors, group
awareness tools, group meeting

00:25:33.260 --> 00:25:35.360
rooms, to support
face-to-face meetings

00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:38.170
or geographically
distributed meetings.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:43.220
Now typically, in
much of this work,

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:46.220
the focus is on a small group.

00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:49.700
I suspect a part of the reason
is it's sort of hard enough

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:52.820
to get the technology to work
for a small number of people.

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:56.150
I'm not clear that people
working in this area

00:25:56.150 --> 00:26:01.490
believe that the small group
is the only useful or important

00:26:01.490 --> 00:26:03.260
level at which people do work.

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:05.840
But at any event, much
of the work going on now

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:10.460
focuses on groups from
size, say, two to 10.

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:15.170
Also, much of this work
takes a single task focus.

00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:18.590
For instance, there's
work on the task

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:21.020
of collaborative design.

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:27.110
There's work on the task of
brainstorming in meetings.

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:32.600
These applications are
often highly structured.

00:26:32.600 --> 00:26:36.470
They may force people
to state assumptions

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:43.670
or to specify commitments and
to state their binary choices.

00:26:43.670 --> 00:26:46.520
You're either in a meeting
room or you're not.

00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:50.210
You're either present for the
video conference or you're not.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:55.430
You're either explicitly making
a commitment or you're not.

00:26:55.430 --> 00:27:02.450
I think that computer support
for the productive scientist,

00:27:02.450 --> 00:27:04.130
who doesn't just
process information

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:06.680
but who works through
relationships with other people

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:11.720
and organizations, needs to
have a whole different flavor.

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:14.450
Could I have the
next slide, please?

00:27:14.450 --> 00:27:21.230
It shouldn't just support
a task, a manifest task.

00:27:21.230 --> 00:27:22.830
Because the
scientist, of course,

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:25.550
is doing multiple different
tasks simultaneously,

00:27:25.550 --> 00:27:29.090
only one of which
is the manifest one.

00:27:29.090 --> 00:27:33.190
It should not just
support a group,

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:37.060
because the scientist
is simultaneously

00:27:37.060 --> 00:27:39.970
participating in multiple
groups at different levels

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:42.110
of social aggregation.

00:27:42.110 --> 00:27:45.040
So what should this
computer support do?

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:46.720
It should support
the scientist who

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:50.620
works in multiple
groups simultaneously,

00:27:50.620 --> 00:27:54.040
who works at different
levels of social aggregation

00:27:54.040 --> 00:27:55.840
simultaneously.

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:57.640
It should move
with the scientist

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.940
as he or she physically and
cognitively moves from group

00:28:00.940 --> 00:28:03.520
to group throughout the day.

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:05.170
And it should
allow the scientist

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:07.900
to map his information
environment

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:12.970
to his social contexts, not to
arbitrary physical locations

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:17.930
like a meeting room or
temporal constraints.

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:21.160
Now, since I'm not
a technologist,

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:24.850
it would be extraordinarily
silly for me to tell you

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:28.240
what the technology
should look like.

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:33.460
Instead, what I'd like to do
is describe some social facts

00:28:33.460 --> 00:28:37.480
that, if we had technology
support for them,

00:28:37.480 --> 00:28:40.810
could lead to more
productive behavior.

00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:44.710
Your assignment is going to
be to invent the technology

00:28:44.710 --> 00:28:47.140
to support the social facts.

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:48.960
Let me offer you three examples.

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:50.147
Could I have the next one?

00:28:52.829 --> 00:28:56.250
The first collection
of social facts

00:28:56.250 --> 00:29:01.620
has to do with the absent
meeting participant.

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:09.210
People have multiple
ongoing responsibilities.

00:29:09.210 --> 00:29:12.570
Their responsibilities
obligate them

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:18.540
to participate in particular
real-time meetings.

00:29:18.540 --> 00:29:21.690
Conflicting obligations
mean that not everyone

00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:25.260
who should attend a given
meeting will be able to do so.

00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:30.540
In fact, I'll bet that
every one of you who

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:33.570
is sitting in this
room today is missing

00:29:33.570 --> 00:29:36.360
one or more meetings
back in your home spot

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:38.830
that you should be attending.

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:41.970
I think we need technology
support for the partially

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:43.453
present meeting participant.

00:29:43.453 --> 00:29:45.870
Now, I have to tell you, I've
been in a number of meetings

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:48.060
where the people in the
room were partially present,

00:29:48.060 --> 00:29:49.800
but that's not what I mean.

00:29:49.800 --> 00:29:52.560
What I mean is
for the person who

00:29:52.560 --> 00:29:56.580
should be at the real-time
meeting but can't be.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:29:58.560
There should be
easy ways to review

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:00.970
the meeting after the fact.

00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:06.720
And I think, actually, in Raja's
videotape of earlier today,

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:10.560
I caught a glimpse of how
that might be able to happen.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:12.870
But that's only
half of the story.

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:16.140
That's good for the person
who can't be at the meeting.

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.630
But for the people
who are present

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:21.510
at the real-time
meeting, it should

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:23.700
be just as easy
for them to signal

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:28.020
the attention of an absent
member as a present one,

00:30:28.020 --> 00:30:32.250
to ask questions of the absent
member at particular points,

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:34.140
to request action.

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:36.960
The only difference will be
that the partially present

00:30:36.960 --> 00:30:41.200
participants will be
delayed for a bit.

00:30:41.200 --> 00:30:44.790
So I hope you can invent
technology support

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:48.180
for the partially present
meeting participant.

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:54.600
Second social fact-- the meeting
isn't over when it's over.

00:30:54.600 --> 00:31:01.470
Most meetings are embedded
in an ongoing social context.

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:03.990
A meeting doesn't
end when it's over.

00:31:03.990 --> 00:31:07.440
People figure out, after
they walk out the door,

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:09.930
what they wish they had said.

00:31:09.930 --> 00:31:12.877
They think of new
ideas to contribute.

00:31:12.877 --> 00:31:15.210
They do what they said they
would do during the meeting,

00:31:15.210 --> 00:31:18.000
or they don't and they
have to be reminded.

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.540
And so I want you to
invent technology support

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:23.790
for the post-meeting meeting.

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:26.010
After the face-to-face
meeting is over,

00:31:26.010 --> 00:31:27.840
it should be easy
for participants

00:31:27.840 --> 00:31:33.060
to slide new ideas, insights,
clarifications, contributions,

00:31:33.060 --> 00:31:37.120
into the ambient information
environment of the meeting.

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:40.590
These could be from fully
present participants

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:44.010
or partially present
participants.

00:31:44.010 --> 00:31:49.860
Third social fact-- group
membership is dynamic.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:52.350
Groups add new members.

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:56.250
These members not only come
with a different set of skills,

00:31:56.250 --> 00:31:58.590
which presumably is why
you put groups together

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:00.390
in the first place,
but they also

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:03.570
arrive with different
obligations, different ways

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:05.620
of doing business.

00:32:05.620 --> 00:32:08.370
Bringing a new
member up to speed

00:32:08.370 --> 00:32:11.820
is necessary for the member
to function productively

00:32:11.820 --> 00:32:15.030
in the group and for the group
to function productively.

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:19.800
Creating a new group is simply
a special case of adding members

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:23.250
to an initially empty group.

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:25.590
I know that Marjory
Blumenthal had

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:27.480
been invited to
attend this meeting

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:29.430
and was unable to be present.

00:32:29.430 --> 00:32:35.760
Marjorie runs the
panels that are

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:39.180
associated with
one of the National

00:32:39.180 --> 00:32:41.370
Academy of Sciences committees.

00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:47.430
And I think Marjory
will particularly

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:51.240
appreciate your
invention of technology

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:54.690
to support the dynamic
group member, technology

00:32:54.690 --> 00:33:01.110
to support adding members
to an initially empty group.

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:05.520
The process of convening
these advisory committees

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:08.160
is one that she certainly
handles with expertise.

00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:13.000
But it takes a lot
of time and energy.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:15.420
The rest of us who
have to convene groups

00:33:15.420 --> 00:33:17.280
of people from time
to time I think

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:19.540
could certainly appreciate that.

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:22.620
So what would technology support
for the dynamic group member

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:24.000
look like?

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:27.510
Well, one of the things that
we certainly would want to have

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:32.790
is a "who are these
people" button.

00:33:32.790 --> 00:33:35.290
When a new person
enters a group,

00:33:35.290 --> 00:33:41.010
he or she can easily learn about
the accomplishments, skills,

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:47.430
social circles of other
members, and vice versa.

00:33:47.430 --> 00:33:49.500
Of course, it will
be easy to go deeper

00:33:49.500 --> 00:33:52.490
if you wish by reading papers
or examining other work

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:56.420
products that are referenced
in the first level of the "who

00:33:56.420 --> 00:33:59.220
are these people" display.

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:01.080
So that's one piece of it.

00:34:01.080 --> 00:34:04.460
Second piece is
automatic configuration

00:34:04.460 --> 00:34:06.870
of group communication
protocols.

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:09.739
Now, Nick was telling
us this morning

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:13.429
that, in some number of years,
we'll be able to shake hands

00:34:13.429 --> 00:34:17.360
and all of that stuff
will pass automatically.

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:18.860
That's one way of doing it.

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:22.580
I guess I don't know
that I always want

00:34:22.580 --> 00:34:24.080
to shake hands with everybody.

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:27.080
But it would be nice
to have other way--

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:30.830
well, it would be nice
to have other ways, maybe

00:34:30.830 --> 00:34:34.340
that computers, without having
to get your hands involved,

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:36.980
could exchange
information about email

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:42.590
addresses, video conferencing
connections, the conventions

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:47.449
for producing work products that
each individual person brings

00:34:47.449 --> 00:34:51.440
with him or her into
the particular group.

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:54.258
What versions of what
software do people use?

00:34:54.258 --> 00:34:54.800
I don't know.

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:56.690
Maybe in the future,
there won't be versions.

00:34:56.690 --> 00:34:59.630
But I sort of think
there will be.

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:01.700
And all of that
information should

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:05.000
get exchanged automatically.

00:35:05.000 --> 00:35:06.800
That's automatic configuration.

00:35:06.800 --> 00:35:11.090
Then there should be easy ways
to configure at least some

00:35:11.090 --> 00:35:15.210
of the relationship protocols.

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:16.620
What do I mean by that?

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:19.340
How open or closed is the group?

00:35:22.310 --> 00:35:26.360
When and in what form
can its work products

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:29.210
be sent outside the group?

00:35:29.210 --> 00:35:32.120
Are drafts confidential?

00:35:32.120 --> 00:35:35.060
Can drafts be
circulated to colleagues

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:37.580
outside the membership
or the particular group?

00:35:40.490 --> 00:35:44.030
Who must review work
products before they

00:35:44.030 --> 00:35:45.350
can be shared more broadly?

00:35:45.350 --> 00:35:47.000
Who's in charge?

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:49.810
What's the calendar?

00:35:49.810 --> 00:35:58.550
Now, these are [? gizzies. ?] I
think that's a technical term.

00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:02.030
And I hope it's clear that
I'm not asking you just

00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:06.080
to build [? gizzies. ?] Can I
have the next slide, please?

00:36:06.080 --> 00:36:10.430
These are just
examples of what we

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:14.210
could hope to find in a
technology that supports

00:36:14.210 --> 00:36:20.420
a scientist who is not simply a
solo information processor, not

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:24.660
simply a generic
communicator to the galaxy,

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:29.060
but a person who works through
simultaneous relationships,

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:30.980
structured relationships,
with other people

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:32.280
and other organizations.

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:35.570
I also hope it's clear
that my vision will require

00:36:35.570 --> 00:36:38.210
new social inventions, too.

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:42.410
For example, the partially
present meeting participant

00:36:42.410 --> 00:36:44.090
is a social role.

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:48.890
We'll have to develop norms and
beliefs about this role, ways

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:50.330
of proceeding.

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:55.430
The "who are these
people" button requires--

00:36:55.430 --> 00:36:58.700
I can imagine this being
used with [? Avida ?]

00:36:58.700 --> 00:37:00.200
for academic people.

00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:03.830
But for non-academic
people, people

00:37:03.830 --> 00:37:07.580
who do not work in an
academic organization,

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:11.480
we need useful
ways of summarizing

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:15.440
a person's accomplishments,
skills, social circle,

00:37:15.440 --> 00:37:18.560
and so on.

00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:21.770
Although I have
explicitly tried to focus

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:26.570
on science and the doing of
productive scientific work,

00:37:26.570 --> 00:37:30.260
I'd like to look beyond
that domain for a moment

00:37:30.260 --> 00:37:36.140
and suggest that technology
that can support scientists, who

00:37:36.140 --> 00:37:39.950
already are working in multiple
simultaneous relationships,

00:37:39.950 --> 00:37:43.610
may also support people
who need but do not

00:37:43.610 --> 00:37:47.660
have a multiplicity of
ongoing relationships.

00:37:47.660 --> 00:37:51.290
Many human beings
are physically alone

00:37:51.290 --> 00:37:54.540
or feel socially isolated.

00:37:54.540 --> 00:37:57.350
They perhaps could benefit
from having more people

00:37:57.350 --> 00:38:00.080
in their ambient
information environment.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:03.320
Some usenet groups, as I suspect
all of you in this room know,

00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:07.280
already serve this function
to some extent for some people

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:07.890
today.

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:11.960
There is a newsgroup or listserv
for practically every malady

00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:14.390
or misfortune of
the human condition.

00:38:14.390 --> 00:38:18.140
And some of these have
been extraordinarily

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:23.090
important in the lives of
people who participate in them.

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:27.320
Now, technology to support
the dynamic group member

00:38:27.320 --> 00:38:31.910
could be used to help manage
electronic support groups.

00:38:31.910 --> 00:38:34.790
Support for the
post-meeting meeting

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:38.990
could be useful perhaps after a
parent-teacher conference, when

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:40.970
the parents think of
additional questions

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:44.930
they should have asked during
their 15 minutes allocated

00:38:44.930 --> 00:38:46.580
with the teacher.

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:50.720
Support for the partially
present meeting participant

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:55.610
could be useful for someone who
is unable to attend a spouse's

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:58.550
medical appointment,
but who needs to be

00:38:58.550 --> 00:39:00.395
involved in the treatment plan.

00:39:03.120 --> 00:39:08.450
I think that the vision of
the solo individual made

00:39:08.450 --> 00:39:13.010
more powerful, more
creative, more productive

00:39:13.010 --> 00:39:15.350
through technology
will continue to be

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:20.900
beguiling, seductive, important,
fundamentally important.

00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:24.740
New inventions for personal
computing based on that vision

00:39:24.740 --> 00:39:26.450
will and should continue.

00:39:26.450 --> 00:39:29.300
Could I have the
next slide, please?

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:30.860
What I've been
trying to do, though,

00:39:30.860 --> 00:39:33.890
is ask you to think about
how the person embedded

00:39:33.890 --> 00:39:37.940
in multiple social contexts can
be made more creative and more

00:39:37.940 --> 00:39:39.260
productive.

00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:41.630
Perhaps the real
challenge for the web

00:39:41.630 --> 00:39:45.890
is to support social context
through socially structured

00:39:45.890 --> 00:39:47.360
information.

00:39:47.360 --> 00:39:51.350
I think that the challenge for
technologists, more generally,

00:39:51.350 --> 00:39:54.830
is to invent support for
people who are not just

00:39:54.830 --> 00:39:59.960
solo information processors,
not just generic communicators,

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:03.020
but who are embedded
in and accomplish work

00:40:03.020 --> 00:40:06.060
through interdependence
with other people.

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:06.822
Thank you.

00:40:06.822 --> 00:40:09.654
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:14.620 --> 00:40:16.818
That was sort of
different, wasn't it?

00:40:16.818 --> 00:40:18.110
MODERATOR: Thank you very much.

00:40:18.110 --> 00:40:19.110
SPROULL: You're welcome.

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.395
ENGELBART: Folks, question time.

00:40:21.395 --> 00:40:23.270
Let's see, can we have
the house lights a bit

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:25.946
so that Lee can see you?

00:40:25.946 --> 00:40:27.920
SPROULL: Hello, Michael.

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:28.730
Yes, sir.

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:30.140
AUDIENCE: Michael Lesk.

00:40:30.140 --> 00:40:33.110
Garrison Keillor
writes about the rise

00:40:33.110 --> 00:40:37.740
of the shy, this feeling that,
as computer technology means

00:40:37.740 --> 00:40:42.230
that interaction at a distance
replaces or supplements

00:40:42.230 --> 00:40:45.770
face-to-face interaction, the
personality type that I guess

00:40:45.770 --> 00:40:48.740
we associate with
automobile salesmen

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:51.110
will become less
important in society,

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:53.900
and the type associated with
the wimp sitting at a typewriter

00:40:53.900 --> 00:40:56.810
will become more
important and dominant.

00:40:56.810 --> 00:40:58.760
Do you think this is
accurate or that anything

00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:00.590
like it will happen?

00:41:00.590 --> 00:41:04.700
SPROULL: Actually, I've done
some research documenting

00:41:04.700 --> 00:41:05.960
the rise of the shy.

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:07.970
And since I am one
of those folks,

00:41:07.970 --> 00:41:11.600
I'm delighted to report that
computer communication does,

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:16.790
in fact, bring with it
benefits for folks like me.

00:41:16.790 --> 00:41:18.680
I guess, though, I
think we'll always

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:20.240
still have automobile salesmen.

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:26.960
So I don't see it as one becomes
more influential or present

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:27.980
and one less.

00:41:27.980 --> 00:41:31.310
I don't see it as a 0
sum game, necessarily.

00:41:31.310 --> 00:41:34.460
Nor do I think that
real human beings

00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:39.780
are going to stop talking to
real human beings face-to-face.

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:40.280
Stu.

00:41:40.280 --> 00:41:41.197
AUDIENCE: Stuart Card.

00:41:44.660 --> 00:41:47.780
As communication becomes easier
with larger and larger numbers

00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:51.110
of people around the
world, and, in fact,

00:41:51.110 --> 00:41:55.220
as we succeed in
being able to collect

00:41:55.220 --> 00:42:02.470
more and more information
from the net and so on,

00:42:02.470 --> 00:42:04.160
there is more stuff to do.

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:08.190
But the amount of time
somebody has remains constant.

00:42:08.190 --> 00:42:11.510
So one of the real
issues seems to me

00:42:11.510 --> 00:42:15.690
is the management of attention
among all of these things.

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:19.490
So can you conceive
of ways to support

00:42:19.490 --> 00:42:24.050
the scientists to get
less communication

00:42:24.050 --> 00:42:27.160
or to have more time,
to be more solo.

00:42:27.160 --> 00:42:28.120
[LAUGHS]

00:42:28.120 --> 00:42:28.620
SPROULL: No.

00:42:28.620 --> 00:42:30.580
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:32.050 --> 00:42:35.000
No, Stu, I didn't
make my point clear.

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:38.630
The scientist doesn't
do science solo.

00:42:38.630 --> 00:42:43.850
The scientist does science in
a socially structured group.

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:47.450
And the group may be
able to figure out

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:53.040
ways to sustain its boundaries
so that information,

00:42:53.040 --> 00:42:58.010
or stuff, data, isn't
totally swamping them.

00:42:58.010 --> 00:43:03.020
I think that
graduate students are

00:43:03.020 --> 00:43:06.020
wonderful information
filter retrieval

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:08.540
devices for professors.

00:43:08.540 --> 00:43:12.590
And I don't want to propose
that we get rid of them

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:16.700
or replace their functions
with computing technology.

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:18.470
I really was trying
to make the point

00:43:18.470 --> 00:43:21.980
that, if you try to
invent technology

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:25.640
for the solo
information processor

00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:30.620
or the generic
society, you miss out

00:43:30.620 --> 00:43:35.510
on a lot of the helpful stuff
that social organizations are

00:43:35.510 --> 00:43:38.990
already doing for us,
structuring people's attention

00:43:38.990 --> 00:43:44.960
already and providing
mechanisms and roles for helping

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:47.060
manage information flow.

00:43:49.780 --> 00:43:52.130
AUDIENCE: Samuel Epstein
with Sense Media.

00:43:52.130 --> 00:43:53.810
We operate something
called the sprawl.

00:43:53.810 --> 00:43:55.310
Over a little a
year and a half ago,

00:43:55.310 --> 00:43:56.685
we took some
technology developed

00:43:56.685 --> 00:43:59.090
at Xerox PARC called LambdaMOO.

00:43:59.090 --> 00:44:01.790
And we modified it enhanced it--

00:44:01.790 --> 00:44:04.370
that's what we call it, some
people think we defiled it--

00:44:04.370 --> 00:44:09.020
to support hypermedia, by
adding support for hypertext

00:44:09.020 --> 00:44:11.380
and being able to inline
graphics and things like that.

00:44:11.380 --> 00:44:14.120
And right now as a result
over a year and a half, we

00:44:14.120 --> 00:44:17.220
now over 4,000 people
on our server in Hawaii.

00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:19.550
And we call it a collaborative
hyper media server.

00:44:19.550 --> 00:44:22.400
And it integrates together
text and graphics and chat

00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:25.172
and mail and news posts.

00:44:25.172 --> 00:44:26.630
And what I'm
wondering is if you've

00:44:26.630 --> 00:44:29.060
done any research into some of
the social interactions that

00:44:29.060 --> 00:44:29.852
are going on there.

00:44:29.852 --> 00:44:32.138
It's a very rich
and diverse society

00:44:32.138 --> 00:44:33.680
with a bunch of
people really working

00:44:33.680 --> 00:44:36.660
together all over
the world to create

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:38.130
hypermedia presentations.

00:44:38.130 --> 00:44:39.820
And this technology
is now spreading out

00:44:39.820 --> 00:44:41.070
into lots of servers all over.

00:44:41.070 --> 00:44:43.100
There's some stuff being done
at Brown University, as well.

00:44:43.100 --> 00:44:44.690
And I was wondering
if you studied

00:44:44.690 --> 00:44:47.090
any of these new
societies are starting up.

00:44:47.090 --> 00:44:50.330
They're mostly populated with
people under the age of 20,

00:44:50.330 --> 00:44:53.240
but they're doing
some incredible work.

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:54.110
SPROULL: Let's see.

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:56.450
The short answer to
your question is no.

00:44:56.450 --> 00:44:59.450
I haven't yet studied
these communities.

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:03.380
I'm certainly looking
forward to doing so.

00:45:03.380 --> 00:45:09.890
I mentioned, when I was
talking about the inventions

00:45:09.890 --> 00:45:14.240
that I hope you folks will do,
the fact that there have to be

00:45:14.240 --> 00:45:16.800
new social inventions as well.

00:45:16.800 --> 00:45:21.110
And at the moment,
I'm not sure that we

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:24.560
have good social
inventions for how

00:45:24.560 --> 00:45:26.930
to have 4,000
people work together

00:45:26.930 --> 00:45:29.900
productively on anything.

00:45:29.900 --> 00:45:34.910
People that you're describing
are doing that invention now.

00:45:34.910 --> 00:45:38.840
At the same time, you're
inventing or modifying

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:41.570
or whatever the
technology tools.

00:45:41.570 --> 00:45:48.050
So I look forward to having the
chance to try to systematically

00:45:48.050 --> 00:45:51.320
understand some of these kinds
of things that are going on.

00:45:51.320 --> 00:45:55.250
However, let me return for a
moment to productive science.

00:45:55.250 --> 00:46:00.890
Whereas there may have been
400 authors on the top quark

00:46:00.890 --> 00:46:05.030
papers, there certainly
were 4,000 people

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:09.590
involved in some way the
production of the research that

00:46:09.590 --> 00:46:11.060
led to those papers.

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:16.440
And those organizations are
highly socially structured.

00:46:16.440 --> 00:46:22.040
And I think it would be
interesting to learn from how--

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:24.740
to take the best of how
those organizations work--

00:46:24.740 --> 00:46:27.110
and lord knows they do
not work perfectly--

00:46:27.110 --> 00:46:29.150
but to take the best
of that and to try

00:46:29.150 --> 00:46:31.070
to understand how
that can also be

00:46:31.070 --> 00:46:33.640
useful in your
kind of new world.

00:46:37.340 --> 00:46:39.380
Ron.

00:46:39.380 --> 00:46:42.950
AUDIENCE: Ron Becker,
University of Toronto.

00:46:42.950 --> 00:46:45.920
Lee, I'd like to thank you for
a very interesting presentation.

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:50.000
And it strikes me, though, that
the scientific communities,

00:46:50.000 --> 00:46:54.710
in general, are resistant to
understanding and appreciating

00:46:54.710 --> 00:46:57.500
and believing in the
social context of science.

00:46:57.500 --> 00:46:59.780
And I go back to Bush.

00:46:59.780 --> 00:47:03.110
It strikes me that he probably
should have seen some of that,

00:47:03.110 --> 00:47:06.440
in terms of the
Manhattan Project, which

00:47:06.440 --> 00:47:10.052
was sort of one of the first
examples of big science.

00:47:10.052 --> 00:47:11.510
And I wonder whether,
first of all,

00:47:11.510 --> 00:47:16.070
you agree that communities
are resistant to believing

00:47:16.070 --> 00:47:18.740
this and appreciating
this and why this is.

00:47:18.740 --> 00:47:23.858
Is it something about the way we
educate scientists or whatever?

00:47:23.858 --> 00:47:25.400
SPROULL: Thank you
for the thank you.

00:47:25.400 --> 00:47:31.040
I think it's partly a function
of how we educate scientists.

00:47:31.040 --> 00:47:33.860
I think it's partly a function
of how scientists are.

00:47:36.530 --> 00:47:39.830
It's important that
there be a hubris,

00:47:39.830 --> 00:47:46.340
that there be a belief that
the solo inventor, thinker can

00:47:46.340 --> 00:47:47.990
change the world.

00:47:47.990 --> 00:47:50.540
Usually, it doesn't
work out, but you

00:47:50.540 --> 00:47:52.160
have to have that belief.

00:47:52.160 --> 00:47:54.890
It's fundamentally
important to the survival

00:47:54.890 --> 00:47:57.200
of the scientific enterprise.

00:47:57.200 --> 00:48:03.050
So I'm not discouraged or
unhappy when I see that.

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:04.430
It's natural.

00:48:08.540 --> 00:48:17.780
I think that often, as
scientists filled with hubris,

00:48:17.780 --> 00:48:23.480
age, they come a bit more
to appreciate the importance

00:48:23.480 --> 00:48:26.390
and necessity of
the social structure

00:48:26.390 --> 00:48:29.090
and the social enterprise
that supports their work.

00:48:29.090 --> 00:48:32.360
Unfortunately, that
sometimes happens too late

00:48:32.360 --> 00:48:36.350
for them to then proselytize for
the funding of social science

00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:36.980
research.

00:48:36.980 --> 00:48:39.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:41.630
Peter.

00:48:41.630 --> 00:48:43.750
AUDIENCE: Peter Denning.

00:48:43.750 --> 00:48:48.950
There is a book that I always
like to take inspiration from

00:48:48.950 --> 00:48:51.860
to combat the solo scientist.

00:48:51.860 --> 00:48:57.230
And that's the book by Bruno
Latour, Science in Action.

00:48:57.230 --> 00:48:59.990
Many of the other scientists
I've given that to to read

00:48:59.990 --> 00:49:01.260
loved the book.

00:49:01.260 --> 00:49:04.700
But when they get back to
their own academic departments

00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:07.880
and start considering
merit and promotion,

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:10.830
they always look for
the solo contribution.

00:49:10.830 --> 00:49:13.220
And they have all sorts
of ways to dig and see

00:49:13.220 --> 00:49:14.840
if they can find it.

00:49:14.840 --> 00:49:18.650
So we really do have kind of
a contradiction between where

00:49:18.650 --> 00:49:22.790
your heart wants to go-- your
heart wants to appreciate

00:49:22.790 --> 00:49:25.020
the social context.

00:49:25.020 --> 00:49:28.100
But when you put
it in action, you

00:49:28.100 --> 00:49:33.455
want to find the solo
inventor contribution.

00:49:33.455 --> 00:49:37.000
Do you have any suggestions
on how to change that?

00:49:37.000 --> 00:49:37.500
SPROULL: No.

00:49:37.500 --> 00:49:42.650
And I don't think,
actually, that we need

00:49:42.650 --> 00:49:44.930
to worry about changing that.

00:49:44.930 --> 00:49:51.980
I think what you need to do
is continue to push, to invent

00:49:51.980 --> 00:49:56.060
the technologies, to invent the
technology environments that

00:49:56.060 --> 00:49:59.300
will make it obvious
to one and all

00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:02.300
that scientific
collective work is

00:50:02.300 --> 00:50:04.790
where the productive
work of science occurs.

00:50:04.790 --> 00:50:07.820
But again, I don't want
to stamp out hubris.

00:50:07.820 --> 00:50:09.980
I mean, it's kind of
obnoxious in faculty meetings.

00:50:09.980 --> 00:50:12.980
[LAUGHTER] But
it's very important

00:50:12.980 --> 00:50:16.940
for the scientific
enterprise to continue.

00:50:16.940 --> 00:50:18.530
AUDIENCE: Another
question for you--

00:50:18.530 --> 00:50:24.990
you named a few names that are
more on the solo inventor side.

00:50:24.990 --> 00:50:27.770
What are some of the names that
you would hold up as examples

00:50:27.770 --> 00:50:29.540
to look at for some
of the other parts

00:50:29.540 --> 00:50:32.390
that you were
talking about there--

00:50:32.390 --> 00:50:35.810
for example, workflow or CSCW?

00:50:35.810 --> 00:50:38.780
SPROULL: Well, Doug
Engelbart, Doug Englebart,

00:50:38.780 --> 00:50:41.580
and Doug Engelbart, to begin.

00:50:41.580 --> 00:50:42.080
[LAUGHTER]

00:50:42.080 --> 00:50:47.510
And I hope you heard that I
did acknowledge him initially.

00:50:47.510 --> 00:50:52.940
All the work that
all of the people

00:50:52.940 --> 00:50:59.600
in computer communications and
shared files are doing today--

00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:03.170
I guess since I'm
not a technologist,

00:51:03.170 --> 00:51:08.150
I never feel the need to
put down anybody's stuff.

00:51:08.150 --> 00:51:09.800
I say, yeah, do more
of that, but let's

00:51:09.800 --> 00:51:11.160
do some other things, too.

00:51:11.160 --> 00:51:16.280
I want to expand your world,
not stamp out some things.

00:51:16.280 --> 00:51:19.130
But let's add more
people and more ways

00:51:19.130 --> 00:51:21.950
of thinking about inventing
technology support

00:51:21.950 --> 00:51:24.035
for interdependent people.

00:51:24.035 --> 00:51:24.910
MODERATOR: Thank you.

00:51:33.724 --> 00:51:34.550
You getting hungry?

00:51:34.550 --> 00:51:36.920
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:36.920 --> 00:51:37.868
Thank you, again.

00:51:37.868 --> 00:51:38.342
SPROULL: Thank you.

00:51:38.342 --> 00:51:39.175
MODERATOR: Terrific.

00:51:43.090 --> 00:51:45.130
What's been really fun
about this symposium

00:51:45.130 --> 00:51:48.580
for me is that I've been able
to introduce so many insanely

00:51:48.580 --> 00:51:52.690
great people, in the words
of the late great Steve Jobs.

00:51:52.690 --> 00:51:56.950
And certainly one of the
very topmost of those people

00:51:56.950 --> 00:51:59.260
is Alan Kay.

00:51:59.260 --> 00:52:02.290
Most of you identify him
with personal computers

00:52:02.290 --> 00:52:06.970
and personal computing, and at
a time when that idea was still

00:52:06.970 --> 00:52:12.070
embryonic or, as some of
us thought, lunatic fringe.

00:52:12.070 --> 00:52:16.900
He's made seminal contributions
to object-oriented programming,

00:52:16.900 --> 00:52:20.440
graphical user interfaces,
and window managers

00:52:20.440 --> 00:52:25.390
via the Smalltalk language and
its programming environment.

00:52:25.390 --> 00:52:28.600
He's always been interested in
the appropriate use of computer

00:52:28.600 --> 00:52:31.480
technology, and
especially in the area

00:52:31.480 --> 00:52:35.780
that appeals most
to me, education.

00:52:35.780 --> 00:52:37.720
Alan is also, as
most of you probably

00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:39.395
know, a very serious musician.

00:52:39.395 --> 00:52:42.130
He was a professional musician.

00:52:42.130 --> 00:52:44.005
And I try to characterize him--

00:52:44.005 --> 00:52:46.060
I first thought of
him as a polymath.

00:52:46.060 --> 00:52:48.580
But many people don't
know what that is.

00:52:48.580 --> 00:52:51.740
So then I thought about
interdisciplinarian.

00:52:51.740 --> 00:52:53.140
And Alan didn't like that.

00:52:53.140 --> 00:52:57.140
So we came up together
with calling him--

00:52:57.140 --> 00:52:59.560
Alan, what was the word?

00:52:59.560 --> 00:53:00.370
Trans--

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:02.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:05.640
Then we had to fish for that.

00:53:05.640 --> 00:53:08.920
AUDIENCE: [? Transcendalist. ?]

00:53:08.920 --> 00:53:12.030
MODERATOR: So trans
everything that I know about,

00:53:12.030 --> 00:53:14.100
here is Alan to
finish up for us.

00:53:14.100 --> 00:53:14.670
KAY: Thanks.

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:17.190
Thank you.

00:53:17.190 --> 00:53:20.370
We had to use the trans prefix
somehow since Ted is here.

00:53:24.720 --> 00:53:27.990
Well, when I was
thinking about what

00:53:27.990 --> 00:53:33.300
to do for this talk, when
I kept on thinking back

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:37.170
to the period when
I think that Bush

00:53:37.170 --> 00:53:41.790
was most influential
in the ARPA world,

00:53:41.790 --> 00:53:52.110
and I was thinking that it might
make a nice talk to just think

00:53:52.110 --> 00:54:01.680
about the '60s, just to cut
it off at the end of 1969.

00:54:01.680 --> 00:54:05.750
It just happened that 1970 is
when Xerox PARC started up.

00:54:05.750 --> 00:54:09.090
So cut it off before Xerox PARC.

00:54:09.090 --> 00:54:13.020
And when I was
thinking back, the more

00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:14.550
I thought back,
the more I thought

00:54:14.550 --> 00:54:18.600
of some of the
interesting people who

00:54:18.600 --> 00:54:23.460
influenced that very, very
interesting and exciting

00:54:23.460 --> 00:54:24.070
decade.

00:54:24.070 --> 00:54:29.910
So I thought to start off with
a little trip down memory lane.

00:54:29.910 --> 00:54:36.940
And as a kind of a
context for the talk,

00:54:36.940 --> 00:54:41.400
I thought I'd start off by
showing you Arthur Koestler's--

00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:44.390
Arthur Koestler was
a great novelist

00:54:44.390 --> 00:54:46.260
who, in the last
years of his life,

00:54:46.260 --> 00:54:52.050
became a psychologist
doing research

00:54:52.050 --> 00:54:55.530
in creativity at Stanford.

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:59.820
And one of his
characterizations of creativity

00:54:59.820 --> 00:55:01.120
sort of goes as follows.

00:55:01.120 --> 00:55:05.010
He said that, our
minds are kind of

00:55:05.010 --> 00:55:09.150
constrained to think within
contexts or within systems

00:55:09.150 --> 00:55:10.632
of belief.

00:55:10.632 --> 00:55:12.090
Sometimes they're
called paradigms.

00:55:12.090 --> 00:55:14.550
But he was thinking of
something perhaps larger,

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:19.680
that we have a set of
commonsensical things

00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:21.930
that we accept as reality.

00:55:21.930 --> 00:55:25.500
And our thought patterns
sort of jog along,

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:29.520
staying in this particular,
here, a pink plane.

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:32.130
Every once in a while, we
have little outlaw thoughts

00:55:32.130 --> 00:55:34.720
that are regarded as
lies by the pink plane.

00:55:34.720 --> 00:55:39.300
And they're usually just
suppressed and pushed back.

00:55:39.300 --> 00:55:42.810
And every once in a while,
when you're taking a shower

00:55:42.810 --> 00:55:49.320
or out jogging or something,
you are relaxed enough to let

00:55:49.320 --> 00:55:52.980
one of these outlaw thoughts
flourish a little bit.

00:55:52.980 --> 00:55:55.170
And sometimes what
you find out is

00:55:55.170 --> 00:56:00.600
that that little pink idea, when
expanded into the blue context,

00:56:00.600 --> 00:56:04.890
is much more interesting than
it was in the pink context.

00:56:04.890 --> 00:56:07.620
So the idea here is, terrific
ideas often hide behind

00:56:07.620 --> 00:56:08.662
good ones.

00:56:08.662 --> 00:56:10.245
Good ones are the
ones that are-- this

00:56:10.245 --> 00:56:12.180
is like peer review and NSF.

00:56:12.180 --> 00:56:13.582
[LAUGHTER]

00:56:13.582 --> 00:56:15.540
They always fund good
research, but they almost

00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:17.580
never fund great research.

00:56:17.580 --> 00:56:21.360
Because six people
are trying to decide

00:56:21.360 --> 00:56:24.480
whether what you're trying to
do is reasonable ahead of time.

00:56:24.480 --> 00:56:25.980
And usually the way
it works is, you

00:56:25.980 --> 00:56:30.810
decide whether it is reasonable
by looking what happened.

00:56:30.810 --> 00:56:32.640
And then Koestler said--

00:56:32.640 --> 00:56:34.920
and what happens
when you get one

00:56:34.920 --> 00:56:38.370
of these big blue inspirations--

00:56:38.370 --> 00:56:41.090
oh, sorry.

00:56:41.090 --> 00:56:41.715
Well, I goofed.

00:56:44.260 --> 00:56:47.820
I left out a slide, so
I'll just tell you here.

00:56:47.820 --> 00:56:49.620
He said, there are
three reactions.

00:56:49.620 --> 00:56:53.910
If you are telling a joke,
then the reaction is ha-ha.

00:56:53.910 --> 00:56:59.190
If you're doing science,
then the reaction is a-ha.

00:56:59.190 --> 00:57:02.480
And if you're doing art,
then the reaction is ah.

00:57:02.480 --> 00:57:04.670
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:04.670 --> 00:57:07.680
Because he points out that,
in each of these cases--

00:57:07.680 --> 00:57:09.210
for instance, a
joke is something

00:57:09.210 --> 00:57:10.950
that takes you down
the garden path

00:57:10.950 --> 00:57:13.650
and then suddenly reveals
that the context you're in

00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:15.810
is not the one you
thought you're in.

00:57:15.810 --> 00:57:19.410
Science has this
aspect, as well.

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:22.110
You suddenly see it
was there all the time.

00:57:22.110 --> 00:57:25.530
And often, besides saying
a-ha, you start laughing.

00:57:25.530 --> 00:57:27.990
And of course, in art,
the whole idea in art

00:57:27.990 --> 00:57:31.020
is to try and take
you out of the world

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:33.210
that you're in, your
commonplace world,

00:57:33.210 --> 00:57:35.640
and reveal that there's
more to the world.

00:57:35.640 --> 00:57:41.430
And one of the maxims
here is that, knowing more

00:57:41.430 --> 00:57:44.900
than your own field is
really helpful in doing this.

00:57:44.900 --> 00:57:47.400
And I've always thought that
one of the reasons the '60s was

00:57:47.400 --> 00:57:51.000
so interesting is that nobody
was a computer scientist back

00:57:51.000 --> 00:57:52.560
then.

00:57:52.560 --> 00:57:54.780
And everybody who came
into it came into it

00:57:54.780 --> 00:57:58.930
with lots of other
knowledge and interests.

00:57:58.930 --> 00:58:02.650
Then they tried to figure
out what computers were.

00:58:02.650 --> 00:58:06.300
And the only place they
had to use for analogies

00:58:06.300 --> 00:58:07.930
were other areas.

00:58:07.930 --> 00:58:12.870
And so we got some extremely
interesting ideas from that.

00:58:12.870 --> 00:58:15.660
And of course, the
reason being educated

00:58:15.660 --> 00:58:19.150
is important is simply because
you don't have any blue context

00:58:19.150 --> 00:58:21.510
if you don't have any
other kinds of knowledge

00:58:21.510 --> 00:58:23.370
to think with.

00:58:23.370 --> 00:58:27.090
So engineering is one
of the hardest fields

00:58:27.090 --> 00:58:31.530
to be creative in, just because
it's all about optimizing.

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:36.060
And you don't
optimize without being

00:58:36.060 --> 00:58:39.192
very firmly anchored to
the context that you're in.

00:58:39.192 --> 00:58:40.650
So what we're
talking about here is

00:58:40.650 --> 00:58:43.320
something that is not about
optimization, but actually

00:58:43.320 --> 00:58:46.530
in kind of rotating
the point of view.

00:58:49.180 --> 00:58:53.460
Now, I was five
years old in 1945.

00:58:53.460 --> 00:58:57.160
And I must have seen the
Life magazine article,

00:58:57.160 --> 00:58:59.450
because I used to peel
through Life magazine.

00:58:59.450 --> 00:59:02.550
But at that age, I was much
more interested in the manila

00:59:02.550 --> 00:59:07.290
envelopes that life came in
because you could draw on them.

00:59:07.290 --> 00:59:10.890
I'm sure that memex made no
impression on me back then.

00:59:10.890 --> 00:59:14.820
I ran across it when reading
a science fiction story

00:59:14.820 --> 00:59:17.880
by Robert Heinlein in the '50s.

00:59:17.880 --> 00:59:23.400
And Heinlein had this
nice thing of taking terms

00:59:23.400 --> 00:59:27.300
that he had found out about
and making them generic.

00:59:27.300 --> 00:59:30.060
So he spoke rather casually
in the science fiction

00:59:30.060 --> 00:59:32.250
story of a memex.

00:59:32.250 --> 00:59:35.460
And whenever he did
that, I had found out

00:59:35.460 --> 00:59:39.210
that that was a good
reason to go to the library

00:59:39.210 --> 00:59:44.260
and trying to find out
what basis memex had.

00:59:44.260 --> 00:59:49.020
That brought me to reading
Bush's article in the '50s.

00:59:49.020 --> 00:59:52.140
And I'm ashamed
to say that, aside

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:55.020
from being interesting in
the science fiction way,

00:59:55.020 --> 00:59:57.270
it made nowhere near
the impression on me

00:59:57.270 --> 01:00:01.050
that it did on Doug Engelbart
and some other people.

01:00:01.050 --> 01:00:05.760
It wasn't actually until
after I had met Doug Engelbart

01:00:05.760 --> 01:00:12.420
and had heard him talk that I
realized that the ideas of Bush

01:00:12.420 --> 01:00:14.160
had tremendous significance.

01:00:14.160 --> 01:00:17.970
But I got that through
the eyes and the ideas

01:00:17.970 --> 01:00:22.350
of Doug Engelbart.

01:00:22.350 --> 01:00:29.430
So for me, I start off in
the very early '60s, 1961,

01:00:29.430 --> 01:00:32.880
doing data processing
for the Air Force.

01:00:32.880 --> 01:00:37.360
And back then, they didn't
really have operating systems.

01:00:37.360 --> 01:00:39.200
They were very ad hoc.

01:00:39.200 --> 01:00:43.530
And Air Training
Command had many bases.

01:00:43.530 --> 01:00:45.690
And they had to
move files around.

01:00:45.690 --> 01:00:48.930
And somebody, to
this day unknown--

01:00:48.930 --> 01:00:52.440
I once tried to find out who it
was that thought up this idea--

01:00:52.440 --> 01:00:56.010
had come up with
a wonderful notion

01:00:56.010 --> 01:01:01.890
for allowing data to be
used from base to base

01:01:01.890 --> 01:01:05.700
without having to have lots
of standards and formats

01:01:05.700 --> 01:01:07.560
and operating systems.

01:01:07.560 --> 01:01:09.270
And the way it
worked was this-- is

01:01:09.270 --> 01:01:16.120
that each file on the Burroughs
220 tapes had three sections.

01:01:16.120 --> 01:01:18.150
The first section
was a set of pointers

01:01:18.150 --> 01:01:21.720
into the second section,
which was a set of code.

01:01:21.720 --> 01:01:24.130
And the code knew about
the third section,

01:01:24.130 --> 01:01:27.560
which was the formats
of the data records.

01:01:27.560 --> 01:01:30.180
So in modern terms,
what you have here

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:35.460
is a generic interface, a
set of methods, and then

01:01:35.460 --> 01:01:37.950
the data parts of objects.

01:01:37.950 --> 01:01:42.270
So that is the
first example that I

01:01:42.270 --> 01:01:47.490
know of in which this
object-oriented idea was

01:01:47.490 --> 01:01:48.210
actually used.

01:01:48.210 --> 01:01:51.510
Of course, it wasn't thought
of in those terms back then.

01:01:51.510 --> 01:01:54.510
But it was something that was
used quite a bit in the Air

01:01:54.510 --> 01:01:59.700
Force until they decided to
standardize on COBOL, in which

01:01:59.700 --> 01:02:01.770
case all of this
stuff got thrown out,

01:02:01.770 --> 01:02:04.560
and they went back
to processing data.

01:02:07.960 --> 01:02:10.680
Another thing I saw
that didn't make

01:02:10.680 --> 01:02:13.250
an enormous impression on me--

01:02:13.250 --> 01:02:15.980
in fact, I should say, the
other thing that happened was

01:02:15.980 --> 01:02:18.180
is that the third machine
I learned down here

01:02:18.180 --> 01:02:22.920
in the Air Force was the
Burroughs B-5000, which

01:02:22.920 --> 01:02:25.410
I think has seven of
the 10 best software

01:02:25.410 --> 01:02:28.490
ideas directly in the hardware.

01:02:28.490 --> 01:02:31.500
And at that time, I only
appreciated a couple of them.

01:02:31.500 --> 01:02:34.020
The fact that it was
very easy to compile too

01:02:34.020 --> 01:02:36.880
was pretty much all I
understood about that machine.

01:02:36.880 --> 01:02:40.620
But it also had something
like this technique

01:02:40.620 --> 01:02:46.163
of being able to deal with
[INAUDIBLE] indirectly.

01:02:46.163 --> 01:02:48.330
They could do all of the
things that you're normally

01:02:48.330 --> 01:02:50.820
used to doing with
bits but without having

01:02:50.820 --> 01:02:54.000
to know what the bits look
like or where they were.

01:02:54.000 --> 01:02:58.260
I didn't appreciate
that at the time.

01:02:58.260 --> 01:03:03.230
In '65, I was actually
helping debug the Control Data

01:03:03.230 --> 01:03:07.696
6600, which is my vote for
the first RISC machine.

01:03:07.696 --> 01:03:10.360
It was about 1965.

01:03:10.360 --> 01:03:16.450
It was a 10 MIP machine with
the kind of instruction set

01:03:16.450 --> 01:03:20.830
and parallel operations that
we associate with RISC machines

01:03:20.830 --> 01:03:21.620
today.

01:03:21.620 --> 01:03:26.890
And while I was working on that
machine at Seymour Cray's lab,

01:03:26.890 --> 01:03:32.890
I read this first
article by Gordon Moore

01:03:32.890 --> 01:03:35.320
showing what had been happening
in integrated circuits

01:03:35.320 --> 01:03:37.977
up to 1965.

01:03:37.977 --> 01:03:40.060
Up to that point, it had
been doubling every year.

01:03:40.060 --> 01:03:43.786
And so his first
cut at it, was what

01:03:43.786 --> 01:03:47.080
if it kept on
doubling every year?

01:03:47.080 --> 01:03:48.740
So that was interesting
to think about.

01:03:48.740 --> 01:03:56.320
But from my standpoint, I really
couldn't make any extrapolation

01:03:56.320 --> 01:04:00.880
or jump from this
freon-cooled 6600

01:04:00.880 --> 01:04:04.060
that could detect how
many people were actually

01:04:04.060 --> 01:04:05.650
in the room at a
given time, because

01:04:05.650 --> 01:04:09.250
of the capacitance on its
doors from human beings

01:04:09.250 --> 01:04:13.530
move moving around, to what
this might actually mean.

01:04:13.530 --> 01:04:17.130
Now, a couple of years later,
Gordon Moore looked at it

01:04:17.130 --> 01:04:21.370
a little more closely, looked
at the physics of the silicon,

01:04:21.370 --> 01:04:24.130
and said, well,
it probably won't

01:04:24.130 --> 01:04:26.450
be worse than a factor
of two every two years.

01:04:26.450 --> 01:04:30.940
So that's that lower,
less sloped line.

01:04:30.940 --> 01:04:33.850
And just for interest,
what actually

01:04:33.850 --> 01:04:40.150
happened from about 1970 on
is that magenta line there.

01:04:40.150 --> 01:04:43.330
So an interesting
thing about Moore's law

01:04:43.330 --> 01:04:47.350
was, it was actually
a 30-year prediction.

01:04:47.350 --> 01:04:53.020
And at least the one that
he did in 1967 or '68 or so,

01:04:53.020 --> 01:04:54.880
he was able to
bracket successfully

01:04:54.880 --> 01:04:56.560
what was going to
happen exponentially

01:04:56.560 --> 01:04:59.680
in silicon for about 30 years.

01:04:59.680 --> 01:05:02.748
Quite a remarkable, almost
unbelievable-- and I

01:05:02.748 --> 01:05:04.540
think that most people
in the industry that

01:05:04.540 --> 01:05:06.940
were going to be
affected incredibly by it

01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:08.710
couldn't believe it at all.

01:05:08.710 --> 01:05:10.540
At the most, they
thought it might

01:05:10.540 --> 01:05:13.750
mean that they would have more
margins on their mainframes.

01:05:13.750 --> 01:05:16.570
But the idea that this might
mean millions and millions

01:05:16.570 --> 01:05:20.230
of computers, I don't
think affected anybody

01:05:20.230 --> 01:05:22.450
at the time, including
Gordon Moore.

01:05:26.060 --> 01:05:29.540
But when I went to
the University of Utah

01:05:29.540 --> 01:05:33.470
sort of by an accident,
the first person I ran into

01:05:33.470 --> 01:05:35.480
was Dave Evans, the
head of the department.

01:05:35.480 --> 01:05:38.720
And the "this" that's
referred to there

01:05:38.720 --> 01:05:40.160
was Ivan Sutherland's thesis.

01:05:40.160 --> 01:05:43.340
He had a stack of
these brown theses

01:05:43.340 --> 01:05:45.170
from Lincoln Labs on his desk.

01:05:45.170 --> 01:05:48.860
And he had to take one of
these and go away and read it

01:05:48.860 --> 01:05:50.930
before you got a desk.

01:05:50.930 --> 01:05:56.280
And I must say, I didn't really
understand Sketchpad very well.

01:05:56.280 --> 01:05:59.720
But it seemed to be about
a system that was not

01:05:59.720 --> 01:06:02.576
just about making drawings.

01:06:02.576 --> 01:06:04.280
And that was an
idea I hadn't really

01:06:04.280 --> 01:06:07.500
thought about either back then.

01:06:07.500 --> 01:06:10.760
But it was also the idea
that the drawings generally

01:06:10.760 --> 01:06:13.880
had meaning, especially
in engineering.

01:06:13.880 --> 01:06:16.910
And what you would really
like to do with these,

01:06:16.910 --> 01:06:19.160
most of the drawings
you make in engineering

01:06:19.160 --> 01:06:21.690
is to see whether
they actually work.

01:06:21.690 --> 01:06:26.360
So the underlying notion
of Sketchpad-- in fact,

01:06:26.360 --> 01:06:28.220
it was conditioned
by the fact that

01:06:28.220 --> 01:06:31.190
the display on the TX-2
at Lincoln Lab-- which

01:06:31.190 --> 01:06:34.190
was a machine, by the way,
about the size of this room,

01:06:34.190 --> 01:06:37.020
maybe a little larger.

01:06:37.020 --> 01:06:39.800
You're just seeing one
small part of it here.

01:06:39.800 --> 01:06:42.440
The display was so bad
that it had discouraged

01:06:42.440 --> 01:06:44.180
previous people
who had been asked

01:06:44.180 --> 01:06:47.000
to do computer graphics on it.

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:49.730
And the difference
here was that Ivan

01:06:49.730 --> 01:06:52.610
looked at this terrible
display and asked the question

01:06:52.610 --> 01:06:56.662
we should all ask more often,
which is, what else can it do?

01:06:56.662 --> 01:06:58.370
And when he thought
about it, he realized

01:06:58.370 --> 01:07:01.340
that, although the pictures
were going to be terrible

01:07:01.340 --> 01:07:03.080
and the drawings
done by the plotter

01:07:03.080 --> 01:07:05.150
were not going to
be up to engineering

01:07:05.150 --> 01:07:08.950
standards, that what else
it could do was to simulate.

01:07:08.950 --> 01:07:12.140
And this is an idea that
was not strongly in any

01:07:12.140 --> 01:07:15.410
of Bush's writings, in
spite of the fact that he

01:07:15.410 --> 01:07:18.200
was one of the developers of
early analog computers, which

01:07:18.200 --> 01:07:20.600
in fact did simulate.

01:07:20.600 --> 01:07:24.950
And of course, he had funded
ENIAC, which simulated up

01:07:24.950 --> 01:07:26.505
to a certain point.

01:07:26.505 --> 01:07:28.130
But if you look in
his writings, you'll

01:07:28.130 --> 01:07:33.440
see that he thought that
certain logical reasoning would

01:07:33.440 --> 01:07:36.080
eventually be able to
be done by computing.

01:07:36.080 --> 01:07:41.720
And he had some real jumps,
as far as transcribing speech.

01:07:41.720 --> 01:07:45.560
But the notion of being
able to deal interactively

01:07:45.560 --> 01:07:47.090
with simulations
was not something

01:07:47.090 --> 01:07:50.300
that was really in his writing.

01:07:50.300 --> 01:07:52.810
This was an idea that came
about from other people.

01:07:52.810 --> 01:07:55.520
And I thought, just
for the fun of it,

01:07:55.520 --> 01:08:00.620
to show you one
of the movies made

01:08:00.620 --> 01:08:04.490
the late summer of '62,
the first set of movies

01:08:04.490 --> 01:08:06.020
made of Sketchpad.

01:08:06.020 --> 01:08:08.810
And in reviewing this,
there's one place

01:08:08.810 --> 01:08:11.270
on the tape where
there's some tape damage.

01:08:11.270 --> 01:08:13.130
So we'll just ignore
that as we go along.

01:08:13.130 --> 01:08:15.020
Let's roll that first tape.

01:08:20.756 --> 01:08:25.229
See you dip the light
pan into the ink.

01:08:25.229 --> 01:08:28.850
And this rubber band
technique Ivan invented.

01:08:28.850 --> 01:08:30.479
And why they call
it Sketchpad is

01:08:30.479 --> 01:08:34.080
that you could put in something
very quickly and loosely,

01:08:34.080 --> 01:08:35.310
and then give it some rules.

01:08:35.310 --> 01:08:38.819
And here, the rules are to
be mutually perpendicular.

01:08:38.819 --> 01:08:40.680
And what you saw
there is Sketchpad

01:08:40.680 --> 01:08:42.279
figuring out how to do that.

01:08:42.279 --> 01:08:44.279
Now, you'll notice this
is the first system ever

01:08:44.279 --> 01:08:46.510
to have a window.

01:08:46.510 --> 01:08:49.470
And in fact, the simulated
paper that he's drawing on

01:08:49.470 --> 01:08:52.819
is about 1/3 of a mile
on a side, I believe.

01:08:52.819 --> 01:08:56.399
And again, a couple of
lines drawn in quickly,

01:08:56.399 --> 01:08:59.160
and then giving the constraints
to make them be perpendicular

01:08:59.160 --> 01:09:02.880
to the lines that
they're terminated on.

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:06.479
And now the constraint
is colinearity.

01:09:06.479 --> 01:09:10.680
So he's using these
two lines he drew

01:09:10.680 --> 01:09:12.750
as guidelines for these dashes.

01:09:18.520 --> 01:09:25.010
And now he will tell the
guidelines to be invisible.

01:09:25.010 --> 01:09:27.326
So now he's drawn a
hole in this flange.

01:09:33.399 --> 01:09:36.880
I think this is where
the damage shows up.

01:09:36.880 --> 01:09:39.700
So now he wants to do a rivet.

01:09:39.700 --> 01:09:42.620
It'll clear up in a second.

01:09:42.620 --> 01:09:43.120
Sorry.

01:09:46.060 --> 01:09:49.220
Or maybe it won't.

01:09:49.220 --> 01:09:52.010
So he's using the
center of the crossbars

01:09:52.010 --> 01:09:55.750
there as the center
of the circle.

01:09:55.750 --> 01:09:58.160
And again, he's saying,
let's make these guys

01:09:58.160 --> 01:09:59.460
mutually perpendicular.

01:09:59.460 --> 01:10:02.120
Well, that changes the crossbars
and that changes the circle,

01:10:02.120 --> 01:10:05.730
so you get a nice,
symmetric rivet.

01:10:05.730 --> 01:10:09.290
In fact, Sketchpad could solve
some very interesting nonlinear

01:10:09.290 --> 01:10:10.790
problems.

01:10:10.790 --> 01:10:14.720
That bridge that you
saw on the slide,

01:10:14.720 --> 01:10:18.410
you could draw bridges in,
give the rules for stresses

01:10:18.410 --> 01:10:21.890
and strain of beams,
and it would calculate

01:10:21.890 --> 01:10:24.830
how much stress and strain there
would be for given weights hung

01:10:24.830 --> 01:10:26.330
from the bridge.

01:10:26.330 --> 01:10:29.060
You could draw in electric
circuit elements, put

01:10:29.060 --> 01:10:31.500
in Ohm's law, and so forth.

01:10:40.320 --> 01:10:44.010
So now what he's going
to do is make some rivets

01:10:44.010 --> 01:10:45.320
for his drawing here.

01:10:48.880 --> 01:10:53.860
The controls here, he's using
one hand with the light pen.

01:10:53.860 --> 01:10:59.077
And on the other hand, he has
some switches for commands

01:10:59.077 --> 01:11:00.160
for different constraints.

01:11:00.160 --> 01:11:03.250
And he also has some
knobs that he's turning.

01:11:06.520 --> 01:11:09.250
So you can see this system
led to an interest in better

01:11:09.250 --> 01:11:10.630
displays.

01:11:10.630 --> 01:11:12.970
It's actually not even
a line drawing display.

01:11:12.970 --> 01:11:15.340
It's putting up each
dot individually.

01:11:18.340 --> 01:11:21.900
And in fact, there's
a kind of a twinkling

01:11:21.900 --> 01:11:24.970
pattern that's put up there.

01:11:24.970 --> 01:11:29.840
So he says, oh, I forgot there's
these crossbars on the rivets.

01:11:29.840 --> 01:11:32.760
So I'll go to the master drawing
and I'll get rid of those guys.

01:11:32.760 --> 01:11:38.010
And lo and behold, we see
that these rivets here

01:11:38.010 --> 01:11:39.930
were actually instances.

01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:42.450
So Sketchpad was
really the world's

01:11:42.450 --> 01:11:47.490
first object-oriented system.

01:11:47.490 --> 01:11:50.970
And he really had pretty
much all of the paraphernalia

01:11:50.970 --> 01:11:54.180
of object-oriented programming.

01:11:54.180 --> 01:11:57.630
And now he's made a
flange with a rivet in it.

01:11:57.630 --> 01:12:00.990
And he can use anything
that he makes as a master.

01:12:00.990 --> 01:12:04.260
So now he's making instances
of the flange with the rivet,

01:12:04.260 --> 01:12:07.780
and positioning them and
scaling them and so forth.

01:12:07.780 --> 01:12:11.910
So I once asked Ivan, how
could you possibly-- okay,

01:12:11.910 --> 01:12:13.200
stop the tape.

01:12:13.200 --> 01:12:14.430
That's enough.

01:12:14.430 --> 01:12:17.670
So I once asked Ivan,
how could you possibly

01:12:17.670 --> 01:12:20.370
have invented computer
graphics, the world's

01:12:20.370 --> 01:12:24.780
first object-oriented software
system, and, as you can see,

01:12:24.780 --> 01:12:26.640
one of the very
first dynamic problem

01:12:26.640 --> 01:12:30.540
solvers that could do both
linear and nonlinear problems?

01:12:30.540 --> 01:12:32.622
And Ivan said, well, I
didn't know it was hard.

01:12:32.622 --> 01:12:34.010
[LAUGHTER]

01:12:34.010 --> 01:12:36.300
And in fact, if you
read his thesis--

01:12:36.300 --> 01:12:40.690
Bob, do you know, did it ever
get published out in the world?

01:12:40.690 --> 01:12:42.690
No.

01:12:42.690 --> 01:12:44.670
Besides still
being great today--

01:12:44.670 --> 01:12:47.070
I mean, it's really
saying something

01:12:47.070 --> 01:12:51.580
to be able to read something
that is interesting today.

01:12:51.580 --> 01:12:54.030
That is, you can actually
learn something from it

01:12:54.030 --> 01:12:57.150
by reading it today and
comparing what's going on.

01:12:57.150 --> 01:13:00.240
What is really interesting
is the diffident

01:13:00.240 --> 01:13:05.010
and even apologetic
tone in Sketchpad

01:13:05.010 --> 01:13:08.460
because it didn't do more.

01:13:08.460 --> 01:13:10.980
And this I think is true
of most of the people

01:13:10.980 --> 01:13:13.680
that I think of as the
great people in the '60s,

01:13:13.680 --> 01:13:16.370
is that they really
wanted it all.

01:13:16.370 --> 01:13:18.510
So the image of what
Sketchpad should do

01:13:18.510 --> 01:13:20.962
went far beyond what
it was able to do.

01:13:20.962 --> 01:13:22.920
Although, when we look
at it today, we are just

01:13:22.920 --> 01:13:27.180
awestruck by what he was willing
to take on just from scratch

01:13:27.180 --> 01:13:29.064
and what he was
able to accomplish.

01:13:32.270 --> 01:13:36.650
Well, right after
that, I found my desk.

01:13:36.650 --> 01:13:39.770
And they had a tradition at
Utah that the latest graduate

01:13:39.770 --> 01:13:42.590
student got the
latest dirty task.

01:13:42.590 --> 01:13:47.000
And actually, I was graduate
student number seven.

01:13:47.000 --> 01:13:49.880
So there were actually
quite a few dirty tasks

01:13:49.880 --> 01:13:52.340
left for people to do.

01:13:52.340 --> 01:13:55.190
And there was a pile
of listings and tapes

01:13:55.190 --> 01:13:59.870
on the desk that were supposed
to be the ALGOL for the campus

01:13:59.870 --> 01:14:00.860
computer.

01:14:00.860 --> 01:14:03.770
It turned out to be the
first SIMULA system.

01:14:03.770 --> 01:14:07.378
And the documentation was
quite incomprehensible.

01:14:07.378 --> 01:14:09.170
And finally, another
graduate student and I

01:14:09.170 --> 01:14:11.240
unrolled the listings
for it down the hall

01:14:11.240 --> 01:14:15.590
of Merrill engineering building,
some 80 feet long or so.

01:14:15.590 --> 01:14:18.440
And he crawled on it on
one part of the listing,

01:14:18.440 --> 01:14:21.680
and I crawled-- imagine doing
this on a display today.

01:14:21.680 --> 01:14:24.290
It was very useful that he
could crawl over it down there

01:14:24.290 --> 01:14:27.450
and I was crawling over it down
here reading the machine code,

01:14:27.450 --> 01:14:29.690
try and figure out
what the thing did.

01:14:29.690 --> 01:14:34.610
And we finally realized that
it was allocating storage

01:14:34.610 --> 01:14:36.290
exactly the way Sketchpad did.

01:14:36.290 --> 01:14:39.050
In fact, it was kind of
a programming language

01:14:39.050 --> 01:14:41.570
version of the same
kind of internal model

01:14:41.570 --> 01:14:43.620
that Sketchpad had.

01:14:43.620 --> 01:14:49.430
And the good news was,
Sketchpad couldn't solve

01:14:49.430 --> 01:14:51.710
every problem you posed to it.

01:14:51.710 --> 01:14:55.250
So it had a limited range
of things it could do.

01:14:55.250 --> 01:14:57.530
But it did those very elegantly.

01:14:57.530 --> 01:15:00.770
SIMULA was a generalized
programming language.

01:15:00.770 --> 01:15:02.960
And so you could
pretty much do anything

01:15:02.960 --> 01:15:05.480
that you could write
programs for, but none of it

01:15:05.480 --> 01:15:07.360
was particularly elegant.

01:15:11.590 --> 01:15:18.220
Now, in my checkered past was
a degree in molecular biology.

01:15:18.220 --> 01:15:22.040
And this book had
come out in 1965.

01:15:22.040 --> 01:15:25.120
This visit to Utah
started in '66 or so.

01:15:25.120 --> 01:15:28.060
And I'd read this
book by Watson.

01:15:28.060 --> 01:15:30.400
And one of the neat things
he did in this book--

01:15:30.400 --> 01:15:35.560
this book is still in print,
but in a current edition.

01:15:35.560 --> 01:15:37.180
The first edition
of this, I think,

01:15:37.180 --> 01:15:39.730
was the first book
that ever tried to do

01:15:39.730 --> 01:15:44.810
an assay of living things.

01:15:44.810 --> 01:15:48.130
So the favorite animal
that molecular biologists

01:15:48.130 --> 01:15:51.430
looked at then
was E. coli, which

01:15:51.430 --> 01:15:54.430
is a bacterium we have in
our gut by the millions.

01:15:54.430 --> 01:15:58.060
It helps us digest
certain kinds of food.

01:15:58.060 --> 01:16:03.820
And by that time, they were
able to tell us that about 70%

01:16:03.820 --> 01:16:12.100
of E. coli were water and
small charge molecules, which

01:16:12.100 --> 01:16:15.640
actually were, of course,
important for the reactions

01:16:15.640 --> 01:16:18.310
that were going on
there, but didn't

01:16:18.310 --> 01:16:20.650
interact informationally.

01:16:20.650 --> 01:16:25.240
But there were about 120
million organic components

01:16:25.240 --> 01:16:29.140
that interact
informationally, mostly

01:16:29.140 --> 01:16:35.210
enzymes, proteins of various
kinds, and DNA and so forth.

01:16:35.210 --> 01:16:37.810
And if you work
out what these are

01:16:37.810 --> 01:16:41.830
in terms of representing them
on a computer in a fairly

01:16:41.830 --> 01:16:46.760
compact way, it's about 100
gigabytes of stuff going on.

01:16:46.760 --> 01:16:50.372
And so I wrote down
there, it's about 50,000

01:16:50.372 --> 01:16:55.060
2-megabyte desktops
worth in an E. coli.

01:16:55.060 --> 01:16:58.750
And one of things
to think about here

01:16:58.750 --> 01:17:02.290
is how fast the reactions
are actually going on.

01:17:02.290 --> 01:17:08.090
At the temperature 37
degrees centigrade,

01:17:08.090 --> 01:17:11.050
which is pretty much what your
body temperature is inside

01:17:11.050 --> 01:17:15.880
of you, these medium-sized
protein molecules,

01:17:15.880 --> 01:17:18.790
those little white things
that look like popcorn there,

01:17:18.790 --> 01:17:20.380
are able to move
their own length

01:17:20.380 --> 01:17:21.820
in about two nanoseconds.

01:17:24.820 --> 01:17:28.030
People, when they take
chemistry in high school,

01:17:28.030 --> 01:17:30.700
most chemistry teachers
actually don't understand

01:17:30.700 --> 01:17:33.820
why chemistry works,
and they will shy away

01:17:33.820 --> 01:17:38.530
from any questions from students
asking, just how can atoms

01:17:38.530 --> 01:17:42.670
and molecules find each other in
order to have these reactions.

01:17:42.670 --> 01:17:45.790
And the answer is, is
that the thermal agitation

01:17:45.790 --> 01:17:49.150
at that level of scale
is unbelievably violent.

01:17:49.150 --> 01:17:50.710
I'll give you an example.

01:17:50.710 --> 01:17:55.960
If a carbon atom is about
the size of a tennis ball,

01:17:55.960 --> 01:17:57.490
then one of these
protein molecules

01:17:57.490 --> 01:18:00.440
is about the size
of a Volkswagen.

01:18:00.440 --> 01:18:02.980
So in our scale, if you can
imagine a Volkswagen made out

01:18:02.980 --> 01:18:06.640
of tennis balls, moving from
here, its own length in two

01:18:06.640 --> 01:18:09.580
nanoseconds, and if you
multiply that out, you'll

01:18:09.580 --> 01:18:11.920
see that that is a
scale speed that's

01:18:11.920 --> 01:18:14.210
faster than the speed of light.

01:18:14.210 --> 01:18:17.620
So if we were actually
transported down in size

01:18:17.620 --> 01:18:21.400
to being able to look at what an
E. coli is doing at normal body

01:18:21.400 --> 01:18:25.060
temperature, we would be
stunned by the violence.

01:18:25.060 --> 01:18:29.080
In fact, we could not see
anything that's going on.

01:18:29.080 --> 01:18:31.150
It's just unbelievably violent.

01:18:31.150 --> 01:18:34.510
And that is why biology
and chemistry actually

01:18:34.510 --> 01:18:37.420
works, something
that's very hard

01:18:37.420 --> 01:18:40.930
to get into people's intuition.

01:18:40.930 --> 01:18:44.590
So that is a very complex
and interesting structure.

01:18:44.590 --> 01:18:48.580
And it happens to
be 1/500 the size

01:18:48.580 --> 01:18:51.000
of a typical cell in our body.

01:18:51.000 --> 01:18:54.490
And we happen to have between
10 trillion and 100 trillion

01:18:54.490 --> 01:18:57.590
of these cells in our body.

01:18:57.590 --> 01:19:02.230
And yet 50 cell divisions are
all it takes to grow a baby.

01:19:02.230 --> 01:19:06.970
Now, think of taking something
much, much simpler, like a 747

01:19:06.970 --> 01:19:10.930
that Lee Sproull was-- she
was talking about a 777.

01:19:10.930 --> 01:19:13.840
And imagine what you have to do
to make it six inches longer.

01:19:16.390 --> 01:19:19.295
The manuals for it are
bigger than the plane,

01:19:19.295 --> 01:19:20.660
if they were listed out.

01:19:20.660 --> 01:19:23.410
I'm sure they're on CD-ROMs now.

01:19:23.410 --> 01:19:26.252
And yet a baby gets six
inches longer about 10 times

01:19:26.252 --> 01:19:28.210
in its life, and you
never have to take it down

01:19:28.210 --> 01:19:30.470
for maintenance.

01:19:30.470 --> 01:19:33.700
So what we have
here is something

01:19:33.700 --> 01:19:37.480
that is a kind of
informational machinery

01:19:37.480 --> 01:19:40.830
that is just in a
different league

01:19:40.830 --> 01:19:46.320
than computers are even
today, and certainly,

01:19:46.320 --> 01:19:50.260
if you look at the number of
components in animal cells

01:19:50.260 --> 01:19:51.880
and think of how many we have.

01:19:51.880 --> 01:19:55.690
The other thing about
them is that they're

01:19:55.690 --> 01:19:57.320
dying all the time.

01:19:57.320 --> 01:19:59.110
And not only that,
they're actually

01:19:59.110 --> 01:20:01.760
exchanging their structure.

01:20:01.760 --> 01:20:03.970
So another interesting
thing that I

01:20:03.970 --> 01:20:08.650
knew from molecular biology
is that, no atom in our body

01:20:08.650 --> 01:20:12.160
stays in our body for
more than seven years.

01:20:12.160 --> 01:20:15.600
And in fact, more than 90%
of the atoms in our body

01:20:15.600 --> 01:20:19.022
are recycled every two weeks.

01:20:19.022 --> 01:20:21.480
So if you think about this,
this is really quite startling,

01:20:21.480 --> 01:20:24.420
if you think of yourself
as actually a pattern

01:20:24.420 --> 01:20:28.830
moving through time and space,
sucking up energy and material

01:20:28.830 --> 01:20:31.910
and reformulating
it to be yourself.

01:20:31.910 --> 01:20:34.860
And yet every seven
years, you have not

01:20:34.860 --> 01:20:37.350
a single atom, not
even in your bones,

01:20:37.350 --> 01:20:40.380
that you had seven years before.

01:20:40.380 --> 01:20:42.330
So this is a completely
different way

01:20:42.330 --> 01:20:44.190
of thinking about things.

01:20:44.190 --> 01:20:46.990
And things are going
wrong all the time.

01:20:46.990 --> 01:20:51.150
But in structures like this,
more things are going right.

01:20:51.150 --> 01:20:56.580
Well, something about SIMULA
got me thinking about computing.

01:21:00.840 --> 01:21:02.310
I suddenly realized
that computers

01:21:02.310 --> 01:21:04.950
were tiny, tiny, tiny,
little simple things.

01:21:07.557 --> 01:21:08.640
Physically, they were big.

01:21:08.640 --> 01:21:11.100
But compared to biology,
they were nothing.

01:21:11.100 --> 01:21:12.400
Hardly anything was going on.

01:21:12.400 --> 01:21:15.480
And the only reason
programming worked on them

01:21:15.480 --> 01:21:18.660
was because people were
hardly doing anything.

01:21:18.660 --> 01:21:21.987
And what came to my mind, it was
kind of like making a doghouse.

01:21:21.987 --> 01:21:23.820
Which, unless you make
it out of toothpicks,

01:21:23.820 --> 01:21:26.852
you can make a doghouse
out of almost anything,

01:21:26.852 --> 01:21:29.310
because the structure is so
simple compared to the strength

01:21:29.310 --> 01:21:30.980
of the materials.

01:21:30.980 --> 01:21:32.670
It's only when you
go to big things

01:21:32.670 --> 01:21:34.740
that you have to really
introduce architectures

01:21:34.740 --> 01:21:36.550
to make them work.

01:21:36.550 --> 01:21:39.120
And it suddenly
leapt into my mind

01:21:39.120 --> 01:21:43.380
that all of the
programming that I

01:21:43.380 --> 01:21:46.650
had known how to do in
the '60s was nothing, even

01:21:46.650 --> 01:21:48.540
though it seemed hard to do.

01:21:48.540 --> 01:21:51.120
But compared to what the
actual potential for building

01:21:51.120 --> 01:21:53.108
structures was, we were
going to have to shift

01:21:53.108 --> 01:21:54.400
to a different way of doing it.

01:21:54.400 --> 01:21:58.210
So I had one of my
Arthur Koestler bingos.

01:21:58.210 --> 01:22:00.990
And the bingo was that
the computers are not

01:22:00.990 --> 01:22:05.830
like the clocks they seem to
be, where the clock, if anything

01:22:05.830 --> 01:22:07.838
goes wrong, it stops.

01:22:07.838 --> 01:22:09.630
The clock is something
you fix, and a clock

01:22:09.630 --> 01:22:11.710
is something you build.

01:22:11.710 --> 01:22:14.220
But in a biological
organism, you don't fix them.

01:22:14.220 --> 01:22:16.178
You negotiate with them.

01:22:16.178 --> 01:22:17.220
And you don't build them.

01:22:17.220 --> 01:22:19.810
You grow them.

01:22:19.810 --> 01:22:23.250
And so this notion
of, why shouldn't we

01:22:23.250 --> 01:22:26.130
be building everything
on the computer out

01:22:26.130 --> 01:22:30.240
of self-healing
intercommunicating parts

01:22:30.240 --> 01:22:33.690
the way biology had?

01:22:33.690 --> 01:22:37.938
And so this was very
compelling to me.

01:22:37.938 --> 01:22:39.480
Because of course,
one of the reasons

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:45.300
why cells are a good idea is
that nature is very, very messy

01:22:45.300 --> 01:22:50.910
about doing biochemistry and
requires enormous numbers--

01:22:50.910 --> 01:22:54.750
for anybody who's
discovered it, you

01:22:54.750 --> 01:22:57.390
might know that there
are hundreds of reactions

01:22:57.390 --> 01:23:00.700
just to move electrons around.

01:23:00.700 --> 01:23:03.750
And the reason is that the
energy margins are so small,

01:23:03.750 --> 01:23:06.240
and that there was no grand
designer figuring out how

01:23:06.240 --> 01:23:07.900
to move these electrons along.

01:23:07.900 --> 01:23:10.080
Nature found out how, gradually.

01:23:10.080 --> 01:23:12.900
But biochemistry is very kludgy.

01:23:12.900 --> 01:23:16.420
And it requires
stable conditions.

01:23:16.420 --> 01:23:21.180
So what the cell did
was allow this kludgery

01:23:21.180 --> 01:23:23.670
to continue in a protected way.

01:23:23.670 --> 01:23:25.980
Because nature
rarely undoes things.

01:23:25.980 --> 01:23:28.290
It basically adds on
more kinds of things.

01:23:28.290 --> 01:23:30.930
And this was a way of
taking messy things that

01:23:30.930 --> 01:23:34.720
sort of worked and guaranteeing
that they would work better.

01:23:34.720 --> 01:23:36.630
And then eventually,
of course, these cells

01:23:36.630 --> 01:23:39.940
found out how to work together.

01:23:39.940 --> 01:23:45.105
And now, looking back again
at SIMULA, what SIMULA was

01:23:45.105 --> 01:23:48.120
was almost one of these things.

01:23:48.120 --> 01:23:53.790
And they had been thinking
about simulating oil refineries.

01:23:53.790 --> 01:23:56.670
And the thing that I
got out of it is, gee,

01:23:56.670 --> 01:23:59.430
why not simulate
programmatic things?

01:23:59.430 --> 01:24:02.240
Let's get rid of data
structures entirely.

01:24:02.240 --> 01:24:03.930
We'll simulate those.

01:24:03.930 --> 01:24:05.860
We can simulate
servers on a network.

01:24:05.860 --> 01:24:08.550
In fact, these kinds
of systems are kind

01:24:08.550 --> 01:24:10.230
of like servers on a network.

01:24:10.230 --> 01:24:12.660
We can simulate
control structures.

01:24:12.660 --> 01:24:14.040
We can simulate
physical objects.

01:24:14.040 --> 01:24:15.270
We can simulate biology.

01:24:15.270 --> 01:24:17.760
In fact, simulation,
again, thinking

01:24:17.760 --> 01:24:19.620
about what Sketchpad
was about, is

01:24:19.620 --> 01:24:21.990
one of the dominant
metaphors that is

01:24:21.990 --> 01:24:23.910
in the destiny of the computer.

01:24:26.880 --> 01:24:28.950
And there's one thing you
can do with a computer

01:24:28.950 --> 01:24:30.990
that biology doesn't do well.

01:24:30.990 --> 01:24:34.080
And that is, you
can share the DNA.

01:24:34.080 --> 01:24:37.420
Biology does it by
copying the DNA.

01:24:37.420 --> 01:24:39.360
And so when you want
to replace something,

01:24:39.360 --> 01:24:41.250
it is really a
complex operation.

01:24:41.250 --> 01:24:45.390
People are discovering how to
do that now for cystic fibrosis.

01:24:45.390 --> 01:24:48.390
In the computer, you can
actually share the DNA

01:24:48.390 --> 01:24:52.440
by extracting it and having
all the cells look to a master

01:24:52.440 --> 01:24:52.950
copy.

01:24:52.950 --> 01:24:55.350
So this is very powerful,
because it gives you

01:24:55.350 --> 01:25:01.290
meta control over what the
system actually is all about.

01:25:01.290 --> 01:25:05.700
So that was my first revelation.

01:25:05.700 --> 01:25:07.740
It came in the end of '66.

01:25:07.740 --> 01:25:09.450
And a little bit
later, Dave Evans

01:25:09.450 --> 01:25:12.510
got me a consulting
job with a guy

01:25:12.510 --> 01:25:14.520
by the name of Ed
Cheadle, who wanted

01:25:14.520 --> 01:25:18.000
to build a little
computer for engineers.

01:25:18.000 --> 01:25:21.090
And eventually, we wound
up building a version

01:25:21.090 --> 01:25:24.180
of it called the FLEX Machine.

01:25:24.180 --> 01:25:26.730
This is a picture of
it on its own display.

01:25:30.570 --> 01:25:34.260
And we knew that it wasn't
the first personal computer,

01:25:34.260 --> 01:25:37.260
because Wes Clark and
Charlie Molnar had already

01:25:37.260 --> 01:25:42.193
done one called the LINC at
Lincoln Labs in the early '60s.

01:25:42.193 --> 01:25:43.860
And we hadn't thought
about this machine

01:25:43.860 --> 01:25:45.870
until Ed Cheadle
wanted to do one.

01:25:45.870 --> 01:25:48.480
And then we realized that
somebody had already done it.

01:25:48.480 --> 01:25:51.630
And we were able to learn quite
a bit of what you might try

01:25:51.630 --> 01:25:54.310
and do on such a small machine.

01:25:54.310 --> 01:25:57.990
This machine, by the way,
was made in some quantity.

01:25:57.990 --> 01:25:59.730
And a lot of
fundamental inventions

01:25:59.730 --> 01:26:02.890
were done on it, like the
things that look like deck tapes

01:26:02.890 --> 01:26:03.390
there.

01:26:03.390 --> 01:26:05.970
But deck tapes were actually
invented for this machine

01:26:05.970 --> 01:26:09.150
by Wes Clark.

01:26:09.150 --> 01:26:10.650
And here's a shadowy figure.

01:26:10.650 --> 01:26:13.680
Now, this actually isn't
a picture of Bob Barton.

01:26:13.680 --> 01:26:16.200
Because, as far as
I know, his pictures

01:26:16.200 --> 01:26:19.950
have never shown
up on photographs.

01:26:19.950 --> 01:26:23.100
He was called the Ghost Who
Walks because he was seen

01:26:23.100 --> 01:26:25.000
outside of Merrill
engineering building,

01:26:25.000 --> 01:26:27.420
and he was seen inside of
Merrill engineering building,

01:26:27.420 --> 01:26:30.060
but he was never seen
entering or leaving.

01:26:30.060 --> 01:26:31.410
Suddenly he would be there.

01:26:31.410 --> 01:26:35.920
And then just as suddenly,
he would not be there.

01:26:35.920 --> 01:26:39.570
He was the guy who designed
the Burroughs B5000,

01:26:39.570 --> 01:26:41.880
and I think was the
greatest computer

01:26:41.880 --> 01:26:44.130
designer who has ever lived.

01:26:44.130 --> 01:26:49.560
And his influence in
his own inimitable way

01:26:49.560 --> 01:26:54.040
had a lot to do with how this
little machine of Cheadle's

01:26:54.040 --> 01:26:56.350
and mine executed.

01:26:56.350 --> 01:27:00.300
We made it execute directly
one of the earliest

01:27:00.300 --> 01:27:03.090
object-oriented languages.

01:27:03.090 --> 01:27:05.360
And you see one of his
famous sayings up there.

01:27:05.360 --> 01:27:08.260
Systems programmers are
high priests of a low cult.

01:27:08.260 --> 01:27:13.150
So his idea was, if you have
to have an operating system,

01:27:13.150 --> 01:27:15.480
then you might be
designing wrong.

01:27:15.480 --> 01:27:19.060
And we realized that if you
had an object-oriented system

01:27:19.060 --> 01:27:21.060
that you really didn't
have to have an operating

01:27:21.060 --> 01:27:24.450
system, because it
actually would supply all

01:27:24.450 --> 01:27:27.300
of the things you normally
associated with an operating

01:27:27.300 --> 01:27:31.390
system, all by itself.

01:27:31.390 --> 01:27:36.270
And so as I said, Sketchpad
had the first windows.

01:27:36.270 --> 01:27:38.250
The first multiple
windows system I know of

01:27:38.250 --> 01:27:40.980
was Sketchpad 3 that was
done by Timothy Johnson.

01:27:40.980 --> 01:27:47.130
It showed you different views
of a three-dimensional object.

01:27:47.130 --> 01:27:52.410
And so we needed to
have a windowing system.

01:27:52.410 --> 01:27:55.470
This was done at the same time
as Ivan Sutherland and Bob

01:27:55.470 --> 01:27:58.200
Sproull at Harvard.

01:27:58.200 --> 01:28:00.300
We came up with a
clipping divider idea

01:28:00.300 --> 01:28:02.100
that was rather
similar to, I think,

01:28:02.100 --> 01:28:08.980
Danny Cohen's algorithm,
multiple views and so forth.

01:28:08.980 --> 01:28:12.240
Now, right as we were
working on this stuff,

01:28:12.240 --> 01:28:15.360
we were visited
by Doug Engelbart.

01:28:15.360 --> 01:28:18.780
And Doug, I'm sure you remember,
but when you traveled around

01:28:18.780 --> 01:28:22.050
back then, you used
to travel with--

01:28:22.050 --> 01:28:23.430
you didn't travel light.

01:28:23.430 --> 01:28:25.660
[LAUGHTER]

01:28:27.600 --> 01:28:29.440
This was before video and stuff.

01:28:29.440 --> 01:28:32.400
So he traveled with a
16-millimeter projector

01:28:32.400 --> 01:28:33.060
and a film.

01:28:33.060 --> 01:28:35.143
And the reason he traveled
with his own projector,

01:28:35.143 --> 01:28:37.817
he'd had it modified so
he could do stop frame.

01:28:37.817 --> 01:28:39.900
And the reason he did that
is that people were not

01:28:39.900 --> 01:28:42.768
used to looking at cursors.

01:28:42.768 --> 01:28:44.310
A lot of what he
wanted people to see

01:28:44.310 --> 01:28:46.500
was what people did with
the cursors on the screen.

01:28:46.500 --> 01:28:51.150
As I recall, the movie on
that one was a green display.

01:28:51.150 --> 01:28:53.130
Because I think what
you showed back then

01:28:53.130 --> 01:29:00.540
was the 3100, the control data
single user version of NLS.

01:29:00.540 --> 01:29:03.820
My memory has it being in
color, and it was green.

01:29:03.820 --> 01:29:06.310
So I think it was that
system that was shown.

01:29:06.310 --> 01:29:12.960
So this was the visit that
actually changed our lives.

01:29:12.960 --> 01:29:15.300
It certainly changed my life.

01:29:15.300 --> 01:29:19.230
Because what Engelbart
brought was not just

01:29:19.230 --> 01:29:22.350
a vision of interacting
with the system,

01:29:22.350 --> 01:29:26.012
but he had a philosophical
underpinning.

01:29:26.012 --> 01:29:27.710
And that philosophical
underpinning

01:29:27.710 --> 01:29:30.590
is as important today,
maybe more important today,

01:29:30.590 --> 01:29:32.635
even than it was then.

01:29:32.635 --> 01:29:34.760
One of the phrases that he
used that I particularly

01:29:34.760 --> 01:29:38.130
liked-- and I'm not sure
I still understand it.

01:29:38.130 --> 01:29:42.050
But on the other hand, when
you're listening to this stuff,

01:29:42.050 --> 01:29:44.850
total understanding
is not necessary.

01:29:44.850 --> 01:29:48.640
But his phrase was, thought
vectors and concept space.

01:29:48.640 --> 01:29:53.420
And what he meant, I think,
was that what you're doing

01:29:53.420 --> 01:29:59.720
is creating an extension
of the kinds of spaces

01:29:59.720 --> 01:30:03.500
that you think in terms
of inside of your head.

01:30:03.500 --> 01:30:08.660
So it was actually, you were
creating an augmentation

01:30:08.660 --> 01:30:11.300
of the ways of thinking and
the ways of representing

01:30:11.300 --> 01:30:15.350
and the ways of
associating that was now

01:30:15.350 --> 01:30:18.320
going to be extended, in a way,
somewhat analogous to the way

01:30:18.320 --> 01:30:20.660
writing is extended,
but something more

01:30:20.660 --> 01:30:23.180
like the way we actually think.

01:30:23.180 --> 01:30:24.860
And that what we're
really interested in

01:30:24.860 --> 01:30:27.740
is, in particular,
the collisions

01:30:27.740 --> 01:30:30.210
of different kinds of ideas.

01:30:30.210 --> 01:30:33.260
So if you think of
Koestler's diagram there,

01:30:33.260 --> 01:30:35.270
you can see that
one of the things

01:30:35.270 --> 01:30:38.240
that you would like to
do for maximum creativity

01:30:38.240 --> 01:30:41.330
and getting out of your
rut is to constantly

01:30:41.330 --> 01:30:45.680
have the system help you see
different sides to things,

01:30:45.680 --> 01:30:48.650
to remind you that
you're in a context

01:30:48.650 --> 01:30:52.185
and that there are
other contexts.

01:30:52.185 --> 01:30:58.040
But anyway, his talk
was so fantastic

01:30:58.040 --> 01:31:00.260
that, like all
really good talks,

01:31:00.260 --> 01:31:04.430
I think everybody came away from
that realizing that this was

01:31:04.430 --> 01:31:09.350
an actual turning point in the
history of design for computer

01:31:09.350 --> 01:31:09.850
science.

01:31:09.850 --> 01:31:13.370
And I think all of us were
very interested in trying

01:31:13.370 --> 01:31:15.770
to do something with that.

01:31:15.770 --> 01:31:17.750
I'm not going to show
you the movie because you

01:31:17.750 --> 01:31:25.013
saw yesterday, of the 1968
demonstration in San Francisco.

01:31:25.013 --> 01:31:26.180
Those of us who were there--

01:31:26.180 --> 01:31:29.240
I was sick as a dog,
but I went anyway.

01:31:29.240 --> 01:31:31.770
I had strep throat
and maybe pneumonia,

01:31:31.770 --> 01:31:33.680
but I wasn't going to
miss it for the world.

01:31:33.680 --> 01:31:35.760
And it still sticks in my mind.

01:31:35.760 --> 01:31:40.730
It was one of the greatest
expositions of a new set

01:31:40.730 --> 01:31:41.990
of ideas that I've ever seen.

01:31:41.990 --> 01:31:44.428
I think everybody who
was there remembers.

01:31:44.428 --> 01:31:45.970
And there were a
lot of people there.

01:31:45.970 --> 01:31:49.760
There must have been somewhere
between 2,000 and 3,000 people

01:31:49.760 --> 01:31:51.860
witnessing that.

01:31:51.860 --> 01:31:56.090
Actually, I remember, that
was where I met Andy van Dam.

01:31:56.090 --> 01:31:58.280
And for those of you
who have only known

01:31:58.280 --> 01:32:00.770
Andy in the last
20 years or so, I

01:32:00.770 --> 01:32:04.595
want to reveal to you that,
in 1968, Andy was a hippie.

01:32:04.595 --> 01:32:05.810
[LAUGHTER]

01:32:05.810 --> 01:32:08.630
He had long hair, and at
the very least a goatee,

01:32:08.630 --> 01:32:10.350
if not more.

01:32:10.350 --> 01:32:15.540
And he was a wonderful, angry
young man at that point.

01:32:15.540 --> 01:32:18.170
And I remember him being
both tremendously impressed

01:32:18.170 --> 01:32:22.730
with the Engelbart system
and amazed that it actually

01:32:22.730 --> 01:32:24.990
did what it did.

01:32:24.990 --> 01:32:29.500
That was my impression
of Andy back then.

01:32:29.500 --> 01:32:34.670
And right around this time,
I visited Ivan Sutherland

01:32:34.670 --> 01:32:38.000
at Harvard and met Bob Sproull.

01:32:38.000 --> 01:32:42.200
Bob Sproull then
was an undergraduate

01:32:42.200 --> 01:32:44.570
who was simultaneously
graduating from Harvard,

01:32:44.570 --> 01:32:47.075
I believe, editor
of the yearbook.

01:32:47.075 --> 01:32:49.910
But he spent almost
24 hours a day

01:32:49.910 --> 01:32:53.210
helping to wire up
the clipping divider

01:32:53.210 --> 01:32:56.540
for this first head-mounted
display machine.

01:32:56.540 --> 01:32:58.990
And I understand that he
slowed down quite a bit.

01:32:58.990 --> 01:33:04.340
He only works 20 hours a day
now instead of 23 and 1/2.

01:33:08.090 --> 01:33:12.920
Now just one little plug here
for why all this stuff worked.

01:33:12.920 --> 01:33:15.800
I think these are the most
enlightened funders that we

01:33:15.800 --> 01:33:18.680
know of in this half century.

01:33:18.680 --> 01:33:23.910
And I haven't heard Licklider's
name mentioned too much today,

01:33:23.910 --> 01:33:26.030
although Lee mentioned it.

01:33:26.030 --> 01:33:30.680
A lot of this was because of
his understanding, personality,

01:33:30.680 --> 01:33:32.360
and vision.

01:33:32.360 --> 01:33:37.250
And the succession of the ARPA
funders during this golden age,

01:33:37.250 --> 01:33:41.330
before it became DARPA, were
Licklider, Ivan Sutherland,

01:33:41.330 --> 01:33:44.900
when he was only 26,
Taylor, who later went on

01:33:44.900 --> 01:33:48.020
to be the founder of the
computer stuff at Xerox PARC,

01:33:48.020 --> 01:33:50.390
and Larry Roberts.

01:33:50.390 --> 01:33:54.120
And their basic idea is to fund
people instead of projects,

01:33:54.120 --> 01:33:56.360
one of the great
ideas of all time,

01:33:56.360 --> 01:34:00.210
and fund directions
instead of goals.

01:34:00.210 --> 01:34:02.360
I don't think anybody
else would have

01:34:02.360 --> 01:34:05.990
funded Engelbart in those days.

01:34:05.990 --> 01:34:10.400
Because I believe he must have
appeared, to most people--

01:34:10.400 --> 01:34:12.590
in fact, I know he
appeared to most people--

01:34:12.590 --> 01:34:16.650
as somebody who was completely
deranged and out of his mind.

01:34:16.650 --> 01:34:20.810
And it's a real pity that the
ARPA funding from the '60s

01:34:20.810 --> 01:34:23.180
didn't carry over
into the '70s, or I

01:34:23.180 --> 01:34:26.330
think history would have
been quite different today.

01:34:26.330 --> 01:34:28.700
Because sometimes the people
who can see the farthest

01:34:28.700 --> 01:34:31.460
and see the deepest
are the ones that

01:34:31.460 --> 01:34:38.450
are most difficult for the
bean counters to understand.

01:34:38.450 --> 01:34:41.810
So I met Marvin back then.

01:34:44.750 --> 01:34:48.560
So a typical Marvin
comment, I'd like to say you

01:34:48.560 --> 01:34:53.240
don't understand anything if
you only understand it one way.

01:34:53.240 --> 01:34:56.150
And he gave a talk on
education and mentioned

01:34:56.150 --> 01:34:58.550
some of the stuff he'd been
doing with Seymour Papert.

01:34:58.550 --> 01:35:01.640
And the talk was so
interesting, I read his book.

01:35:01.640 --> 01:35:04.500
My other major was
in mathematics.

01:35:04.500 --> 01:35:09.320
So I found in this book, besides
a lot of finite automata stuff,

01:35:09.320 --> 01:35:11.720
some really interesting
ways of thinking

01:35:11.720 --> 01:35:15.020
about universal computing.

01:35:15.020 --> 01:35:18.560
Not just Turing machines, but
Marvin did a very funny thing

01:35:18.560 --> 01:35:20.540
of, when he was
explaining Godel numbers

01:35:20.540 --> 01:35:26.180
and how Godel made a kind of a
list structure out of exponents

01:35:26.180 --> 01:35:29.450
of factorizations of integers.

01:35:29.450 --> 01:35:33.230
Marvin built an entire LISP
system in one of the chapters

01:35:33.230 --> 01:35:36.710
in this book using that
as the representation.

01:35:36.710 --> 01:35:38.570
It was a really weird
way of learning LISP,

01:35:38.570 --> 01:35:43.590
but it got me interested in this
way of thinking about things.

01:35:43.590 --> 01:35:50.420
And let's show the
next little film.

01:35:50.420 --> 01:35:52.030
Then I went to Rand Corporation.

01:35:52.030 --> 01:35:55.150
This is still in '68.

01:35:55.150 --> 01:35:57.320
And I saw this system.

01:35:57.320 --> 01:36:00.380
Yep, go ahead and run it.

01:36:00.380 --> 01:36:01.160
It's got sound.

01:36:01.160 --> 01:36:01.827
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:36:01.827 --> 01:36:04.580
- First, we erase a flow arrow,
then move the connector out

01:36:04.580 --> 01:36:08.000
of the way so that we may
draw a box in its place.

01:36:08.000 --> 01:36:09.050
KAY: Sees he wants a box.

01:36:09.050 --> 01:36:09.990
It makes ones for him.

01:36:09.990 --> 01:36:12.823
Now it's recognizing
his printing.

01:36:12.823 --> 01:36:14.240
- The printing in
the box is being

01:36:14.240 --> 01:36:17.690
used as commentary
only in this case.

01:36:17.690 --> 01:36:20.930
The box is slightly too large
so we may change its size.

01:36:20.930 --> 01:36:23.180
KAY: That's where modern day
window control came from,

01:36:23.180 --> 01:36:23.870
literally.

01:36:23.870 --> 01:36:26.820
- Then draw a flow from
the connector to the box.

01:36:29.730 --> 01:36:32.340
Attach a decision
element to the box,

01:36:32.340 --> 01:36:35.880
and draw a flow from it to scan.

01:36:35.880 --> 01:36:37.770
We then erase the
flow arrows attached

01:36:37.770 --> 01:36:41.550
to the process Post New
Area, and move the box

01:36:41.550 --> 01:36:42.972
to a new position.

01:36:47.720 --> 01:36:51.756
This allows us to
draw a new box.

01:36:51.756 --> 01:36:53.250
[END PLAYBACK]

01:36:53.250 --> 01:36:57.980
KAY: Okay, now you may have
seen character recognition done

01:36:57.980 --> 01:37:04.970
worse on some products
today, partly because it's--

01:37:04.970 --> 01:37:08.280
as Roger was saying, is that
they weren't trying to do the--

01:37:08.280 --> 01:37:10.520
this was an 80-20
thing, where it

01:37:10.520 --> 01:37:14.600
did what it did essentially
perfectly, almost perfectly.

01:37:14.600 --> 01:37:17.060
You had to learn a little bit.

01:37:17.060 --> 01:37:20.020
It did what it did, and
there were a bunch of things

01:37:20.020 --> 01:37:22.350
that it wouldn't do.

01:37:22.350 --> 01:37:25.400
But the result of it was that
the character recognition part

01:37:25.400 --> 01:37:28.250
on this particular interface--
which the system was called

01:37:28.250 --> 01:37:29.960
the GRAALL system--

01:37:29.960 --> 01:37:33.590
the character recognition
was almost foolproof

01:37:33.590 --> 01:37:38.930
and very, very transparent
in the user interface.

01:37:38.930 --> 01:37:40.430
And there were some
further advances

01:37:40.430 --> 01:37:44.030
done at Harvard and
elsewhere on doing this.

01:37:44.030 --> 01:37:48.270
And this really
struck me as a very,

01:37:48.270 --> 01:37:50.570
I thought of as a kind
of an intimate way

01:37:50.570 --> 01:37:55.760
of interacting with a computer.

01:37:55.760 --> 01:37:58.665
And then just a
little bit after that,

01:37:58.665 --> 01:38:00.290
a couple of weeks
after that, I visited

01:38:00.290 --> 01:38:03.800
Seymour Papert for the first
time out here in Lexington

01:38:03.800 --> 01:38:06.410
and saw his work with children.

01:38:06.410 --> 01:38:08.016
And that was another one--

01:38:08.016 --> 01:38:11.000
see, for me, the
'60s was pretty much

01:38:11.000 --> 01:38:14.360
a continual series
of holy shits,

01:38:14.360 --> 01:38:15.740
just one after the other.

01:38:18.830 --> 01:38:23.330
I think part of it is the
golden nostalgia, I'm sure,

01:38:23.330 --> 01:38:24.920
from old farts.

01:38:24.920 --> 01:38:28.850
But also I think actually this
stuff is still pretty neat,

01:38:28.850 --> 01:38:30.212
even today.

01:38:30.212 --> 01:38:31.670
I think the GRAALL
system was neat.

01:38:31.670 --> 01:38:32.795
I think Sketchpad was neat.

01:38:32.795 --> 01:38:35.030
I think NLS was really neat.

01:38:35.030 --> 01:38:38.120
It was really a joy
to learn NLS have

01:38:38.120 --> 01:38:40.670
a sense of what it
could mean to be

01:38:40.670 --> 01:38:45.470
a virtuoso in dealing with
your external representations.

01:38:45.470 --> 01:38:49.880
So Papert had another profound
idea, and that was that,

01:38:49.880 --> 01:38:54.890
whatever the computer was, it
wasn't really a tool or even

01:38:54.890 --> 01:38:58.490
a vehicle, in the sense
of being an automobile,

01:38:58.490 --> 01:39:03.410
as compared to IBM's railroads.

01:39:03.410 --> 01:39:05.660
But Papert thought
of it as being

01:39:05.660 --> 01:39:07.430
a medium for powerful ideas.

01:39:07.430 --> 01:39:11.210
Now Nicholas was hedging on
the definition of a medium.

01:39:11.210 --> 01:39:13.830
I like Neil
Postman's definition.

01:39:13.830 --> 01:39:17.270
Neil Postman says
technology is a machinery.

01:39:17.270 --> 01:39:19.370
Like the printing
press is a machine,

01:39:19.370 --> 01:39:20.630
the computer is a machine.

01:39:20.630 --> 01:39:22.820
And he says, media
are the purposes

01:39:22.820 --> 01:39:25.550
to which we put that machinery.

01:39:25.550 --> 01:39:28.190
So for him the media
on the printing press

01:39:28.190 --> 01:39:32.210
are things like Bibles,
indulgences, money,

01:39:32.210 --> 01:39:36.830
comic books, romance novels,
pornography, political essays,

01:39:36.830 --> 01:39:38.360
scientific essays.

01:39:38.360 --> 01:39:41.570
So it was a wide range of
media that is all printed,

01:39:41.570 --> 01:39:43.880
all represented,
all brought forth,

01:39:43.880 --> 01:39:45.870
by the same piece of technology.

01:39:45.870 --> 01:39:49.400
So if you think of media
as purposeful aims,

01:39:49.400 --> 01:39:52.833
you see the strongest things
that Ivan Sutherland and Doug

01:39:52.833 --> 01:39:54.500
Engelbart and some
of these other people

01:39:54.500 --> 01:39:56.480
did were actually
creating media.

01:39:56.480 --> 01:39:58.310
I didn't realize
that until I saw

01:39:58.310 --> 01:40:00.035
Seymour working with the kids.

01:40:00.035 --> 01:40:02.390
And he realized that
there's only one way

01:40:02.390 --> 01:40:03.720
of thinking about this, to me.

01:40:03.720 --> 01:40:07.108
And that was, it was a kind
of medium being brought forth

01:40:07.108 --> 01:40:08.900
from machinery, and it
was going to be very

01:40:08.900 --> 01:40:10.560
much like the printing press.

01:40:10.560 --> 01:40:18.890
So flying back to Utah,
I sketched this thing,

01:40:18.890 --> 01:40:21.830
which I later made a cardboard
model of, which later

01:40:21.830 --> 01:40:24.080
became called the Dynabook.

01:40:24.080 --> 01:40:25.560
And it was hollow.

01:40:25.560 --> 01:40:27.500
I filled it up with
lead pellets to see

01:40:27.500 --> 01:40:29.660
how heavy you could make
it before people didn't

01:40:29.660 --> 01:40:31.650
want to carry it anymore.

01:40:31.650 --> 01:40:33.380
And the result was
about two pounds.

01:40:35.955 --> 01:40:38.525
So we had this
notion that, if you

01:40:38.525 --> 01:40:42.890
were going to have an
intimate medium, then

01:40:42.890 --> 01:40:46.575
you'd have to do mundane
things, casual things on it.

01:40:46.575 --> 01:40:48.200
So we asked the
question, what would it

01:40:48.200 --> 01:40:52.915
be like to take a
grocery list and actually

01:40:52.915 --> 01:40:54.290
put it on a computer
and actually

01:40:54.290 --> 01:40:58.010
carry it into the grocery store
and be willing to carry it out

01:40:58.010 --> 01:40:59.900
with two bags of groceries?

01:40:59.900 --> 01:41:03.840
What kind of computer
would that have to be?

01:41:03.840 --> 01:41:06.080
And one of things
that was happening

01:41:06.080 --> 01:41:09.680
in the end of the
'60s was that ARPA

01:41:09.680 --> 01:41:11.420
had been doing the ARPANET.

01:41:11.420 --> 01:41:16.730
But Larry Roberts and some of
the other people who were--

01:41:16.730 --> 01:41:19.400
Bob Kahn and other people--
who were driving the ARPANET

01:41:19.400 --> 01:41:22.900
were also thinking of
packet radio back then.

01:41:22.900 --> 01:41:27.440
So in '69, I remember
driving around,

01:41:27.440 --> 01:41:31.070
I think it was a Ford van with
a model 33 teletype in the back

01:41:31.070 --> 01:41:34.430
and all kinds of radio
equipment, communicating with--

01:41:34.430 --> 01:41:36.710
I think it was actually
Engelbart's computer

01:41:36.710 --> 01:41:45.040
at SRI by radio at 10 characters
a second on this model 33

01:41:45.040 --> 01:41:45.620
teletype.

01:41:45.620 --> 01:41:50.210
So there's a whole
sense that computing

01:41:50.210 --> 01:41:51.780
was going to get to be mobile.

01:41:51.780 --> 01:41:54.890
And I think many you may
know that Larry Roberts

01:41:54.890 --> 01:41:57.170
last set of things that
he was interested in doing

01:41:57.170 --> 01:42:02.810
were to have a handheld radio
terminal that would be portable

01:42:02.810 --> 01:42:08.290
and would connect you through
packet radio to the ARPANET.

01:42:08.290 --> 01:42:09.290
And the other thing is--

01:42:09.290 --> 01:42:11.900
I actually tried to
get the picture here.

01:42:11.900 --> 01:42:15.570
But Lee, I'm sorry, we
actually were thinking

01:42:15.570 --> 01:42:16.820
of this thing being networked.

01:42:16.820 --> 01:42:20.300
Because I think networking
was part of the ARPA dream.

01:42:20.300 --> 01:42:22.820
And the first cartoon I
ever did of this machine,

01:42:22.820 --> 01:42:24.260
I'll show you later.

01:42:24.260 --> 01:42:26.840
It shows two kids
playing Spacewar!

01:42:26.840 --> 01:42:28.370
with each other,
each on their own

01:42:28.370 --> 01:42:30.295
Dynabook,
communicating by radio.

01:42:34.850 --> 01:42:43.040
So just having these ideas
here led to something

01:42:43.040 --> 01:42:44.240
kind of interesting.

01:42:44.240 --> 01:42:48.680
And that is, it struck me that
having a time-sharing graphics

01:42:48.680 --> 01:42:51.950
terminal, this desktop computer
that Cheadle and I had been

01:42:51.950 --> 01:42:54.650
working on and this
cardboard model,

01:42:54.650 --> 01:42:57.710
reminded me of something that
had already happened hundreds

01:42:57.710 --> 01:42:58.520
of years before.

01:42:58.520 --> 01:43:02.660
And that was the
development of printing.

01:43:02.660 --> 01:43:06.980
So on the far left
is a manuscript book

01:43:06.980 --> 01:43:09.170
chained to its little desk.

01:43:09.170 --> 01:43:11.600
And if you've ever been
in a medieval library--

01:43:11.600 --> 01:43:13.820
they have one
still in Florence--

01:43:13.820 --> 01:43:15.380
they're like the
size of this room

01:43:15.380 --> 01:43:17.540
or maybe twice the
size of this room.

01:43:17.540 --> 01:43:21.620
But they only have
about 70 or 80 books.

01:43:21.620 --> 01:43:24.590
One of the largest
libraries in Europe in 1400

01:43:24.590 --> 01:43:25.610
was the Vatican Library.

01:43:25.610 --> 01:43:28.100
It had 372 books.

01:43:28.100 --> 01:43:30.860
The richest man in France, who
was the brother of the King,

01:43:30.860 --> 01:43:33.590
was a bibliophile and
had books made for him.

01:43:33.590 --> 01:43:37.850
He had 154 when he died in 1435.

01:43:37.850 --> 01:43:39.290
So these books
cost the equivalent

01:43:39.290 --> 01:43:41.150
of millions of dollars.

01:43:41.150 --> 01:43:46.310
And they are so rare that only
institutions could afford them.

01:43:46.310 --> 01:43:49.580
In 1454, at the
Nuremberg Book Fair,

01:43:49.580 --> 01:43:52.040
Gutenberg showed 20 bibles.

01:43:52.040 --> 01:43:54.000
And people marveled
at two things.

01:43:54.000 --> 01:43:57.500
One is how alike the
bibles were to each other.

01:43:57.500 --> 01:44:00.050
That was a really new--
you could look at one page

01:44:00.050 --> 01:44:03.590
and it looked just like the
same page on another Bible.

01:44:03.590 --> 01:44:05.630
They'd never seen that before.

01:44:05.630 --> 01:44:08.450
And the other thing was that
the costs had come way down.

01:44:08.450 --> 01:44:13.130
These Bibles only cost three
years of a clerk's wages, maybe

01:44:13.130 --> 01:44:14.660
$60,000 today.

01:44:14.660 --> 01:44:17.930
And so you could think of those
as being workstations, kind

01:44:17.930 --> 01:44:24.080
of things that smaller
institutions and towns

01:44:24.080 --> 01:44:25.950
and some individuals could own.

01:44:25.950 --> 01:44:29.270
And you'll notice that the
Gutenberg Bible was illuminated

01:44:29.270 --> 01:44:32.000
because they made
every effort to make

01:44:32.000 --> 01:44:35.900
it look like the manuscript
books on the left.

01:44:35.900 --> 01:44:39.620
And then 50 years later, to
me, is when printing actually

01:44:39.620 --> 01:44:40.770
happened.

01:44:40.770 --> 01:44:44.120
And we have books that,
instead of being this size,

01:44:44.120 --> 01:44:46.790
are now this size.

01:44:46.790 --> 01:44:49.610
These are printed by Aldus,
whose last name was not

01:44:49.610 --> 01:44:50.330
PageMaker.

01:44:50.330 --> 01:44:52.890
[LAUGHTER]

01:44:52.890 --> 01:44:54.420
His name was Aldus Manutius.

01:44:54.420 --> 01:44:57.020
But he was the person
that the Aldus Corporation

01:44:57.020 --> 01:44:58.880
took their name from.

01:44:58.880 --> 01:45:02.090
And he was a Venetian printer
who had been a scholar.

01:45:02.090 --> 01:45:04.400
And he went out
and decided books

01:45:04.400 --> 01:45:06.890
should be this size because
he went out and measured

01:45:06.890 --> 01:45:10.310
saddlebags in Venice,
because the first offering

01:45:10.310 --> 01:45:13.910
from his printing press in
1495 was called the Portable

01:45:13.910 --> 01:45:15.280
Library.

01:45:15.280 --> 01:45:16.910
And instead of
printing just one book,

01:45:16.910 --> 01:45:21.290
one kind of book, the Bible,
Aldus, over the next 35 years,

01:45:21.290 --> 01:45:24.530
printed 40,000 different titles.

01:45:24.530 --> 01:45:27.278
He and his sons went around and
gathered up all of the writing

01:45:27.278 --> 01:45:29.820
that the Greeks and the Romans
had done that they could find.

01:45:29.820 --> 01:45:32.010
They printed each one of them.

01:45:32.010 --> 01:45:33.830
And many of the fonts
that we have today

01:45:33.830 --> 01:45:35.900
were invented by
Aldus in order to make

01:45:35.900 --> 01:45:38.850
this stuff more readable.

01:45:38.850 --> 01:45:44.030
Aldus is also the inventor
of something quite unusual,

01:45:44.030 --> 01:45:46.250
and that is page numbers.

01:45:46.250 --> 01:45:49.490
Page numbers went in
to books the first time

01:45:49.490 --> 01:45:52.970
ever 65 years
after the invention

01:45:52.970 --> 01:45:55.326
of the printing press.

01:45:55.326 --> 01:45:59.180
So they went in in
1516, and we know why.

01:45:59.180 --> 01:46:02.012
They did not go in
there for sequencing.

01:46:02.012 --> 01:46:03.470
Because the way
sequencing was done

01:46:03.470 --> 01:46:09.020
was to print the word on the
next page down in the last line

01:46:09.020 --> 01:46:12.940
so you could see how
the pages went together.

01:46:12.940 --> 01:46:16.550
It did not go in there in
order to refer to other books,

01:46:16.550 --> 01:46:19.220
because there were no really
standard editions of books.

01:46:19.220 --> 01:46:23.120
Each press run was
2,000 to 5,000 copies.

01:46:23.120 --> 01:46:25.040
Page numbers went
in there partly

01:46:25.040 --> 01:46:28.530
at the instigation of Erasmus.

01:46:28.530 --> 01:46:30.440
And they went in there
because they were just

01:46:30.440 --> 01:46:32.900
starting to write
down arguments that

01:46:32.900 --> 01:46:34.850
were so complicated
that they wanted

01:46:34.850 --> 01:46:38.600
to refer people back to what
they had said previously.

01:46:38.600 --> 01:46:40.700
So the first page
numbering systems

01:46:40.700 --> 01:46:45.440
were done in order to, if you
will, hyperlink a piece of text

01:46:45.440 --> 01:46:47.370
within itself.

01:46:47.370 --> 01:46:49.790
So as you were following along
an argument sequentially,

01:46:49.790 --> 01:46:53.200
the author could say, and
as I said back on page 162,

01:46:53.200 --> 01:46:53.850
blah, blah.

01:46:53.850 --> 01:46:56.280
And you could go back
there and check it out.

01:46:56.280 --> 01:46:58.970
So page numbers
came in as people

01:46:58.970 --> 01:47:05.720
started realizing that the
reproducibility of the books

01:47:05.720 --> 01:47:08.420
allowed for much more
complicated arguments

01:47:08.420 --> 01:47:10.880
to put in there
and not be garbled

01:47:10.880 --> 01:47:14.460
by somebody who was trying
to copy the book by hand.

01:47:14.460 --> 01:47:16.370
And this led, in
turn, to this idea

01:47:16.370 --> 01:47:18.690
of trying to link the
arguments closer together.

01:47:18.690 --> 01:47:20.990
That was a very
interesting sidelight.

01:47:20.990 --> 01:47:25.340
And given that it took
65 years to happen

01:47:25.340 --> 01:47:27.500
from the invention of
the printing press,

01:47:27.500 --> 01:47:30.560
I also use it as a kind
of a metaphor for what

01:47:30.560 --> 01:47:33.440
we haven't thought of yet.

01:47:33.440 --> 01:47:35.260
Because clearly,
we are somewhere

01:47:35.260 --> 01:47:39.670
between the desktop computer and
this Dynabook kind of machine.

01:47:39.670 --> 01:47:41.500
And it's sobering to
realize that nothing

01:47:41.500 --> 01:47:45.440
in the history of printing
counted in those 50 years.

01:47:45.440 --> 01:47:47.680
There was a complete
transitional stage

01:47:47.680 --> 01:47:50.213
where almost everybody
was looking backwards.

01:47:50.213 --> 01:47:52.630
And if we look at the way most
people use computers today,

01:47:52.630 --> 01:47:54.370
it's really pathetic.

01:47:54.370 --> 01:47:57.010
Because most people do
not do hyperlinking.

01:47:57.010 --> 01:47:59.800
Most people do not use computers
the way Engelbart thought

01:47:59.800 --> 01:48:00.637
they should be used.

01:48:00.637 --> 01:48:02.470
And most people do not
use computers the way

01:48:02.470 --> 01:48:05.020
Ivan Sutherland thought
they should be used.

01:48:05.020 --> 01:48:09.340
What most people are doing
is imitating paper, either

01:48:09.340 --> 01:48:13.470
in prose, or imitating paper
in terms of accounting.

01:48:13.470 --> 01:48:16.750
And that echoes all the
way back to Sumerian times.

01:48:16.750 --> 01:48:18.430
Because as far as
anybody can tell,

01:48:18.430 --> 01:48:22.900
the invention of writing itself
came about first to account

01:48:22.900 --> 01:48:24.743
for the king's wine jars.

01:48:24.743 --> 01:48:26.410
And then a few hundred
years after that,

01:48:26.410 --> 01:48:28.077
they realized they
could also write down

01:48:28.077 --> 01:48:30.400
some of their poetry.

01:48:30.400 --> 01:48:32.960
So what's been going on in
computing has been very,

01:48:32.960 --> 01:48:35.560
very-- as McLuhan
once said, we're

01:48:35.560 --> 01:48:37.660
driving faster and
faster into the future,

01:48:37.660 --> 01:48:41.010
but steering only by looking
through the rear view mirror.

01:48:41.010 --> 01:48:43.600
And I think that's
been going on.

01:48:43.600 --> 01:48:46.660
We can think of a
corner being turned

01:48:46.660 --> 01:48:48.610
into the actual modern era.

01:48:48.610 --> 01:48:51.610
I just gave a talk
in Korea whose title

01:48:51.610 --> 01:48:55.280
was, "The Computer Revolution
Hasn't Happened yet."

01:48:55.280 --> 01:48:57.670
And I would say that
it hasn't happened yet

01:48:57.670 --> 01:49:02.410
until people start using
the computer for dealing

01:49:02.410 --> 01:49:06.820
with thought vectors and concept
space and dealing with the idea

01:49:06.820 --> 01:49:10.390
that you can have active dynamic
models of the things you're

01:49:10.390 --> 01:49:11.320
trying to represent.

01:49:14.180 --> 01:49:16.770
So that led to a kind
of a sense that there

01:49:16.770 --> 01:49:20.560
were three different religions.

01:49:20.560 --> 01:49:23.020
And we didn't know very much
about what the right-hand hand

01:49:23.020 --> 01:49:24.110
religion was going to be.

01:49:24.110 --> 01:49:25.818
But we could see that
they were certainly

01:49:25.818 --> 01:49:29.980
at a mainframe religion, of
which Tom Watson, Jr. was

01:49:29.980 --> 01:49:34.360
the Pope, that there
was going to be

01:49:34.360 --> 01:49:39.010
a desktop revolution and
religion, because Moore's law

01:49:39.010 --> 01:49:40.880
said that was going to happen.

01:49:40.880 --> 01:49:44.800
And then we had this
more mythical notion

01:49:44.800 --> 01:49:48.190
of pervasively networked
systems with computers that

01:49:48.190 --> 01:49:49.315
were kind of like clothing.

01:49:54.100 --> 01:49:56.420
So one of the last characters
I'm going to mention here

01:49:56.420 --> 01:50:02.720
is Butler Lampson, who
talked to even faster back

01:50:02.720 --> 01:50:05.520
in the '60s than he does today.

01:50:05.520 --> 01:50:09.260
And he once came to,
actually, a meeting

01:50:09.260 --> 01:50:13.670
on the ARPANET standards
and gave a talk

01:50:13.670 --> 01:50:16.070
on capability operating
systems, in which he had been

01:50:16.070 --> 01:50:18.063
working on one at Berkeley.

01:50:18.063 --> 01:50:20.480
And what had happened is, the
meeting went later and later

01:50:20.480 --> 01:50:21.750
and later.

01:50:21.750 --> 01:50:25.492
And finally, Butler said, well,
I have a plane I have to catch.

01:50:25.492 --> 01:50:27.200
And they said, when
do you have to leave?

01:50:27.200 --> 01:50:31.340
And he says, I have to
leave in about 15 minutes.

01:50:31.340 --> 01:50:34.190
And they said, well, would you
like to wait until next time

01:50:34.190 --> 01:50:34.940
to give your talk?

01:50:34.940 --> 01:50:37.430
And he says, oh, no, I'll
give my talk right now.

01:50:37.430 --> 01:50:39.170
And he got up and
gave an hour talk

01:50:39.170 --> 01:50:42.440
in 15 minutes on capability
operating systems.

01:50:42.440 --> 01:50:46.960
And it was one of the clearest
talks I have ever heard.

01:50:46.960 --> 01:50:50.690
And part of the idea
here is something

01:50:50.690 --> 01:50:56.780
that is part and parcel
of objects, which is--

01:50:56.780 --> 01:50:58.700
but in this kind of
operating system,

01:50:58.700 --> 01:51:01.920
you think of clients
and services.

01:51:01.920 --> 01:51:03.920
The services tend to be
generic, like printing

01:51:03.920 --> 01:51:07.040
and searching and updating
and viewing and delegating.

01:51:07.040 --> 01:51:10.020
And the clients are
different kinds of objects.

01:51:10.020 --> 01:51:13.460
Objects in this sense
are usually large things.

01:51:13.460 --> 01:51:16.260
They're the size
of Unix processes.

01:51:16.260 --> 01:51:19.370
But they have certain
things that they

01:51:19.370 --> 01:51:24.540
can do that are as
generic as possible.

01:51:24.540 --> 01:51:26.620
There are a variety of
these kinds of things.

01:51:26.620 --> 01:51:31.460
And then each one of these
clients of the services

01:51:31.460 --> 01:51:33.500
has a different
set of privileges.

01:51:33.500 --> 01:51:35.000
And the way it's
usually implemented

01:51:35.000 --> 01:51:38.630
is by adding a little bitmap
to a protected pointer that

01:51:38.630 --> 01:51:43.170
says which of these
privileges you have access to.

01:51:43.170 --> 01:51:47.960
So this is a very compelling
way of thinking about--

01:51:47.960 --> 01:51:50.340
in my mind, what I was
thinking about was,

01:51:50.340 --> 01:51:52.980
gee, everything
should be an object.

01:51:52.980 --> 01:51:54.800
And so the question
is, how could you

01:51:54.800 --> 01:51:57.380
implement this kind
of protection, which

01:51:57.380 --> 01:52:00.200
seemed very, very
useful and necessary,

01:52:00.200 --> 01:52:01.940
even for ordinary programming.

01:52:01.940 --> 01:52:05.490
How could you do it
in a fine-grained way?

01:52:05.490 --> 01:52:10.910
And that leads to this notion of
designing in terms of concepts,

01:52:10.910 --> 01:52:13.640
that you think of
behavioral concepts

01:52:13.640 --> 01:52:16.880
and transmit it into
object-oriented terms,

01:52:16.880 --> 01:52:19.550
you think of having
different kinds

01:52:19.550 --> 01:52:21.200
of things, different
classes of things,

01:52:21.200 --> 01:52:22.610
different types of things.

01:52:22.610 --> 01:52:24.830
But you're trying to
design everything in terms

01:52:24.830 --> 01:52:26.570
of these generic concepts.

01:52:26.570 --> 01:52:29.405
And what you wind
up with is something

01:52:29.405 --> 01:52:31.280
that, if you can limit
the number of concepts

01:52:31.280 --> 01:52:33.560
that you're dealing with,
then your design tasks

01:52:33.560 --> 01:52:37.260
become much easier.

01:52:37.260 --> 01:52:38.370
And finally, we had--

01:52:38.370 --> 01:52:44.660
although, again, I was only
thinking about the middle panel

01:52:44.660 --> 01:52:48.240
here as opposed to the
one on the far left.

01:52:48.240 --> 01:52:53.660
We realized that the ARPANET
would actually need objects,

01:52:53.660 --> 01:52:56.300
for sure, because
networking, particularly

01:52:56.300 --> 01:52:59.570
pervasive networking,
means heterogeneity.

01:52:59.570 --> 01:53:02.600
When you have heterogeneity,
you better not

01:53:02.600 --> 01:53:04.883
require people to
have to parse things

01:53:04.883 --> 01:53:06.050
in order to understand them.

01:53:06.050 --> 01:53:08.450
You really want to be able
to execute them in order

01:53:08.450 --> 01:53:11.270
to understand them so that they
have their own understanding

01:53:11.270 --> 01:53:12.890
with them.

01:53:12.890 --> 01:53:15.590
And we felt that
something like objects

01:53:15.590 --> 01:53:18.817
should actually be
adapted for the ARPANET.

01:53:18.817 --> 01:53:20.150
That was a little bit too early.

01:53:20.150 --> 01:53:22.810
That was brought up in one
of the meetings back there.

01:53:22.810 --> 01:53:25.050
It was felt to be a
little bit too early.

01:53:25.050 --> 01:53:27.680
But I think we can see from
the state of the internet

01:53:27.680 --> 01:53:32.810
today that this is absolutely
critical to get out of a data

01:53:32.810 --> 01:53:35.360
structure-oriented way
of doing things, which

01:53:35.360 --> 01:53:38.210
is kind of like
going back to MS-DOS

01:53:38.210 --> 01:53:42.890
and trying to get to a situation
where the objects that we

01:53:42.890 --> 01:53:45.860
interact with don't have to be
from the same object-oriented

01:53:45.860 --> 01:53:48.860
language, don't have
to have been created

01:53:48.860 --> 01:53:51.620
on the same kind of
machine, but nonetheless,

01:53:51.620 --> 01:53:54.593
can interact and
interoperate with each other.

01:53:57.480 --> 01:54:01.460
And then finally, towards
the end of the '60s,

01:54:01.460 --> 01:54:04.130
I finally went through
this half page of code

01:54:04.130 --> 01:54:07.010
in the LISP 1.5
Programmer's Manual

01:54:07.010 --> 01:54:09.140
and realized that McCarthy
had actually come up

01:54:09.140 --> 01:54:12.830
with an incredible way of
thinking about what programming

01:54:12.830 --> 01:54:13.840
should be.

01:54:13.840 --> 01:54:16.760
Now, he was doing it to prove
a different kind of point.

01:54:16.760 --> 01:54:19.280
And as he says himself,
it wasn't his fault

01:54:19.280 --> 01:54:22.570
that Steve Russell sat
down and made it work.

01:54:22.570 --> 01:54:28.010
But once it did get going, it
provided a completely different

01:54:28.010 --> 01:54:30.960
way of thinking about what it
means to implement programming

01:54:30.960 --> 01:54:31.460
languages.

01:54:31.460 --> 01:54:33.680
And I started thinking
very seriously

01:54:33.680 --> 01:54:36.020
about what an OOP
language would be like

01:54:36.020 --> 01:54:40.400
if it were set up like LISP.

01:54:40.400 --> 01:54:44.420
I think of this as the Maxwell's
equations for computers.

01:54:44.420 --> 01:54:46.910
Because everything you
can do with a computer

01:54:46.910 --> 01:54:49.910
and in a high level form
is there on that half page.

01:54:49.910 --> 01:54:51.920
So that's a very,
very interesting thing

01:54:51.920 --> 01:54:54.860
to spend a Sunday
afternoon going through.

01:54:54.860 --> 01:54:58.340
And I think everybody
who has gone through this

01:54:58.340 --> 01:55:01.700
and understood it, spent
those couple of hours

01:55:01.700 --> 01:55:05.120
to go through it, remembers that
time when they suddenly said,

01:55:05.120 --> 01:55:06.480
oh, wait a minute.

01:55:06.480 --> 01:55:07.940
Now, it wasn't
helped by the fact

01:55:07.940 --> 01:55:13.150
that this particular rendition
in this book has a bug in it.

01:55:13.150 --> 01:55:14.810
It has one bug.

01:55:14.810 --> 01:55:18.650
And that bug can cause us
an extra couple of hours

01:55:18.650 --> 01:55:20.990
to be taken until you're
quite sure that you understand

01:55:20.990 --> 01:55:23.110
all of it and that
there really is a bug.

01:55:23.110 --> 01:55:24.447
It turns out there is.

01:55:24.447 --> 01:55:25.280
And what is the bug?

01:55:25.280 --> 01:55:28.170
Well, of course, they left
out one set of parentheses.

01:55:28.170 --> 01:55:30.904
[LAUGHTER]

01:55:31.760 --> 01:55:36.405
Then the last part of
the '60s was realizing--

01:55:36.405 --> 01:55:38.030
and this again, I
want to say something

01:55:38.030 --> 01:55:41.810
about Doug Engelbart and NLS.

01:55:41.810 --> 01:55:46.490
The other part of his project
that was so impressive to me

01:55:46.490 --> 01:55:50.870
as a graduate student was, when
you visited the Augmentation

01:55:50.870 --> 01:55:54.680
Research Center at
SRI, what you found

01:55:54.680 --> 01:55:58.370
was kind of the epitome of
what a research project should

01:55:58.370 --> 01:55:59.960
be like.

01:55:59.960 --> 01:56:03.260
They had lofty goals,
lots of committed people.

01:56:03.260 --> 01:56:04.700
They were willing
to do any amount

01:56:04.700 --> 01:56:07.940
of building their own hardware
and software, which they did.

01:56:07.940 --> 01:56:09.440
They built their
own display system.

01:56:09.440 --> 01:56:12.380
And they built their
own IO devices.

01:56:12.380 --> 01:56:15.260
And they built most
of their software.

01:56:15.260 --> 01:56:18.050
And they helped modify the
operating system in order

01:56:18.050 --> 01:56:20.060
to make all of this stuff work.

01:56:20.060 --> 01:56:22.310
And actually, the other thing
I should have mentioned,

01:56:22.310 --> 01:56:23.870
I think everybody
noticed yesterday

01:56:23.870 --> 01:56:27.850
that NLS was kind of
zippy as far as response.

01:56:27.850 --> 01:56:30.020
Wouldn't you like to have
your desktop computer

01:56:30.020 --> 01:56:33.230
be as responsive
as that system was,

01:56:33.230 --> 01:56:36.700
instead of lumbering around
trying to bring stuff up?

01:56:36.700 --> 01:56:42.050
Well, I realized that, in order
to do a computer for kids,

01:56:42.050 --> 01:56:44.465
that what we had to do was
to build a bunch of them.

01:56:44.465 --> 01:56:46.340
And this is one of the
entries in my notebook

01:56:46.340 --> 01:56:49.820
back then of what
I wanted to build.

01:56:49.820 --> 01:56:54.800
And at that point,
Xerox PARC came along,

01:56:54.800 --> 01:56:57.440
and we got a chance to try
some of these ideas out.

01:56:57.440 --> 01:57:02.870
Now, to me, the moral of some
of this looking back in the past

01:57:02.870 --> 01:57:09.360
is that it is incredible how
powerful a new context is.

01:57:09.360 --> 01:57:11.540
And it's also incredible
how stultifying it

01:57:11.540 --> 01:57:14.210
is after a couple of years.

01:57:14.210 --> 01:57:16.138
I didn't realize until
looking back on this,

01:57:16.138 --> 01:57:17.930
but I think all of us
have had this feeling

01:57:17.930 --> 01:57:21.050
that the very things that
made our ideas work when we're

01:57:21.050 --> 01:57:24.500
in a context were also the ones
that held us up from thinking

01:57:24.500 --> 01:57:27.026
about other ideas.

01:57:27.026 --> 01:57:30.320
So these contexts wear out.

01:57:30.320 --> 01:57:33.020
But we can only think about
things in terms of context.

01:57:33.020 --> 01:57:35.030
When we go to one
context to another,

01:57:35.030 --> 01:57:37.363
we get life for a few years.

01:57:37.363 --> 01:57:38.780
But then there's
a syndrome, which

01:57:38.780 --> 01:57:42.260
I call the subgoals becoming
the goals, that sets in.

01:57:42.260 --> 01:57:44.510
Usually the subgoals
are hard enough

01:57:44.510 --> 01:57:46.110
to require a couple
of years work.

01:57:46.110 --> 01:57:48.568
And once you've been working
on them for a couple of years,

01:57:48.568 --> 01:57:50.870
they become the
goals of the project.

01:57:50.870 --> 01:57:53.300
And all of a sudden, it's hard
to remember why you started

01:57:53.300 --> 01:57:54.800
the project in the first place.

01:57:54.800 --> 01:57:56.300
So I think one of
the hardest things

01:57:56.300 --> 01:57:58.700
to do on these large
scale things that

01:57:58.700 --> 01:58:01.370
require so many different
things to be done over

01:58:01.370 --> 01:58:04.160
so many years is to
constantly be able to renew

01:58:04.160 --> 01:58:05.480
what the actual dream was.

01:58:08.100 --> 01:58:11.690
And it takes so long to
get people to understand

01:58:11.690 --> 01:58:12.870
what these ideas are.

01:58:12.870 --> 01:58:15.370
This is one of my
favorite slides,

01:58:15.370 --> 01:58:20.750
because it shows hand axes
from to prehistoric periods.

01:58:20.750 --> 01:58:22.640
The one on the left,
you might notice

01:58:22.640 --> 01:58:25.910
that when you chop
with it, it chops you.

01:58:25.910 --> 01:58:28.430
Because the place where
you grab it on the bottom

01:58:28.430 --> 01:58:30.120
there is pretty sharp.

01:58:30.120 --> 01:58:32.450
It's almost as sharp as the top.

01:58:32.450 --> 01:58:36.680
And then a little
bit later, somebody

01:58:36.680 --> 01:58:39.630
came up with a hand ax that
was blunt around the bottom

01:58:39.630 --> 01:58:41.690
so it wouldn't chop you as much.

01:58:41.690 --> 01:58:44.150
And according to
archaeologists, the period

01:58:44.150 --> 01:58:49.220
of time between these two
was about 200,000 years.

01:58:49.220 --> 01:58:53.450
So you can imagine, every
generation, probably somebody

01:58:53.450 --> 01:58:55.010
sitting around a
campfire chopping

01:58:55.010 --> 01:58:57.680
with one of these things
on the left, said, jeez,

01:58:57.680 --> 01:58:58.430
this is hurting.

01:58:58.430 --> 01:59:00.120
[LAUGHTER]

01:59:00.980 --> 01:59:02.190
I'll just chip this away.

01:59:02.190 --> 01:59:04.830
And probably got
burned at the stake

01:59:04.830 --> 01:59:07.770
for going against the
traditions of the tribe, which

01:59:07.770 --> 01:59:10.170
are probably something
puritanical like,

01:59:10.170 --> 01:59:15.210
work is good for you, sure
it hurts, and so forth.

01:59:15.210 --> 01:59:17.940
And then finally,
probably by an accident,

01:59:17.940 --> 01:59:22.860
some chieftain of some tribe
came up with the blunter end

01:59:22.860 --> 01:59:24.630
one and made it a
rule that everybody

01:59:24.630 --> 01:59:25.780
should have blunt end ones.

01:59:25.780 --> 01:59:28.020
And that's how this
change happened.

01:59:28.020 --> 01:59:32.490
So there's this low
pass filter to reality.

01:59:32.490 --> 01:59:35.340
And the biggest
example of it is what's

01:59:35.340 --> 01:59:38.520
happened to personal
computing since 1980.

01:59:38.520 --> 01:59:41.430
The low pass filter has been
so dominant that all we get

01:59:41.430 --> 01:59:45.893
is about a 60 cycle hum for most
of these ideas from the '60s.

01:59:45.893 --> 01:59:47.310
If we look at what
most people are

01:59:47.310 --> 01:59:48.768
doing with personal
computers, they

01:59:48.768 --> 01:59:51.270
have unbelievable resources.

01:59:51.270 --> 01:59:56.430
Engelbart's SDS 940
had 192 K and it

01:59:56.430 --> 02:00:00.620
ran 20 users as a time-sharing
system with 192 K.

02:00:00.620 --> 02:00:02.310
We've got megabytes.

02:00:02.310 --> 02:00:04.680
The system is much less
responsive than it is.

02:00:04.680 --> 02:00:07.860
The user interface
is worse than it was.

02:00:07.860 --> 02:00:13.920
And humankind has sat down for
one of these 20-year slumbers

02:00:13.920 --> 02:00:17.280
right now, bedazzled by
stuff that isn't very good.

02:00:19.840 --> 02:00:24.748
So the last slide is a
metaphor for this whole talk.

02:00:24.748 --> 02:00:26.790
And that is something that
was actually found out

02:00:26.790 --> 02:00:29.405
about frogs at MIT here.

02:00:29.405 --> 02:00:30.780
And that is that
frogs don't know

02:00:30.780 --> 02:00:32.950
what their food looks like.

02:00:32.950 --> 02:00:36.090
So if you take their normal
food, which are flies,

02:00:36.090 --> 02:00:37.950
paralyze them with
a little chloroform

02:00:37.950 --> 02:00:40.335
and put the flies out
in front of the frog,

02:00:40.335 --> 02:00:41.460
the frog will not eat them.

02:00:41.460 --> 02:00:43.680
The frog will just
starve to death.

02:00:43.680 --> 02:00:46.290
But if you flip a piece
of cardboard at the frog,

02:00:46.290 --> 02:00:48.510
it will try and
eat it every time.

02:00:48.510 --> 02:00:54.630
Because what it thinks food is
an oblong thing that is moving.

02:00:54.630 --> 02:00:58.230
And I think this is a good
metaphor for us scientists.

02:00:58.230 --> 02:01:04.680
Because the way I think of the
flies is that they're ideas.

02:01:04.680 --> 02:01:07.660
The ideas are in front
of us all of the time,

02:01:07.660 --> 02:01:10.500
and we just can't see them.

02:01:10.500 --> 02:01:12.510
What we have to do
is periodically find

02:01:12.510 --> 02:01:15.750
a way of realizing
that you can't see

02:01:15.750 --> 02:01:18.010
unless you admit you're blind.

02:01:18.010 --> 02:01:20.160
And every time we
admit we're blind,

02:01:20.160 --> 02:01:21.450
we can actually see a bit.

02:01:21.450 --> 02:01:23.970
And then we can push
on to the next stage

02:01:23.970 --> 02:01:25.150
of this grand adventure.

02:01:25.150 --> 02:01:25.958
Thank you.

02:01:25.958 --> 02:01:28.398
[APPLAUSE]

02:01:42.080 --> 02:01:44.090
MODERATOR: Thanks a lot, Alan.

02:01:44.090 --> 02:01:48.080
We'll take a few questions
directed to Alan specifically.

02:01:48.080 --> 02:01:51.645
And then we're going to
arrange for the panel.

02:01:51.645 --> 02:01:53.270
AUDIENCE: I just want
to go back to one

02:01:53.270 --> 02:01:55.848
of your early slides
of the dog house,

02:01:55.848 --> 02:01:57.890
that I think actually the
dog house is the better

02:01:57.890 --> 02:02:00.258
model than the bridge.

02:02:00.258 --> 02:02:02.050
Because if we look at
the scale, the bridge

02:02:02.050 --> 02:02:03.592
is about a middle-sized
construction,

02:02:03.592 --> 02:02:06.410
rather tiny compared to
cities and societies,

02:02:06.410 --> 02:02:08.450
which are totally
unarchitectured,

02:02:08.450 --> 02:02:09.620
yet somehow work.

02:02:09.620 --> 02:02:11.870
The thing is, we don't quite
understand how they work.

02:02:11.870 --> 02:02:13.520
I think that's
really a challenge

02:02:13.520 --> 02:02:14.478
for intimate computing.

02:02:14.478 --> 02:02:17.510
KAY: No, but you're
making my point.

02:02:17.510 --> 02:02:20.180
The whole point about
biology is that it

02:02:20.180 --> 02:02:22.550
works in the face
of error and not

02:02:22.550 --> 02:02:24.820
having to be
strictly architected.

02:02:24.820 --> 02:02:27.020
AUDIENCE: Okay, then
I misunderstood.

02:02:27.020 --> 02:02:29.150
KAY: I said dog
houses work simply

02:02:29.150 --> 02:02:31.280
because they're
so simple compared

02:02:31.280 --> 02:02:32.490
to the strength of materials.

02:02:32.490 --> 02:02:35.510
So that's a different
point than the--

02:02:35.510 --> 02:02:38.630
but the very point I was
trying to make about,

02:02:38.630 --> 02:02:40.160
when you go up into
real complexity,

02:02:40.160 --> 02:02:42.650
you have to find a
way of getting things

02:02:42.650 --> 02:02:46.580
to work together, even when
they don't want to terribly.

02:02:46.580 --> 02:02:49.555
So the Constitution of the US
is a pretty interesting meta

02:02:49.555 --> 02:02:50.180
program since--

02:02:50.180 --> 02:02:51.972
AUDIENCE: And the nice
thing is it's small.

02:02:51.972 --> 02:02:53.610
KAY: It's very, very small.

02:02:53.610 --> 02:02:55.520
And it's not a set of laws.

02:02:55.520 --> 02:02:57.590
It's a set of
principles for dealing

02:02:57.590 --> 02:03:03.650
with millions of only semi
co-operative components, namely

02:03:03.650 --> 02:03:06.380
us.

02:03:06.380 --> 02:03:09.015
Other questions?

02:03:09.015 --> 02:03:09.515
Yes.

02:03:13.740 --> 02:03:16.530
AUDIENCE: Not so much a
question, but a comment.

02:03:16.530 --> 02:03:19.680
It seems to me that you
focused on the '60s.

02:03:19.680 --> 02:03:23.310
And it's probably
natural to think

02:03:23.310 --> 02:03:25.650
of the golden age
in the area where

02:03:25.650 --> 02:03:28.320
you get your own awakening and
you start to become productive

02:03:28.320 --> 02:03:30.060
in a creative part of society.

02:03:30.060 --> 02:03:32.280
But for everybody
who's in the field,

02:03:32.280 --> 02:03:34.460
their golden age
is probably going

02:03:34.460 --> 02:03:39.030
to be some other
decade or whatever.

02:03:39.030 --> 02:03:40.860
As I went back and
just sort of picked

02:03:40.860 --> 02:03:43.505
another one, like the
'70s, and just sort of made

02:03:43.505 --> 02:03:45.130
the list of things
that came out there.

02:03:45.130 --> 02:03:49.950
This was the era when ARPA
had a D in front of it.

02:03:49.950 --> 02:03:52.680
I was thinking of Minsky's
adage about not being

02:03:52.680 --> 02:03:54.270
right about something
for too long.

02:03:54.270 --> 02:03:57.120
I made the list of the things
that came out of that decade.

02:03:57.120 --> 02:04:00.990
And it seems to me they
were pretty impressive.

02:04:00.990 --> 02:04:03.360
The first work on
speech understanding

02:04:03.360 --> 02:04:05.940
in a systematic way
started in that decade,

02:04:05.940 --> 02:04:07.880
with Allen Newell's push.

02:04:07.880 --> 02:04:12.990
The work on image understanding
started in that decade.

02:04:12.990 --> 02:04:15.820
The internet came
out of that decade.

02:04:15.820 --> 02:04:19.380
The microprocessor was in
the decade of the '70s.

02:04:19.380 --> 02:04:21.150
The personal computer
really came out

02:04:21.150 --> 02:04:22.650
of the decade of the '70s.

02:04:22.650 --> 02:04:27.150
The work on LANs, radio
networks, and the DLSI design,

02:04:27.150 --> 02:04:28.950
the thing that empowered
the universities,

02:04:28.950 --> 02:04:30.450
came out of the '70s.

02:04:30.450 --> 02:04:33.640
And probably an awful lot of
the object-oriented programming

02:04:33.640 --> 02:04:35.280
work came out of
the '70s, as well,

02:04:35.280 --> 02:04:37.670
although you would probably
know better than I.

02:04:37.670 --> 02:04:41.880
So it seems to me it's a
little unfair to cite the '60s

02:04:41.880 --> 02:04:44.700
as the golden age relative
to all other ages,

02:04:44.700 --> 02:04:46.927
because I think it's
relative to our own contexts.

02:04:46.927 --> 02:04:48.510
KAY: Well, I hope I
wasn't doing that.

02:04:48.510 --> 02:04:51.180
Because of course, the most
productive period in my life

02:04:51.180 --> 02:04:54.180
was in the '70s,
not in the '60s.

02:04:54.180 --> 02:04:57.750
The reason I picked
the '60s was that was

02:04:57.750 --> 02:05:00.330
when I think a
lot of these ideas

02:05:00.330 --> 02:05:02.490
manifested itself in
such a way that we could

02:05:02.490 --> 02:05:04.420
see that they were possible.

02:05:04.420 --> 02:05:08.220
A lot of the shoes were
dropped in the '70s.

02:05:08.220 --> 02:05:14.040
But I certainly think of the
'70s much more as engineering.

02:05:14.040 --> 02:05:16.710
In fact, most of the
stuff we did at Xerox PARC

02:05:16.710 --> 02:05:22.792
was kind of a second engineering
pass on things we'd failed at.

02:05:22.792 --> 02:05:24.750
Because the things were
bubble-gummed together.

02:05:24.750 --> 02:05:26.850
We didn't completely
understand them.

02:05:26.850 --> 02:05:30.450
So for instance, one of the
dicta that Butler Lampson got

02:05:30.450 --> 02:05:32.910
us to agree to in the
early days of Xerox PARC

02:05:32.910 --> 02:05:34.620
is, we should never
do something that

02:05:34.620 --> 02:05:37.320
isn't engineered for 100 users.

02:05:37.320 --> 02:05:39.510
And that made us much
more conservative

02:05:39.510 --> 02:05:41.400
than we had been in the '60s.

02:05:41.400 --> 02:05:45.600
But it also added much
more reality to the things

02:05:45.600 --> 02:05:46.100
that we did.

02:05:46.100 --> 02:05:48.058
And of course, a lot of
the success that we had

02:05:48.058 --> 02:05:49.860
was because we could
make 100 of something

02:05:49.860 --> 02:05:53.750
or make a machine that you
could run 100 people on or make

02:05:53.750 --> 02:05:55.250
software that you
could move around.

02:05:55.250 --> 02:06:00.160
But to me, that was very
different than the--

02:06:00.160 --> 02:06:04.620
I think of it as being less
creative and more engineering

02:06:04.620 --> 02:06:07.440
than the '60s.

02:06:07.440 --> 02:06:11.040
But as you say,
other people might--

02:06:11.040 --> 02:06:13.470
it just depends
on when you start.

02:06:13.470 --> 02:06:20.340
From my standpoint, it's
silly to try and compare

02:06:20.340 --> 02:06:21.900
one person's gods with another.

02:06:21.900 --> 02:06:28.080
But I think that, to me, Ivan
Sutherland and Doug Engelbart

02:06:28.080 --> 02:06:32.610
did the most towering and
important stuff in the last 35

02:06:32.610 --> 02:06:33.190
years.

02:06:33.190 --> 02:06:37.250
And I think that stands
up pretty well today.

02:06:37.250 --> 02:06:39.652
Yes, somebody else was--

02:06:39.652 --> 02:06:41.140
okay, great.

02:06:41.140 --> 02:06:44.116
[APPLAUSE]

02:06:57.995 --> 02:06:59.620
MODERATOR: Before we
go into the panel,

02:06:59.620 --> 02:07:01.460
I want to do a couple of things.

02:07:01.460 --> 02:07:04.600
First of all, Paul Penfield
has to leave for a memorial

02:07:04.600 --> 02:07:06.400
service, and I'd
like to thank him

02:07:06.400 --> 02:07:10.390
on our joint behalf for
a job very well done.

02:07:10.390 --> 02:07:16.480
He and a lot of people at MIT,
like Ed over there and Scott

02:07:16.480 --> 02:07:18.310
and lots and lots
of other people,

02:07:18.310 --> 02:07:21.200
put in real quality
time making this happen.

02:07:21.200 --> 02:07:24.200
So thank you very much,
Paul, and the rest of you.

02:07:24.200 --> 02:07:26.610
[APPLAUSE]

02:07:33.860 --> 02:07:35.440
The second thing
I have to tell you

02:07:35.440 --> 02:07:38.110
is, Paul has an announcement.

02:07:38.110 --> 02:07:40.166
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:07:41.500 --> 02:07:44.040
PENFIELD: I just want to thank
everyone for coming to this.

02:07:44.040 --> 02:07:47.260
This has been a real
wonderful opportunity for me

02:07:47.260 --> 02:07:50.020
to see some aspects
of this community

02:07:50.020 --> 02:07:52.660
that I hadn't been as
familiar with as I should.

02:07:52.660 --> 02:07:56.180
And I want to personally thank
Andy for putting this together.

02:07:56.180 --> 02:07:57.940
Andy says that we did
the [INAUDIBLE] job.

02:07:57.940 --> 02:08:00.970
But I can assure you that
Andy, in setting this up

02:08:00.970 --> 02:08:04.470
over the past several months,
has put it in far more effort

02:08:04.470 --> 02:08:05.950
than anyone at MIT has.

02:08:05.950 --> 02:08:08.770
And I think the whole group
deserves to thank Andy

02:08:08.770 --> 02:08:09.670
for organizing this.

02:08:09.670 --> 02:08:10.762
[APPLAUSE]

02:08:10.762 --> 02:08:11.734
MODERATOR: Thank you.

02:08:19.510 --> 02:08:20.380
Thank you very much.

02:08:20.380 --> 02:08:22.540
As you know, it was
the three guys from EBT

02:08:22.540 --> 02:08:25.050
who really thought of it,
and Lisa who executed it.

02:08:25.050 --> 02:08:26.920
But I got to invite
the speakers.

02:08:26.920 --> 02:08:28.930
That was the best part.

02:08:28.930 --> 02:08:32.514
Speaking about speakers, you
all know what day it is today?

02:08:32.514 --> 02:08:33.460
AUDIENCE: Friday.

02:08:33.460 --> 02:08:34.810
MODERATOR: Friday the 13th.

02:08:34.810 --> 02:08:39.550
Now, I'm not normally
a superstitious person.

02:08:39.550 --> 02:08:43.475
However, I have to find some
explanation for what happened.

02:08:43.475 --> 02:08:44.350
Here's what happened.

02:08:44.350 --> 02:08:49.720
Inadvertently, Ted Nelson's
tape got recorded over.

02:08:49.720 --> 02:08:54.010
So we do not have an official
record of his great speech

02:08:54.010 --> 02:08:55.720
here.

02:08:55.720 --> 02:08:57.710
I just found out about
this a little time ago.

02:08:57.710 --> 02:08:58.560
This is not a joke.

02:08:58.560 --> 02:08:59.060
I'm sorry.

02:08:59.060 --> 02:09:01.870
I wish it were.

02:09:01.870 --> 02:09:05.980
And we are trying to find
people who may have recorded it.

02:09:05.980 --> 02:09:07.740
There are a couple
of possibilities.

02:09:07.740 --> 02:09:11.570
There may be some folks out
over the MBone, who recorded it.

02:09:11.570 --> 02:09:15.010
It's a classic point of failure.

02:09:15.010 --> 02:09:17.290
And despite all
of the duplication

02:09:17.290 --> 02:09:22.420
that we had in equipment and
people, something went wrong.

02:09:22.420 --> 02:09:26.870
In any case, we asked Ted what
he would like to do about it.

02:09:26.870 --> 02:09:31.270
He was a gentleman to the core
and took it exceedingly well.

02:09:31.270 --> 02:09:34.270
And I think I would have gone
straight through the ceiling.

02:09:34.270 --> 02:09:36.330
Fortunately, he's
jet-lagged and so

02:09:36.330 --> 02:09:40.150
he didn't react quite as
strongly as he might have.

02:09:40.150 --> 02:09:43.660
As I said, he handled
it beautifully.

02:09:43.660 --> 02:09:46.920
The folks here at
MIT thought that

02:09:46.920 --> 02:09:49.030
what might be fun
and interesting

02:09:49.030 --> 02:09:50.950
is to let him have
the last word, as it

02:09:50.950 --> 02:09:54.190
were, a reprise performance.

02:09:54.190 --> 02:09:58.150
And any of you are
welcome to stay

02:09:58.150 --> 02:10:00.640
while Ted tries to
lay down another track

02:10:00.640 --> 02:10:02.710
for the historical record.

02:10:02.710 --> 02:10:04.600
And we'll do that
after the panel.

02:10:04.600 --> 02:10:06.010
Some of you have to go.

02:10:06.010 --> 02:10:07.540
Some of you have
other things to do.

02:10:07.540 --> 02:10:10.000
Some of you, whatever.

02:10:10.000 --> 02:10:12.160
You're all more than
welcome to sit in

02:10:12.160 --> 02:10:16.360
on this as Ted tries his
best to recapture the moment.

02:10:16.360 --> 02:10:18.950
We apologize.

02:10:18.950 --> 02:10:20.158
AUDIENCE: Forget that moment.

02:10:20.158 --> 02:10:21.158
We'll do another moment.

02:10:21.158 --> 02:10:24.370
MODERATOR: We'll do
another moment, of course.

02:10:24.370 --> 02:10:25.475
So that's where we are.

02:10:25.475 --> 02:10:27.100
We're going to take
a couple of minutes

02:10:27.100 --> 02:10:32.470
to set up the chairs for the
panel, and then we carry on.

02:10:32.470 --> 02:10:34.720
Well, why don't we
just get started.

02:10:34.720 --> 02:10:37.720
And you can fire at will
at the people who are here.

02:10:51.260 --> 02:10:52.280
Fire when ready.

02:10:55.605 --> 02:10:58.460
AUDIENCE: All right, I'll go.

02:10:58.460 --> 02:11:01.970
We started this
discussion by looking

02:11:01.970 --> 02:11:05.570
at Bush's vision of memex.

02:11:05.570 --> 02:11:08.450
And we just heard Alan
talk about the golden age

02:11:08.450 --> 02:11:09.530
of the '60s.

02:11:09.530 --> 02:11:11.630
And here we are
in 1994, and we've

02:11:11.630 --> 02:11:16.160
ended up with Windows, which
is kind of a bleak thought.

02:11:16.160 --> 02:11:20.150
So I'd ask any of you who
were interested to postulate

02:11:20.150 --> 02:11:23.300
where we would be if
Windows never existed.

02:11:23.300 --> 02:11:25.469
Would we have had to
invent it, or would we

02:11:25.469 --> 02:11:29.870
be someplace different,
and where would we be?

02:11:29.870 --> 02:11:32.287
PRESENTER 1: The question
is Microsoft Windows?

02:11:32.287 --> 02:11:33.370
AUDIENCE: Yeah, Microsoft.

02:11:33.370 --> 02:11:35.037
MODERATOR: Wait a
minute, wait a minute.

02:11:35.037 --> 02:11:35.930
Use the mic.

02:11:35.930 --> 02:11:36.430
I'm sorry.

02:11:36.430 --> 02:11:38.980
Clarification-- is the
question Microsoft Windows

02:11:38.980 --> 02:11:41.350
or windows in general?

02:11:41.350 --> 02:11:43.600
AUDIENCE: If Microsoft didn't
exist as a market force,

02:11:43.600 --> 02:11:45.132
where would we be?

02:11:45.132 --> 02:11:46.840
PRESENTER 1: We would
be missing nothing.

02:11:46.840 --> 02:11:49.420
Microsoft doesn't even
claim that they're

02:11:49.420 --> 02:11:51.100
a highly innovative company.

02:11:51.100 --> 02:11:54.910
They take programs that have
already existed and sold

02:11:54.910 --> 02:11:59.350
well from other vendors, such
as Lotus and WordPerfect,

02:11:59.350 --> 02:12:02.560
and they do a
commercially better job

02:12:02.560 --> 02:12:04.540
rewriting these programs
and selling them,

02:12:04.540 --> 02:12:06.640
as Excel and WordPerfect.

02:12:06.640 --> 02:12:09.285
So we wouldn't be missing
any important technology.

02:12:09.285 --> 02:12:11.660
AUDIENCE: So you think we'd
be in exactly the same place?

02:12:11.660 --> 02:12:13.285
Personal computing
would look the same.

02:12:15.798 --> 02:12:17.337
PRESENTER 1: As
far as I can see.

02:12:21.950 --> 02:12:23.230
ALAN KAY: At PARC--

02:12:23.230 --> 02:12:25.563
because I didn't talk about
the PARC phase of the thing.

02:12:25.563 --> 02:12:27.470
But at PARC, one
of the goals was

02:12:27.470 --> 02:12:31.110
to do NLS as a
distributed system.

02:12:31.110 --> 02:12:35.690
And all of the Altos there
had the five finger keyboards

02:12:35.690 --> 02:12:38.210
as well as the mouse on them.

02:12:43.400 --> 02:12:44.755
We basically loved NLS.

02:12:44.755 --> 02:12:46.130
And we'd done a
few modifications

02:12:46.130 --> 02:12:48.310
which we thought even sped up--

02:12:48.310 --> 02:12:54.830
NLS, part of the interaction
scheme on it, I believe,

02:12:54.830 --> 02:13:00.463
was because the analog mouse,
there was some drift in it.

02:13:00.463 --> 02:13:01.880
So one of the
things that they did

02:13:01.880 --> 02:13:04.260
was to say what kind of a
thing you were pointing at.

02:13:04.260 --> 02:13:08.280
So you'd say, move character
or move word or move paragraph

02:13:08.280 --> 02:13:09.200
and so forth.

02:13:09.200 --> 02:13:12.290
It was kind of a procedural,
where you gave the command

02:13:12.290 --> 02:13:14.510
first, and then you'd
go bug, bug, and then

02:13:14.510 --> 02:13:18.920
Command [? accept. ?] And
we realized at Xerox PARC

02:13:18.920 --> 02:13:23.252
that you wanted to have a
speedy scheme for interacting.

02:13:23.252 --> 02:13:24.710
And we thought we
could go even one

02:13:24.710 --> 02:13:26.743
better by selecting the object.

02:13:26.743 --> 02:13:28.160
So you'd select
the thing that you

02:13:28.160 --> 02:13:30.110
were going to do something to.

02:13:30.110 --> 02:13:32.090
You would give the command.

02:13:32.090 --> 02:13:34.220
And then in the case
of move character,

02:13:34.220 --> 02:13:39.050
you'd go select, move, select.

02:13:39.050 --> 02:13:41.690
And that would do it
with fewer keystrokes.

02:13:41.690 --> 02:13:44.960
Now the abortion that
happened after PARC

02:13:44.960 --> 02:13:47.000
was the misunderstanding
of the user

02:13:47.000 --> 02:13:48.678
interface that we
did for children,

02:13:48.678 --> 02:13:50.720
which was the overlapping
window interface, which

02:13:50.720 --> 02:13:54.530
we made as naive as
absolutely we possibly could,

02:13:54.530 --> 02:13:57.870
to the point of not having
any workflow ideas in it.

02:13:57.870 --> 02:14:00.170
And that was taken
over uncritically

02:14:00.170 --> 02:14:01.520
out into the outside world.

02:14:01.520 --> 02:14:05.150
So we have many systems, like
Lotus Notes and many mail

02:14:05.150 --> 02:14:08.810
systems, that,
when you say reply,

02:14:08.810 --> 02:14:11.120
it comes up with a window
over the very thing you were

02:14:11.120 --> 02:14:13.755
reading, as though there
weren't any connection

02:14:13.755 --> 02:14:14.630
between these things.

02:14:14.630 --> 02:14:17.140
So this is an abortion to me.

02:14:17.140 --> 02:14:19.100
But it's basically part
of the whole field.

02:14:19.100 --> 02:14:21.920
Whereas our notion was
that, you start the kids off

02:14:21.920 --> 02:14:23.870
on this fairly
simple, naive thing,

02:14:23.870 --> 02:14:26.150
and then there would be
an actual progression

02:14:26.150 --> 02:14:28.400
where you would get up
to this several commands

02:14:28.400 --> 02:14:30.620
a second kind of thing
that you could do with NLS.

02:14:30.620 --> 02:14:34.010
If you've ever seen anybody use
NLS, it is really marvelous.

02:14:34.010 --> 02:14:37.160
Because you're kind of flying
along through this stuff,

02:14:37.160 --> 02:14:38.870
several commands a second.

02:14:38.870 --> 02:14:41.330
And there's this
complete different sense

02:14:41.330 --> 02:14:46.010
of what it means to interact
than you have today.

02:14:46.010 --> 02:14:50.890
So I characterize what we
have today is a wonderful bike

02:14:50.890 --> 02:14:51.890
with training wheels on.

02:14:51.890 --> 02:14:53.930
And nobody knows the
training wheels are on,

02:14:53.930 --> 02:14:56.230
so nobody's trying
to take them off.

02:14:56.230 --> 02:14:58.960
So maybe Doug has
a comment on that.

02:14:58.960 --> 02:15:01.130
So I just feel like
we're way, way behind

02:15:01.130 --> 02:15:04.340
where we could have been,
if it weren't for the way

02:15:04.340 --> 02:15:05.690
commercialization turned out.

02:15:08.495 --> 02:15:10.620
ENGELBART: Well, strangely
enough, I feel the same.

02:15:10.620 --> 02:15:12.790
[LAUGHTER]

02:15:12.790 --> 02:15:20.000
Yeah, that's part of the easy to
learn and natural to use thing

02:15:20.000 --> 02:15:23.420
that became sort
of a god to follow,

02:15:23.420 --> 02:15:25.710
and that the marketplace
is driving it.

02:15:25.710 --> 02:15:28.770
And it was successful and you
could market on that basis.

02:15:28.770 --> 02:15:32.030
But some of the diagrams
and pictures I didn't quite

02:15:32.030 --> 02:15:34.820
get to the other day
was, how do you ever

02:15:34.820 --> 02:15:37.760
migrate from a
tricycle to a bicycle?

02:15:37.760 --> 02:15:41.600
Because a bicycle is very
unnatural and very hard

02:15:41.600 --> 02:15:44.660
to learn, compared to getting
on a tricycle and riding.

02:15:44.660 --> 02:15:49.250
And yet, in society, it has
superseded all the tricycles,

02:15:49.250 --> 02:15:52.870
essentially, for anybody over
five years old or something.

02:15:52.870 --> 02:15:53.960
So there's a lot.

02:15:53.960 --> 02:15:58.280
And the whole idea of high
performance knowledge work

02:15:58.280 --> 02:16:04.295
is yet to kind of come
up and be in the domain.

02:16:04.295 --> 02:16:06.440
You got to make it easy.

02:16:06.440 --> 02:16:10.910
It's still the orientation of
automating what you used to do,

02:16:10.910 --> 02:16:13.960
instead of moving to a whole new
domain, which obviously you're

02:16:13.960 --> 02:16:16.700
going to learn quite
a few new skills.

02:16:16.700 --> 02:16:18.770
And so you make
analogies of saying,

02:16:18.770 --> 02:16:21.110
suppose you wanted to
move up on the ski slopes

02:16:21.110 --> 02:16:23.810
and be mobile on skis.

02:16:23.810 --> 02:16:27.950
Well, just visiting them for
an afternoon is not going to--

02:16:27.950 --> 02:16:30.270
you're not going to find--

02:16:30.270 --> 02:16:33.920
I'd love to have photographs
of skateboards and skis

02:16:33.920 --> 02:16:37.459
and wind surfing and
all of that to show you

02:16:37.459 --> 02:16:41.480
what people can really do if
they have a new way supplied

02:16:41.480 --> 02:16:45.530
by technology to be mobile
in a different environment.

02:16:45.530 --> 02:16:49.040
And none of that can be done
if people insisted that it

02:16:49.040 --> 02:16:52.010
was an easy to learn thing.

02:16:52.010 --> 02:16:55.879
Anyway, so moving
your way around those

02:16:55.879 --> 02:16:58.129
thought vectors
and concept space--

02:16:58.129 --> 02:16:59.250
I'd forgotten about that.

02:16:59.250 --> 02:17:00.889
AUDIENCE: You said that, right?

02:17:00.889 --> 02:17:01.389
ENGELBART: I must have.

02:17:01.389 --> 02:17:02.030
That's so good.

02:17:02.030 --> 02:17:03.425
[LAUGHTER]

02:17:03.425 --> 02:17:05.285
[APPLAUSE]

02:17:06.215 --> 02:17:08.390
PRESENTER 2: You did.

02:17:08.390 --> 02:17:10.549
It seemed to come
naturally at that point.

02:17:10.549 --> 02:17:12.854
ENGELBART: Anyway,
that's a nice formulation

02:17:12.854 --> 02:17:14.860
of what I've been saying.

02:17:14.860 --> 02:17:19.840
But it's to externalize
your thoughts in the concept

02:17:19.840 --> 02:17:22.809
structures that are meaningful
outside, and moving around

02:17:22.809 --> 02:17:26.350
flexibly and manipulating
them and viewing them.

02:17:26.350 --> 02:17:31.590
It's a new way to operate on a
new kind of externalized media,

02:17:31.590 --> 02:17:32.809
to explain it like that.

02:17:32.809 --> 02:17:36.309
So to keep doing it in
the model of the old media

02:17:36.309 --> 02:17:39.160
is just a hangup
that someplace we're

02:17:39.160 --> 02:17:43.180
going to break that
perspective and shift.

02:17:43.180 --> 02:17:46.629
And then the idea of high
performance and the idea

02:17:46.629 --> 02:17:48.459
of high performance
teams, who've

02:17:48.459 --> 02:17:51.639
learned to coordinate to
get that ball down the field

02:17:51.639 --> 02:17:54.820
together in all
kinds of operations,

02:17:54.820 --> 02:17:56.510
very different operations.

02:17:56.510 --> 02:17:59.200
So I feel like the
real breakthrough

02:17:59.200 --> 02:18:00.820
for us getting
someplace is going

02:18:00.820 --> 02:18:03.370
to be when we say, all
right, let's put together

02:18:03.370 --> 02:18:05.777
high performance
knowledge work teams.

02:18:05.777 --> 02:18:07.360
And let's pick the
roles they're going

02:18:07.360 --> 02:18:10.030
to play within our
organizations in some way

02:18:10.030 --> 02:18:12.850
and such that, even though
they operate very differently

02:18:12.850 --> 02:18:16.040
from their peers out in the
rest of the organization,

02:18:16.040 --> 02:18:19.520
they can interact with them and
support them very effectively.

02:18:19.520 --> 02:18:22.450
So there are roles like that
that would be very effective.

02:18:22.450 --> 02:18:24.040
And everyone else
can sort of see,

02:18:24.040 --> 02:18:27.910
because they're interacting,
these guys, what they can do.

02:18:27.910 --> 02:18:31.160
And suppose it does take 200
hours of specialized training,

02:18:31.160 --> 02:18:32.260
et cetera, like that.

02:18:32.260 --> 02:18:35.680
That's less than boot
camp, something like that.

02:18:35.680 --> 02:18:40.180
So anyway, to me, that's
kind of the only way

02:18:40.180 --> 02:18:41.680
the evolution--
one of those boxes

02:18:41.680 --> 02:18:44.530
in that paradigm
map about deployment

02:18:44.530 --> 02:18:46.629
was really coming
down and showing you

02:18:46.629 --> 02:18:49.820
that special purpose teams
is one kind of thing,

02:18:49.820 --> 02:18:51.700
the way that they can propagate.

02:18:51.700 --> 02:18:54.790
And very different from
moving a group of people

02:18:54.790 --> 02:18:58.750
who have an existing set
of staff and processes

02:18:58.750 --> 02:19:00.610
and methods and
skills and equipment,

02:19:00.610 --> 02:19:03.209
and trying to move
them all together.

02:19:03.209 --> 02:19:06.280
It's a practically
impossible task

02:19:06.280 --> 02:19:09.670
to do that in any
significantly large step

02:19:09.670 --> 02:19:12.580
without having
casualties in there that

02:19:12.580 --> 02:19:16.681
just aren't all equipped
to be mobile in that space.

02:19:16.681 --> 02:19:18.639
Anyway, so there's a lot
to go with that thing.

02:19:18.639 --> 02:19:21.040
And it all stems
from looking at today

02:19:21.040 --> 02:19:25.209
and saying, why
do we accept that?

02:19:25.209 --> 02:19:26.500
That's the modern thing.

02:19:26.500 --> 02:19:29.440
And it's almost like a religion.

02:19:29.440 --> 02:19:33.070
In any other company, I'm
afraid to bring that out.

02:19:33.070 --> 02:19:37.408
But maybe I have to
run from this one, too.

02:19:37.408 --> 02:19:39.200
NELSON: I hadn't heard
you say this before.

02:19:39.200 --> 02:19:41.010
So you're basically
proposing information--

02:19:41.010 --> 02:19:44.337
MODERATOR: Wait, wait,
wait, use the mic.

02:19:44.337 --> 02:19:46.129
NELSON: I hadn't heard
you say this before.

02:19:46.129 --> 02:19:48.546
You're basically proposing
posing some kind of information

02:19:48.546 --> 02:19:52.080
SWAT team that can move
swiftly through an organization

02:19:52.080 --> 02:19:58.180
or it's going to be some elite
[INAUDIBLE] in the files.

02:19:58.180 --> 02:20:00.540
This is a very exciting
and interesting concept.

02:20:00.540 --> 02:20:03.757
But how would that
function organizationally.

02:20:03.757 --> 02:20:05.665
AUDIENCE: We did
that to your tape.

02:20:05.665 --> 02:20:08.527
[LAUGHTER]

02:20:08.527 --> 02:20:11.260
NELSON: Obviously,
they got in there.

02:20:11.260 --> 02:20:12.460
ENGELBART: No.

02:20:12.460 --> 02:20:15.198
I mentioned the other
day that, if you're

02:20:15.198 --> 02:20:17.740
going to have a strategy to go
ahead with your organizations,

02:20:17.740 --> 02:20:19.310
you just can't lift
them all at once.

02:20:19.310 --> 02:20:21.910
So the strategy I
finally worked out

02:20:21.910 --> 02:20:26.720
is that, to that
improvement infrastructure

02:20:26.720 --> 02:20:29.440
term I mentioned, I didn't
show you much below that.

02:20:29.440 --> 02:20:32.020
But in that improvement
infrastructure,

02:20:32.020 --> 02:20:34.360
there are roles in the
improvement process

02:20:34.360 --> 02:20:37.030
for high performance
teams that would really

02:20:37.030 --> 02:20:40.540
be helping the information
and the improvement process

02:20:40.540 --> 02:20:41.650
go about.

02:20:41.650 --> 02:20:44.770
And then they're roles
for them to be plugged

02:20:44.770 --> 02:20:47.480
into an organization
and to function

02:20:47.480 --> 02:20:48.730
that would be very supportive.

02:20:48.730 --> 02:20:52.240
And this is all assuming
that those are the outposts

02:20:52.240 --> 02:20:54.790
that everybody is going
to start moving there as

02:20:54.790 --> 02:20:55.930
well as you can.

02:20:55.930 --> 02:20:59.110
But you just can't lift
the whole organization

02:20:59.110 --> 02:21:01.420
in some very radical way.

02:21:01.420 --> 02:21:03.460
And so that's a
part of the process.

02:21:03.460 --> 02:21:05.710
So yes, they are an elite team.

02:21:05.710 --> 02:21:08.650
But you're assuming that,
in some number of years,

02:21:08.650 --> 02:21:12.070
you'll learn how to get the
whole organization there.

02:21:12.070 --> 02:21:14.320
PRESENTER 2: I think that
the gruesome practical point

02:21:14.320 --> 02:21:18.437
is that finding a path
of [? thought ?] vector

02:21:18.437 --> 02:21:20.770
through concept space to where
you are may be one thing.

02:21:20.770 --> 02:21:23.140
But the transition path
to get the world there

02:21:23.140 --> 02:21:26.110
may be very, very
different path.

02:21:26.110 --> 02:21:29.740
So that you have to find a
path of whatever they are,

02:21:29.740 --> 02:21:36.970
of pushing the world vectors,
where each step is downhill

02:21:36.970 --> 02:21:38.832
for the person who
has to make that step.

02:21:38.832 --> 02:21:40.290
So now, your original
question was,

02:21:40.290 --> 02:21:45.160
has Microsoft's existence
and the dominance of DOS

02:21:45.160 --> 02:21:47.380
and later Windows helped?

02:21:47.380 --> 02:21:50.500
Basically, if at some point,
Microsoft has to make a change,

02:21:50.500 --> 02:21:53.042
obviously that's going to be
one of the more difficult people

02:21:53.042 --> 02:21:56.040
to put them in a position so
that the next step is downhill.

02:21:56.040 --> 02:21:56.790
And it can happen.

02:21:56.790 --> 02:21:58.748
And one of the things
that can make that easier

02:21:58.748 --> 02:22:01.510
is if instead of having just one
operating system, you have two.

02:22:01.510 --> 02:22:03.040
You might ask, for example,
what would have happened

02:22:03.040 --> 02:22:04.570
if the Mac hadn't been there?

02:22:04.570 --> 02:22:07.390
Maybe we'd still be using
DOS without Windows.

02:22:07.390 --> 02:22:10.810
So there's an argument
which says, this is easier.

02:22:10.810 --> 02:22:12.622
I wouldn't say it's
easier in a situation

02:22:12.622 --> 02:22:14.080
where you don't
have any dominance,

02:22:14.080 --> 02:22:15.670
you don't have any commonality.

02:22:15.670 --> 02:22:17.920
Maybe that's what held up
Unix, the people who are all

02:22:17.920 --> 02:22:20.087
developing things on the
Unix, in fact, because they

02:22:20.087 --> 02:22:21.430
didn't have enough commonality.

02:22:21.430 --> 02:22:23.290
It was difficult to make code
that you could pass around.

02:22:23.290 --> 02:22:25.030
It was difficult to
spread things around.

02:22:25.030 --> 02:22:27.220
So the fact that you've
got DOS there at least

02:22:27.220 --> 02:22:29.887
means that, if you're not trying
to change the operating system,

02:22:29.887 --> 02:22:32.480
you can roll out all kinds of
bits of software very quickly.

02:22:32.480 --> 02:22:34.480
So that's got a great advantage.

02:22:34.480 --> 02:22:36.490
The internet and
DOS, if you can do

02:22:36.490 --> 02:22:38.240
what you want with
the internet and DOS,

02:22:38.240 --> 02:22:40.240
then you can do it very
quickly and roll it out.

02:22:40.240 --> 02:22:41.948
It's when you want to
change the internet

02:22:41.948 --> 02:22:43.870
or you want to change
DOS that you've

02:22:43.870 --> 02:22:47.260
got yourself a big problem.

02:22:47.260 --> 02:22:51.160
The way I hope it will
maybe work for the web

02:22:51.160 --> 02:22:53.270
protocols and all the
these web standards,

02:22:53.270 --> 02:22:55.120
which we hope we'll
be able to evolve

02:22:55.120 --> 02:22:58.120
from their kludgy current
state into the beautiful golden

02:22:58.120 --> 02:23:01.750
dream, is that in any case
where you lay down a standard,

02:23:01.750 --> 02:23:03.435
you also allow there
to be a second.

02:23:03.435 --> 02:23:05.560
And you show a path whereby
if a third one comes up

02:23:05.560 --> 02:23:07.680
which is better, you can
move in that direction.

02:23:07.680 --> 02:23:10.900
So you always have one on
the heels of the first,

02:23:10.900 --> 02:23:11.813
keeping it honest.

02:23:11.813 --> 02:23:13.480
And you have a
transition, a hook in it,

02:23:13.480 --> 02:23:15.960
to be able to unhook it
all together later on.

02:23:15.960 --> 02:23:19.650
KAY: So American television is
kind of a counter example of,

02:23:19.650 --> 02:23:22.090
in the effort to make it
really easy for everybody

02:23:22.090 --> 02:23:23.470
to watch television.

02:23:23.470 --> 02:23:27.100
I'm not sure I see any path back
upwards towards enlightenment,

02:23:27.100 --> 02:23:29.470
because the advertisers
are quite happy.

02:23:29.470 --> 02:23:30.610
And so is Microsoft.

02:23:30.610 --> 02:23:33.370
It's quite happy with
the stage of blindness

02:23:33.370 --> 02:23:34.670
that everybody is in right now.

02:23:34.670 --> 02:23:37.240
So it's a great idea.

02:23:37.240 --> 02:23:40.300
There's an economic law called
Gresham's law, which says,

02:23:40.300 --> 02:23:44.260
the bad that's easily
produced drives out the good.

02:23:44.260 --> 02:23:48.670
Because it resets the
whole notion of normality.

02:23:48.670 --> 02:23:52.060
Personally, I think if
somebody invented a bicycle

02:23:52.060 --> 02:23:55.088
and it didn't exist today, they
couldn't get anybody to buy it.

02:23:55.088 --> 02:23:57.130
Because it takes more than
five minutes to learn.

02:23:57.130 --> 02:23:59.130
That is really pathetic
when you think about it.

02:24:01.650 --> 02:24:03.400
NELSON: As far as
television is concerned,

02:24:03.400 --> 02:24:05.230
the operative phrase
a few minutes ago

02:24:05.230 --> 02:24:07.340
was "a comfortable step down."

02:24:13.450 --> 02:24:18.170
ENGELBART: I'd say you want to
make your steps up comfortable.

02:24:18.170 --> 02:24:20.840
It's kind of take stress
but you have to minimize it.

02:24:20.840 --> 02:24:24.353
And the older you get, the
harder it is to change.

02:24:24.353 --> 02:24:26.520
And I keep getting that
thrown back at me, and says,

02:24:26.520 --> 02:24:28.460
you're going to ask
all the old guys that

02:24:28.460 --> 02:24:31.140
are ready to retire that they're
supposed to shift and change?

02:24:31.140 --> 02:24:32.900
And the only answer
you can really get

02:24:32.900 --> 02:24:36.680
is, A, look, there's a lot of
potential for organizations

02:24:36.680 --> 02:24:39.380
to get a lot more confident
in their knowledge work,

02:24:39.380 --> 02:24:40.980
just a lot more.

02:24:40.980 --> 02:24:42.845
And it's going to
take a lot of change.

02:24:42.845 --> 02:24:44.720
And the organizations
that learn how to do it

02:24:44.720 --> 02:24:47.095
are going to be successful,
maybe the only ones that will

02:24:47.095 --> 02:24:48.930
survive, including mankind.

02:24:48.930 --> 02:24:50.700
So look, we've got to get there.

02:24:50.700 --> 02:24:52.500
So what you need
to do is say, look,

02:24:52.500 --> 02:24:53.990
we have to find
a strategy that's

02:24:53.990 --> 02:24:56.585
as smart as possible
for accommodating all

02:24:56.585 --> 02:24:58.670
of the human and
social characteristics

02:24:58.670 --> 02:25:01.580
that change has to
cope with, as well

02:25:01.580 --> 02:25:04.580
as the dynamics and the
expense of changing, et cetera.

02:25:04.580 --> 02:25:06.650
So we have to find a strategy.

02:25:06.650 --> 02:25:11.210
So that's the major push on our
bootstrapping thing, to say,

02:25:11.210 --> 02:25:14.150
yes, it looks like a
very smart thing to do

02:25:14.150 --> 02:25:17.390
is to build an appropriate
improvement infrastructure.

02:25:17.390 --> 02:25:20.540
And then as rapidly as
possible, get the improvements

02:25:20.540 --> 02:25:23.480
and these capabilities so they
can plug into the improvement

02:25:23.480 --> 02:25:24.830
and structure, too.

02:25:24.830 --> 02:25:27.510
So that you're improving
your improvement process,

02:25:27.510 --> 02:25:29.420
maybe even faster
than the other.

02:25:29.420 --> 02:25:32.560
But that just looked like the
only strategy I could think of.

02:25:32.560 --> 02:25:34.380
And there's a practical
way to go after it.

02:25:34.380 --> 02:25:37.070
So strange as it sounds,
that's what we're after.

02:25:40.340 --> 02:25:41.905
MODERATOR: Next question.

02:25:41.905 --> 02:25:42.530
AUDIENCE: Okay.

02:25:42.530 --> 02:25:47.210
I wanted to thank Alan
for his retrospective.

02:25:47.210 --> 02:25:49.620
Oh, I'm Mike Zyda, Naval
Postgraduate School.

02:25:49.620 --> 02:25:52.102
I want to thank Alan for his
retrospective of the '60s.

02:25:52.102 --> 02:25:53.810
And there was an
important slide in there

02:25:53.810 --> 02:25:56.150
that I think got kind
of muddied, which talks

02:25:56.150 --> 02:25:57.920
about the golden age of ARPA.

02:25:57.920 --> 02:25:59.900
And I liked the print
on the bottom, which

02:25:59.900 --> 02:26:04.040
is, fund people not projects.

02:26:04.040 --> 02:26:07.010
Try and point out, actually,
I receive a lot of ARPA money

02:26:07.010 --> 02:26:07.820
right now.

02:26:07.820 --> 02:26:11.990
And ARPA today is very
different from 30 years ago.

02:26:11.990 --> 02:26:16.100
I've been on my own money
for 22 years, so I know this.

02:26:16.100 --> 02:26:21.400
I had an interesting
experience about in June, July

02:26:21.400 --> 02:26:22.820
of this year.

02:26:22.820 --> 02:26:26.870
I received a note from my
ARPA program manager saying,

02:26:26.870 --> 02:26:28.430
we have a new director of ARPA.

02:26:28.430 --> 02:26:31.010
You need to take your proposal
that I've already sent you

02:26:31.010 --> 02:26:34.730
the money for and provide
a three and four word

02:26:34.730 --> 02:26:40.040
title for each task, a
task number for each task,

02:26:40.040 --> 02:26:44.150
a start date for each task,
an end date for each task.

02:26:44.150 --> 02:26:47.540
Were you over budget on
budget or what for each task?

02:26:47.540 --> 02:26:50.990
And the director of ARPA was
going to look at the tasks

02:26:50.990 --> 02:26:53.940
and say which ones he was
going to fund for money

02:26:53.940 --> 02:26:55.080
that was already there.

02:26:55.080 --> 02:27:00.530
And so when I saw your, fund
people not projects, and then

02:27:00.530 --> 02:27:02.750
I'm saying, fund
projects not tasks.

02:27:02.750 --> 02:27:04.271
[LAUGHTER]

02:27:05.610 --> 02:27:07.850
So I'm a little bit
nervous about the way

02:27:07.850 --> 02:27:09.030
we're going right now.

02:27:09.030 --> 02:27:11.360
So this is not an
attack on ARPA.

02:27:11.360 --> 02:27:14.210
ARPA's been extremely
good to me over the years.

02:27:14.210 --> 02:27:17.900
But just the orientation
of the bottom line

02:27:17.900 --> 02:27:19.550
is a little bit out
of whack, I think.

02:27:19.550 --> 02:27:22.110
So I'd like to
hear some comments.

02:27:22.110 --> 02:27:24.680
ENGELBART: That really isn't
just in ARPA, in our industry,

02:27:24.680 --> 02:27:25.380
too.

02:27:25.380 --> 02:27:29.420
It's like, if you can't explain
it to me in five minutes,

02:27:29.420 --> 02:27:31.320
I don't think I should
even consider it,

02:27:31.320 --> 02:27:32.380
somebody will say to you.

02:27:32.380 --> 02:27:36.170
And you say, well, you can't
explain something very novel

02:27:36.170 --> 02:27:37.510
in five minutes.

02:27:37.510 --> 02:27:39.560
And they want the bottom line.

02:27:39.560 --> 02:27:42.667
And you say, I'm convinced
in the general thing.

02:27:42.667 --> 02:27:44.000
There couldn't be anything else.

02:27:44.000 --> 02:27:45.120
You have to get there.

02:27:45.120 --> 02:27:47.960
But that isn't the kind
of thing that sells.

02:27:47.960 --> 02:27:53.100
And so it's sort of some kind
of modern type way to manage.

02:27:53.100 --> 02:27:55.700
But I think it's going to get
our country and our industry

02:27:55.700 --> 02:27:56.720
into real trouble.

02:27:56.720 --> 02:27:58.280
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
this presupposes I

02:27:58.280 --> 02:27:59.822
know what the system
is going to look

02:27:59.822 --> 02:28:02.720
like precisely at the end when
I'm handing in the proposal.

02:28:02.720 --> 02:28:04.345
ENGELBART: Well, it's
the kind of thing

02:28:04.345 --> 02:28:07.070
that can work when you're
making incremental improvements.

02:28:07.070 --> 02:28:09.920
And so that the guy that's
evaluating knows the system

02:28:09.920 --> 02:28:12.680
and knows that, yes, if
you get delivery that much

02:28:12.680 --> 02:28:14.435
faster to the
customer, and that's

02:28:14.435 --> 02:28:16.310
what your bottom line
is, yeah, I can see it.

02:28:16.310 --> 02:28:17.537
Okay, good.

02:28:17.537 --> 02:28:19.120
But it really doesn't
work if you say,

02:28:19.120 --> 02:28:21.350
I got a brand new
material that'll

02:28:21.350 --> 02:28:23.503
make your products
very different.

02:28:23.503 --> 02:28:25.170
And I can't tell you
how much different,

02:28:25.170 --> 02:28:27.500
but you've got to explore it.

02:28:27.500 --> 02:28:31.010
You can't do that kind of sell.

02:28:31.010 --> 02:28:34.130
PRESENTER 3: Yeah, if I could
respond to that briefly.

02:28:34.130 --> 02:28:36.770
I guess I would start by
saying that one person's bug is

02:28:36.770 --> 02:28:38.780
another person's feature, often.

02:28:38.780 --> 02:28:43.790
And that there are different
models of how the system ought

02:28:43.790 --> 02:28:46.460
to work, just as there are
different models of life,

02:28:46.460 --> 02:28:49.730
depending on what stage of
evolution and involvement in it

02:28:49.730 --> 02:28:51.680
you are.

02:28:51.680 --> 02:28:55.340
ARPA [INAUDIBLE], I would say.

02:28:55.340 --> 02:28:57.470
Not to try and defend
any specific action,

02:28:57.470 --> 02:28:59.970
because I haven't been involved
with them for quite a while.

02:28:59.970 --> 02:29:02.830
It's still an
organization of people.

02:29:02.830 --> 02:29:04.820
And people do what people do.

02:29:04.820 --> 02:29:07.607
And if different
people were involved,

02:29:07.607 --> 02:29:09.440
you'd probably have
different actions taken.

02:29:09.440 --> 02:29:12.180
Now, a lot of that takes
place in a context--

02:29:12.180 --> 02:29:15.270
a political context,
budgetary context,

02:29:15.270 --> 02:29:18.600
state of the world
context, and so forth.

02:29:18.600 --> 02:29:21.300
But it seems to me
that they have never

02:29:21.300 --> 02:29:24.870
been more open to really good
people going down and helping

02:29:24.870 --> 02:29:27.990
out as I'm sure other parts
of the government are.

02:29:27.990 --> 02:29:32.140
And if you have the right
kind of view and mentality

02:29:32.140 --> 02:29:34.560
and capability, then
you have the ability

02:29:34.560 --> 02:29:36.310
to actually change things.

02:29:36.310 --> 02:29:38.640
And so rather than
a comment on the way

02:29:38.640 --> 02:29:41.370
it is, I think the
more helpful thing is

02:29:41.370 --> 02:29:42.930
to say, how can
we become involved

02:29:42.930 --> 02:29:46.020
to change it to be more the
way we think it ought to be.

02:29:46.020 --> 02:29:48.280
AUDIENCE: Well, in fact that
was kind of the comment,

02:29:48.280 --> 02:29:50.500
is to generate a little
bit of discussion.

02:29:50.500 --> 02:29:53.010
And perhaps some of
these people in this room

02:29:53.010 --> 02:29:54.780
filter that back that direction.

02:29:54.780 --> 02:29:58.070
I chat with Duane
Adams all the time.

02:29:58.070 --> 02:29:58.980
[INAUDIBLE]

02:29:58.980 --> 02:30:01.960
ENGELBART: I think that you
have to realize, those guys, who

02:30:01.960 --> 02:30:02.670
they're between.

02:30:02.670 --> 02:30:05.100
They have to go to a
new kind of Congress now

02:30:05.100 --> 02:30:08.950
that's very different and
tough on this sort of stuff.

02:30:08.950 --> 02:30:09.700
So they're not

02:30:10.200 --> 02:30:15.780
But the whole general process
of the quick return and the way

02:30:15.780 --> 02:30:16.650
it is an industry.

02:30:16.650 --> 02:30:19.980
If your stock shifts a little
bit in the next three weeks,

02:30:19.980 --> 02:30:20.640
that's bad.

02:30:20.640 --> 02:30:24.360
So you can't do any long-term
changes that may say,

02:30:24.360 --> 02:30:27.940
I'll drop for a while and
I'll make it up really later.

02:30:27.940 --> 02:30:29.810
It's just isn't
allowable to the CEOs.

02:30:33.030 --> 02:30:34.970
AUDIENCE: Okay, [INAUDIBLE]
[? Frankston, ?]

02:30:34.970 --> 02:30:37.080
despite Microsoft.

02:30:37.080 --> 02:30:38.570
I'm not going to defend Windows.

02:30:38.570 --> 02:30:40.790
But what I want to
try to understand

02:30:40.790 --> 02:30:45.500
is why Windows is such
a problem, in that--

02:30:45.500 --> 02:30:47.360
if you have to change
the world all at once

02:30:47.360 --> 02:30:50.660
and you can't coexist with what
exists, you've got a problem.

02:30:50.660 --> 02:30:53.640
The web is a great example
of, independent of Windows,

02:30:53.640 --> 02:30:56.600
it adds great function
and capability.

02:30:56.600 --> 02:30:59.180
And there are lots of examples,
like Andy's Rollerblades.

02:30:59.180 --> 02:31:01.760
People learning AutoCAD
or graphics systems

02:31:01.760 --> 02:31:03.230
and interacting
are great examples

02:31:03.230 --> 02:31:05.270
where people have
decided it's worth

02:31:05.270 --> 02:31:07.190
investing major amounts
of time to learn

02:31:07.190 --> 02:31:08.712
new paradigms and new systems.

02:31:08.712 --> 02:31:10.295
It doesn't mean they
give up the rest.

02:31:13.865 --> 02:31:15.490
What are you real
problems in getting--

02:31:15.490 --> 02:31:18.948
do you really feel like to
change all the world at once,

02:31:18.948 --> 02:31:20.990
or what are the problems
to get people to try out

02:31:20.990 --> 02:31:25.070
pilots or new ideas in
industry or the world at large?

02:31:27.895 --> 02:31:29.270
ENGELBART: The
only way I can see

02:31:29.270 --> 02:31:31.200
is, you have to get
pilots operating.

02:31:31.200 --> 02:31:33.500
And there has to be some
kind of conscious pursuit

02:31:33.500 --> 02:31:37.340
of that future that you can't
really guarantee it as there.

02:31:37.340 --> 02:31:40.100
But to say, look
at the potential.

02:31:40.100 --> 02:31:42.110
AUDIENCE: I was
going to comment--

02:31:42.110 --> 02:31:44.870
Notes was mentioned, and trying
to get people to adopt that

02:31:44.870 --> 02:31:46.420
had similar barriers.

02:31:46.420 --> 02:31:49.220
Again, far from perfect, but
it had a certain utility which

02:31:49.220 --> 02:31:52.020
some people eventually found.

02:31:52.020 --> 02:31:54.036
But I was just interested
in whether there

02:31:54.036 --> 02:31:56.120
are any examples of
really sort of pilots

02:31:56.120 --> 02:31:58.422
in that kind of new systems.

02:32:03.793 --> 02:32:05.210
KAY: I don't think
it's worthwhile

02:32:05.210 --> 02:32:06.680
commenting at length on it.

02:32:06.680 --> 02:32:11.120
But I think my main point
is that I think MS-DOS got

02:32:11.120 --> 02:32:17.930
millions of people used to, as
a normal mode of interaction,

02:32:17.930 --> 02:32:23.000
to something that would have
been barfed at in the mid-'60s.

02:32:23.000 --> 02:32:25.730
But they didn't know that
they could interact better

02:32:25.730 --> 02:32:26.420
with a machine.

02:32:26.420 --> 02:32:28.100
And so it really
set a low standard.

02:32:28.100 --> 02:32:30.740
Just like television
sets a low standard

02:32:30.740 --> 02:32:33.110
if you don't know
what theater can do.

02:32:33.110 --> 02:32:36.050
You're never going to find
out by watching television.

02:32:36.050 --> 02:32:37.350
That's the problem.

02:32:37.350 --> 02:32:40.850
And so it's not that you can't
do other kinds of things.

02:32:40.850 --> 02:32:44.480
But for instance, if
any of them-- let's

02:32:44.480 --> 02:32:46.040
put Apple in there, as well.

02:32:46.040 --> 02:32:49.070
Because from the standpoint
that we're talking about,

02:32:49.070 --> 02:32:50.750
the Apple interface
isn't any better

02:32:50.750 --> 02:32:53.920
than MS-DOS is and Windows.

02:32:53.920 --> 02:32:57.305
They're basically part
and parcel of monolithic--

02:32:57.305 --> 02:32:59.780
I think, for instance, if you
wanted to put an Engelbartian

02:32:59.780 --> 02:33:04.610
scheme in your application,
in the Apple or Windows user

02:33:04.610 --> 02:33:08.300
interface, it's almost
impossible to do.

02:33:08.300 --> 02:33:10.850
Because the theory
of interaction

02:33:10.850 --> 02:33:13.697
is buried so deeply in
there that you can't say,

02:33:13.697 --> 02:33:15.530
okay, I want to interact
in a different way.

02:33:15.530 --> 02:33:20.360
And yet in any reasonable
object-oriented implementation

02:33:20.360 --> 02:33:22.855
of these things, as in fact
there was at Xerox PARC,

02:33:22.855 --> 02:33:24.980
you could sit down and
design your own interactive.

02:33:24.980 --> 02:33:28.290
In fact, NLS, which Doug
probably didn't mention,

02:33:28.290 --> 02:33:30.665
had this interesting thing
where they actually--

02:33:30.665 --> 02:33:32.960
you could drive the user--

02:33:32.960 --> 02:33:35.780
in any part of the system,
you could make up a new user

02:33:35.780 --> 02:33:37.370
interface if you
wanted to experiment.

02:33:37.370 --> 02:33:40.130
They had this grammar-driven--

02:33:40.130 --> 02:33:44.270
so you could put in an
interface grammar yourself

02:33:44.270 --> 02:33:49.280
and use it to drive commands
if you were experimenting.

02:33:49.280 --> 02:33:53.060
So it was what I would
think of as an open system.

02:33:53.060 --> 02:33:56.700
It didn't force the user
to go just the way it did.

02:33:56.700 --> 02:33:59.520
It was actually extensible in
some very interesting ways.

02:33:59.520 --> 02:34:01.970
I probably have never
even shown that in a talk

02:34:01.970 --> 02:34:05.413
because it's more complex
than the other stuff.

02:34:05.413 --> 02:34:06.830
ENGELBART: Yeah,
it's interesting,

02:34:06.830 --> 02:34:10.310
when he was telling us about the
emergence of Smalltalk there.

02:34:10.310 --> 02:34:12.927
Well, what do you think
we've encapsulated that all?

02:34:12.927 --> 02:34:14.510
And now that's going
to [? augment. ?]

02:34:14.510 --> 02:34:18.560
And now we've got a Smalltalk
encapsulation package that

02:34:18.560 --> 02:34:20.600
gives you a very flexible
kind of interfacing

02:34:20.600 --> 02:34:24.230
that you can go conventional if
you want to that you can shift.

02:34:24.230 --> 02:34:27.740
But anyway, I sort of
take my hat off to Alan

02:34:27.740 --> 02:34:32.470
about providing the way
the Smalltalk [INAUDIBLE]

02:34:36.510 --> 02:34:38.060
MODERATOR: Microphone.

02:34:38.060 --> 02:34:40.760
PRESENTER 1: I get to listen
to a number of researchers--

02:34:40.760 --> 02:34:44.110
MODERATOR: Turn the mic on.

02:34:44.110 --> 02:34:46.450
PRESENTER 1: I get to listen
to a number of researchers

02:34:46.450 --> 02:34:50.950
complain that the development
groups or the users in general

02:34:50.950 --> 02:34:53.530
aren't willing to
adopt their ideas.

02:34:53.530 --> 02:34:56.950
Sometimes I ask these people
to look at their own desk

02:34:56.950 --> 02:35:01.780
and ask, how willing
are they to say,

02:35:01.780 --> 02:35:04.540
I want to be the first user
on this floor of bubble memory

02:35:04.540 --> 02:35:07.150
instead of magnetic disk?

02:35:07.150 --> 02:35:09.580
I've just abandoned
the C compiler

02:35:09.580 --> 02:35:11.908
because I just heard
about [? IFIL ?]

02:35:11.908 --> 02:35:14.200
and I think it's really
wonderful and I want to try it.

02:35:14.200 --> 02:35:16.280
I don't hear that very
often from people.

02:35:16.280 --> 02:35:19.090
And they ought to accept the
idea that the rest of the world

02:35:19.090 --> 02:35:21.814
thinks that they're not
different from the rest

02:35:21.814 --> 02:35:22.528
of the world.

02:35:22.528 --> 02:35:23.956
They're thinking the same way.

02:35:29.792 --> 02:35:32.232
AUDIENCE: Samuel [? Ed ?]
Epstein with sensemedia.net.

02:35:32.232 --> 02:35:34.990
Just kind of a setup here
and then the question-- and I

02:35:34.990 --> 02:35:37.870
guess I'm young enough
to not totally believe

02:35:37.870 --> 02:35:40.540
that there isn't any validity
to the conspiracy theory

02:35:40.540 --> 02:35:43.420
that Ted was referring
to the other day.

02:35:43.420 --> 02:35:44.950
And things are
pretty screwed up,

02:35:44.950 --> 02:35:47.500
if you look at what was going
on 20 years ago and even

02:35:47.500 --> 02:35:48.550
a little bit longer.

02:35:48.550 --> 02:35:51.520
And I have to say that I've had
my own experience, like it was

02:35:51.520 --> 02:35:54.130
mentioned earlier today,
about not being able to turn

02:35:54.130 --> 02:35:56.142
in a laser-printed report.

02:35:56.142 --> 02:35:58.600
The excuse that I was given
was because, well, the computer

02:35:58.600 --> 02:35:59.100
wrote this.

02:35:59.100 --> 02:36:01.263
And for this English class,
you have to write this.

02:36:01.263 --> 02:36:03.480
[LAUGHTER]

02:36:03.480 --> 02:36:05.890
And I have to say
that, when we're

02:36:05.890 --> 02:36:08.523
looking at the big
picture here, I find that,

02:36:08.523 --> 02:36:09.940
in my own personal
case and I know

02:36:09.940 --> 02:36:14.050
there's others like me-- with
very few exceptions of actively

02:36:14.050 --> 02:36:18.280
searching out the people that
are up on this stage here

02:36:18.280 --> 02:36:20.650
and others like you, and
finding this knowledge

02:36:20.650 --> 02:36:22.450
and trying to do
something with it.

02:36:22.450 --> 02:36:26.110
The current educational
system in this country

02:36:26.110 --> 02:36:28.390
seems to be doing a
really good job of helping

02:36:28.390 --> 02:36:31.602
to create and accrete these
established self interests,

02:36:31.602 --> 02:36:33.310
which seemed to prevent
the dissemination

02:36:33.310 --> 02:36:34.270
of this information.

02:36:34.270 --> 02:36:37.330
And also seem to
encourage this, well,

02:36:37.330 --> 02:36:38.950
if it takes more
than five minutes

02:36:38.950 --> 02:36:43.290
don't waste your time to
create this environment.

02:36:43.290 --> 02:36:45.280
Do you have any
recommendations or suggestions

02:36:45.280 --> 02:36:48.910
on how we can restructure,
improve, modify,

02:36:48.910 --> 02:36:51.100
the established
educational system

02:36:51.100 --> 02:36:52.990
so that access to the
information that you're

02:36:52.990 --> 02:36:55.810
talking about is
available to people

02:36:55.810 --> 02:36:57.238
that want to do things with it?

02:36:57.238 --> 02:36:58.780
One of the things
that Alan mentioned

02:36:58.780 --> 02:37:02.260
was, he held up in a slide
the book the Molecular Biology

02:37:02.260 --> 02:37:02.760
of the Gene.

02:37:02.760 --> 02:37:03.885
And I find this very funny.

02:37:03.885 --> 02:37:07.630
If I had to name four
books that did it

02:37:07.630 --> 02:37:09.700
for me, that basically
set my career,

02:37:09.700 --> 02:37:12.040
the first one was Computer
Lib/Dream Machines.

02:37:12.040 --> 02:37:14.880
The next one was the
Molecular Biology of the Gene.

02:37:14.880 --> 02:37:16.630
The other two, which
I kind of do together

02:37:16.630 --> 02:37:19.060
were Newman and Sproull
and Foley and van Dam.

02:37:23.340 --> 02:37:25.340
The last one, which I
just read a few years ago,

02:37:25.340 --> 02:37:27.548
which was a really good one,
was The Decline and Fall

02:37:27.548 --> 02:37:30.670
of the American Programmer
by Edward Yourdon.

02:37:30.670 --> 02:37:32.385
And this is also
really good reading.

02:37:32.385 --> 02:37:33.760
But these are the
kinds of things

02:37:33.760 --> 02:37:36.040
that have allowed me to
build up a career on this,

02:37:36.040 --> 02:37:38.620
and other people, too,
often at the expense,

02:37:38.620 --> 02:37:41.200
in spite or maybe despite
of their academic career.

02:37:41.200 --> 02:37:43.090
How can we integrate
these things

02:37:43.090 --> 02:37:48.710
together so that they don't
seem to be so conflicting?

02:37:48.710 --> 02:37:51.620
KAY: Just a quickie is that--

02:37:51.620 --> 02:37:54.230
just looking back, to
me, one of the paradoxes

02:37:54.230 --> 02:37:58.160
is that I think we made a
complete mistake when we were

02:37:58.160 --> 02:38:01.250
doing the interface at PARC.

02:38:01.250 --> 02:38:06.320
Because we assumed that the kids
would need an easy interface,

02:38:06.320 --> 02:38:09.800
because we're going to try and
teach them to program and stuff

02:38:09.800 --> 02:38:10.320
like that.

02:38:10.320 --> 02:38:12.290
But in fact, the
kids are the ones

02:38:12.290 --> 02:38:15.110
that are willing to put
hours into getting really

02:38:15.110 --> 02:38:16.220
expert at things.

02:38:16.220 --> 02:38:17.870
They'll spend hours
shooting baskets.

02:38:17.870 --> 02:38:19.910
They'll spend hours
learning to hit baseballs,

02:38:19.910 --> 02:38:21.470
learning to ride bikes.

02:38:21.470 --> 02:38:23.830
And now on video
games, the amount--

02:38:23.830 --> 02:38:27.350
I have a four-year-old nephew
who is really incredible.

02:38:27.350 --> 02:38:30.560
And he could use
NLS fantastically

02:38:30.560 --> 02:38:32.030
if it were available.

02:38:32.030 --> 02:38:34.820
He would be flying
through that stuff.

02:38:34.820 --> 02:38:39.500
Because his whole thing is
to become part of the system

02:38:39.500 --> 02:38:40.740
he's interacting with.

02:38:40.740 --> 02:38:43.310
And so if I'd had
that perspective,

02:38:43.310 --> 02:38:45.560
I would have designed a
completely different interface

02:38:45.560 --> 02:38:49.190
for the kids, one in which
how you became expert

02:38:49.190 --> 02:38:51.770
was much more apparent
than what I did.

02:38:51.770 --> 02:38:54.445
So I'm sorry for what I did.

02:38:54.445 --> 02:38:56.570
AUDIENCE: Let me rephrase
my question a little bit.

02:38:56.570 --> 02:38:58.570
SPROULL: But wait, wait,
before you rephrase it,

02:38:58.570 --> 02:39:00.620
let me point out--
you're stuck with it--

02:39:00.620 --> 02:39:07.920
that Alan and Papert actually
did do a very wise thing,

02:39:07.920 --> 02:39:10.070
which was to focus on kids.

02:39:10.070 --> 02:39:13.370
Because not only do
they have the time

02:39:13.370 --> 02:39:17.570
to learn weird and strange new
things, they love doing it.

02:39:17.570 --> 02:39:19.250
And that counts for a lot.

02:39:25.550 --> 02:39:34.520
NELSON: The design of media, as
soon as you establish premises,

02:39:34.520 --> 02:39:36.390
you pretty much
lock it on track.

02:39:36.390 --> 02:39:39.320
For example, the
time slot determines

02:39:39.320 --> 02:39:41.810
the system we call television.

02:39:41.810 --> 02:39:44.300
Once you have the time slot
and commercial television,

02:39:44.300 --> 02:39:45.290
the rest is given.

02:39:45.290 --> 02:39:47.120
Forget it.

02:39:47.120 --> 02:39:51.620
Similarly, once you
have the curriculum

02:39:51.620 --> 02:39:56.570
plus the implicit necessity of
keeping the dance cards of all

02:39:56.570 --> 02:39:57.710
those teachers filled--

02:39:57.710 --> 02:40:02.180
in other words, it's a system
for employment of teachers.

02:40:02.180 --> 02:40:05.120
And so each teacher has
to have an excuse for what

02:40:05.120 --> 02:40:06.560
he's doing all the time.

02:40:06.560 --> 02:40:10.100
Then basically you have
the educational system

02:40:10.100 --> 02:40:10.940
as we now know it.

02:40:15.350 --> 02:40:17.000
As I heard your question, Sam--

02:40:17.000 --> 02:40:20.420
and by the way, Sam and I
go back to when he was 12.

02:40:20.420 --> 02:40:23.790
If I heard your question
correctly, you said,

02:40:23.790 --> 02:40:26.990
how can we fix the educational
system as we now know it?

02:40:26.990 --> 02:40:29.810
And of course, I think that's
a contradiction in terms.

02:40:29.810 --> 02:40:33.320
The point is to get rid
of what is in the way,

02:40:33.320 --> 02:40:35.300
and that is the curriculum.

02:40:35.300 --> 02:40:39.560
And that is the guaranteed
employment for teachers.

02:40:39.560 --> 02:40:43.490
Because that essentially is what
is in the way of the students.

02:40:43.490 --> 02:40:46.790
If we could have a system,
for example, where you simply

02:40:46.790 --> 02:40:49.490
say to a number of
14-year-olds-- right now,

02:40:49.490 --> 02:40:51.440
we say to them,
you must sit here

02:40:51.440 --> 02:40:56.180
for four years enduring
the duke of history,

02:40:56.180 --> 02:40:59.960
the mistress of
mathematics, and whatever

02:40:59.960 --> 02:41:01.940
you dislike about
them because they

02:41:01.940 --> 02:41:03.890
will fix your point of view.

02:41:03.890 --> 02:41:06.650
Education is a process of
ruining subjects for you.

02:41:06.650 --> 02:41:08.240
And the last
subject to be ruined

02:41:08.240 --> 02:41:10.080
determines your profession.

02:41:10.080 --> 02:41:12.126
[LAUGHTER]

02:41:14.870 --> 02:41:19.310
And each subject is
personified with the face

02:41:19.310 --> 02:41:22.700
of this local ogre, or
perhaps, in some cases,

02:41:22.700 --> 02:41:26.000
a wondrous person, who then
represents that subject.

02:41:26.000 --> 02:41:28.550
And you never find out that
every subject has something

02:41:28.550 --> 02:41:30.260
in it of interest to you.

02:41:30.260 --> 02:41:33.890
Every subject can be something
that you take to your heart.

02:41:33.890 --> 02:41:36.680
I thought I hated history, I
thought I hated mathematics,

02:41:36.680 --> 02:41:39.120
because of the
people I encountered.

02:41:39.120 --> 02:41:42.167
And so some other
way around, I would

02:41:42.167 --> 02:41:44.000
like to see a school
system where you simply

02:41:44.000 --> 02:41:45.830
say to the kids at
14, not, you have

02:41:45.830 --> 02:41:47.930
to sit here for
four years enduring

02:41:47.930 --> 02:41:52.620
and enduring and enduring and
being endangered and insulted.

02:41:52.620 --> 02:41:58.970
But rather, you
can get out of here

02:41:58.970 --> 02:42:02.480
as soon as you present
for examination any

02:42:02.480 --> 02:42:06.260
of these 80 mini courses, any
80 of these 1,000 mini courses

02:42:06.260 --> 02:42:07.880
on this sheet.

02:42:07.880 --> 02:42:10.610
You can take more if
you want and learn more

02:42:10.610 --> 02:42:13.070
before we send you out there.

02:42:13.070 --> 02:42:14.320
Have your choice.

02:42:14.320 --> 02:42:16.070
Then I think we would
see real motivation.

02:42:16.070 --> 02:42:17.570
Because right now,
there is no way

02:42:17.570 --> 02:42:20.480
students can
exercise initiative,

02:42:20.480 --> 02:42:24.260
except A, if they're
male disobeying,

02:42:24.260 --> 02:42:29.690
B, if they're female
getting pregnant,

02:42:29.690 --> 02:42:33.200
and C, totally
obeying and outdoing

02:42:33.200 --> 02:42:35.960
whatever the teacher wants,
which is done by very few.

02:42:35.960 --> 02:42:39.480
Whereas, all systems
are based on initiative.

02:42:39.480 --> 02:42:41.990
And if we give people a
way to learn by initiative,

02:42:41.990 --> 02:42:43.280
then we'd fix the problem.

02:42:43.280 --> 02:42:44.500
But nobody wants to do that.

02:42:44.500 --> 02:42:47.000
AUDIENCE: Yeah, that was what
my question was more directed,

02:42:47.000 --> 02:42:50.180
not at the technology, but at
the current system, and how can

02:42:50.180 --> 02:42:53.000
we make it more conducive
to bringing people online

02:42:53.000 --> 02:42:54.830
and getting the kids involved.

02:42:54.830 --> 02:42:57.420
KAY: It's like,
cops need criminals.

02:42:57.420 --> 02:42:58.060
so.

02:42:58.060 --> 02:43:00.680
It's a very complex thing.

02:43:00.680 --> 02:43:03.470
Seymour and I were in
Washington yesterday

02:43:03.470 --> 02:43:06.380
morning talking to two
congressional committees

02:43:06.380 --> 02:43:08.450
about the justice problem.

02:43:08.450 --> 02:43:10.820
And of course, both of us
were conscious of the irony

02:43:10.820 --> 02:43:15.710
that we were actually talking to
the people who were in business

02:43:15.710 --> 02:43:17.520
to stay in business.

02:43:17.520 --> 02:43:21.872
And so the interest in
changing things in any big way

02:43:21.872 --> 02:43:23.330
was something that's
very, very far

02:43:23.330 --> 02:43:27.240
from what they're trying to do.

02:43:27.240 --> 02:43:30.050
For instance, the
average expenditure

02:43:30.050 --> 02:43:35.000
for children in the US
is about $6,500 a year.

02:43:35.000 --> 02:43:36.950
If you multiply that
out on a classroom,

02:43:36.950 --> 02:43:40.340
you get to around $185,000
to $200,000 a classroom.

02:43:40.340 --> 02:43:42.500
Well, those classrooms
are not receiving

02:43:42.500 --> 02:43:46.190
anywhere near $185,000
or $200,000 a classroom.

02:43:46.190 --> 02:43:50.705
From 1/2 to 3/4 is
soaked up by bureaucracy.

02:43:50.705 --> 02:43:53.960
It never gets down to the school
or the kids or anything else.

02:43:53.960 --> 02:43:57.770
And I think that is
an enormous problem.

02:43:57.770 --> 02:44:00.360
And basically we said
exactly what you are saying.

02:44:00.360 --> 02:44:03.210
The problem is that
there is a curriculum.

02:44:03.210 --> 02:44:06.050
And we invoke the
name of Montessori,

02:44:06.050 --> 02:44:09.080
whose idea was that
school should always

02:44:09.080 --> 02:44:11.800
be an extended kindergarten.

02:44:11.800 --> 02:44:14.300
And it's the job of the people
who designed the kindergarten

02:44:14.300 --> 02:44:16.970
to make what happens
when you use it

02:44:16.970 --> 02:44:20.270
for your own reasons
more interesting

02:44:20.270 --> 02:44:22.560
than the regular world is.

02:44:22.560 --> 02:44:24.020
And that's what Montessori did.

02:44:24.020 --> 02:44:25.978
And I think that's an
excellent way of thinking

02:44:25.978 --> 02:44:27.900
of designing a learning system.

02:44:27.900 --> 02:44:30.090
NELSON: Do you know what
curriculum means in Latin?

02:44:30.090 --> 02:44:30.680
KAY: Yes.

02:44:30.680 --> 02:44:33.448
NELSON: Little racetrack.

02:44:33.448 --> 02:44:35.180
PRESENTER 2: Ted,
you suggest basically

02:44:35.180 --> 02:44:37.280
breaking the curriculum
up into small pieces.

02:44:37.280 --> 02:44:39.500
And the Montessori
system, it allows

02:44:39.500 --> 02:44:42.355
you to do whatever you like, so
long as, whichever Montessori

02:44:42.355 --> 02:44:44.480
school, you use the same
colors for the same length

02:44:44.480 --> 02:44:45.590
for the same blocks.

02:44:45.590 --> 02:44:48.500
So you're put within a very
constrained world there.

02:44:48.500 --> 02:44:51.300
And I think one of the things
I took out of your question,

02:44:51.300 --> 02:44:53.630
which was clearly
a good question,

02:44:53.630 --> 02:44:57.268
is, there are four books
which, for you, could

02:44:57.268 --> 02:44:58.310
have been your education.

02:44:58.310 --> 02:45:00.650
Given a computer, the
network, and those four books,

02:45:00.650 --> 02:45:02.120
you would have been happy.

02:45:02.120 --> 02:45:03.230
And you would have
achieved a lot of things

02:45:03.230 --> 02:45:04.520
and wasted a lot less time.

02:45:04.520 --> 02:45:05.120
AUDIENCE: And I was.

02:45:05.120 --> 02:45:06.380
PRESENTER 2: Right, you were.

02:45:06.380 --> 02:45:07.422
You managed to find them.

02:45:07.422 --> 02:45:10.130
Question is, how can
other people find them?

02:45:10.130 --> 02:45:12.080
For you, well, one of
the things you can say,

02:45:12.080 --> 02:45:14.430
that should be a curriculum.

02:45:14.430 --> 02:45:18.450
The reason that I feel that
the curriculum won't go away--

02:45:18.450 --> 02:45:19.940
there's a neat
television program

02:45:19.940 --> 02:45:23.660
in which the BBC took
four poor scientists who

02:45:23.660 --> 02:45:26.450
had been denied acceptance
by the academic community.

02:45:26.450 --> 02:45:27.980
Their papers hadn't
been published.

02:45:27.980 --> 02:45:30.740
These beautiful ideas, wonderful
ideas were just outside.

02:45:30.740 --> 02:45:33.605
They were just too
different and interesting.

02:45:33.605 --> 02:45:35.480
And what they did, in
fact, was to show that,

02:45:35.480 --> 02:45:38.330
by the end of the program,
that they were also total junk.

02:45:38.330 --> 02:45:41.700
So the problem is that if you
don't have the curriculum,

02:45:41.700 --> 02:45:44.767
then you leave somebody-- it's
like throwing somebody out

02:45:44.767 --> 02:45:45.350
straight away.

02:45:45.350 --> 02:45:50.210
You say, right, guys, you're
here to be free, to learn.

02:45:50.210 --> 02:45:51.200
And here's the net.

02:45:51.200 --> 02:45:53.510
And basically, they might
as well do that at home.

02:45:53.510 --> 02:45:55.850
So you're really asking is
there a case from education

02:45:55.850 --> 02:45:57.225
in the sense of
leading somebody.

02:45:57.225 --> 02:45:59.288
Do you want to lead somebody?

02:45:59.288 --> 02:46:00.830
Is there a sense
for having something

02:46:00.830 --> 02:46:04.850
which is established as
being reasonable for people

02:46:04.850 --> 02:46:07.640
to spend their formative
years studying.

02:46:07.640 --> 02:46:09.590
And your problem
is that it takes

02:46:09.590 --> 02:46:11.870
too long to get really
good ideas into that.

02:46:11.870 --> 02:46:14.870
Now people are looking back at
things that Ted said, and said,

02:46:14.870 --> 02:46:15.740
hey, that's cool.

02:46:15.740 --> 02:46:16.850
That should be taught
really early on.

02:46:16.850 --> 02:46:18.630
That should happen
sort of first grade.

02:46:18.630 --> 02:46:21.035
But it's taken a long time
for people to do that.

02:46:21.035 --> 02:46:22.910
And that's because it's
a filtration process.

02:46:22.910 --> 02:46:24.780
That if you leave people to
look at all the information,

02:46:24.780 --> 02:46:25.970
you also want to
lead them and say,

02:46:25.970 --> 02:46:27.380
hey, this is really interesting.

02:46:27.380 --> 02:46:30.170
And sometimes it seems that
the situations we've got,

02:46:30.170 --> 02:46:33.110
just that the mechanism
we have for filtering

02:46:33.110 --> 02:46:35.677
this and reviewing it
just takes generations.

02:46:35.677 --> 02:46:37.260
Now, my only suggestion
if we're going

02:46:37.260 --> 02:46:40.070
to change that filtrating
system, in some cases,

02:46:40.070 --> 02:46:42.890
for example with computing, we
can move to a biological model.

02:46:42.890 --> 02:46:46.232
So you can leave all your
programming people around.

02:46:46.232 --> 02:46:47.690
And you let people
pursue projects.

02:46:47.690 --> 02:46:49.480
And hey, you give
them CPU power,

02:46:49.480 --> 02:46:51.605
and if they make something
which demonstrably works

02:46:51.605 --> 02:46:53.170
and bubbles and
enthusiast people.

02:46:53.170 --> 02:46:54.962
And now you've got the
net you can actually

02:46:54.962 --> 02:46:56.690
use biological
methods, in some cases.

02:46:56.690 --> 02:46:58.440
You can't do it for
learning good history.

02:46:58.440 --> 02:47:01.880
But in some cases, you
can use biological methods

02:47:01.880 --> 02:47:05.840
and get a faster turnaround,
because you can use computing

02:47:05.840 --> 02:47:08.270
and a whole lot of
people out there

02:47:08.270 --> 02:47:12.040
who will participate during
the hours of darkness

02:47:12.040 --> 02:47:14.780
to try these things out.

02:47:14.780 --> 02:47:17.960
ENGELBART: It just occurred to
me that giving its equal time

02:47:17.960 --> 02:47:18.740
is not fair.

02:47:18.740 --> 02:47:21.848
Because in the same amount of
time, he can say so much more.

02:47:21.848 --> 02:47:22.844
[LAUGHTER]

02:47:22.844 --> 02:47:25.995
KAY: That was the
problem with Butler.

02:47:25.995 --> 02:47:27.820
PRESENTER 2: So many
words, so little time.

02:47:27.820 --> 02:47:30.320
ENGELBART: I just want to say
that all these things are very

02:47:30.320 --> 02:47:32.840
relevant to look out about
how you educate children

02:47:32.840 --> 02:47:35.660
and that they can learn
more and differently.

02:47:35.660 --> 02:47:38.758
But the thing is, that the
institution of education

02:47:38.758 --> 02:47:40.550
does not like to be
changed by the children

02:47:40.550 --> 02:47:43.790
to be changed by the people that
are there in the current state

02:47:43.790 --> 02:47:44.990
in order to change it.

02:47:44.990 --> 02:47:47.240
And we can't wait
for the 20 years

02:47:47.240 --> 02:47:50.300
or so till those children are
out integrated into society

02:47:50.300 --> 02:47:53.090
where they have the roles that
they can start making changes

02:47:53.090 --> 02:47:57.650
that change driving things
about both world events, et

02:47:57.650 --> 02:48:00.200
cetera, and the technology
are moving too fast.

02:48:00.200 --> 02:48:02.210
So we're just
faced with the fact

02:48:02.210 --> 02:48:05.930
we have to learn a better
way to shift organizations

02:48:05.930 --> 02:48:08.030
with the people that
are in them now.

02:48:08.030 --> 02:48:11.630
So that's really why you
need a strategy for it.

02:48:11.630 --> 02:48:14.650
And it's exciting to think of
what the children can become

02:48:14.650 --> 02:48:17.060
and to flourish, et cetera.

02:48:17.060 --> 02:48:20.630
But the daunting problem that
I think we really have to face

02:48:20.630 --> 02:48:24.837
is, how do you deal with the
change of the adult world?

02:48:24.837 --> 02:48:25.670
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

02:48:25.670 --> 02:48:29.518
And also, thanks for
the last 20 years, guys.

02:48:29.518 --> 02:48:31.310
AUDIENCE: My name is
Ricky Goldman-Segall--

02:48:31.310 --> 02:48:33.260
I've met some of
you already before--

02:48:33.260 --> 02:48:35.100
from the University
of British Columbia,

02:48:35.100 --> 02:48:37.010
the faculty of Education.

02:48:37.010 --> 02:48:40.160
But I'm not going to pursue
the issue of education,

02:48:40.160 --> 02:48:43.480
because I so vehemently
disagree with some of the things

02:48:43.480 --> 02:48:44.480
that you just said, Ted.

02:48:44.480 --> 02:48:48.350
But you've heard my
story before with that.

02:48:48.350 --> 02:48:51.770
Well, I remind you is
that, A, how many of us--

02:48:51.770 --> 02:48:53.070
I guess, raise your hands.

02:48:53.070 --> 02:48:54.528
How many of us
would have been here

02:48:54.528 --> 02:48:58.040
if it weren't for one fabulous
or several fabulous people

02:48:58.040 --> 02:49:00.110
in your life who
inspired you, and were

02:49:00.110 --> 02:49:02.210
those people your
teachers, if they were?

02:49:02.210 --> 02:49:03.380
I know they were for me.

02:49:03.380 --> 02:49:03.770
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

02:49:03.770 --> 02:49:04.937
AUDIENCE: Some of them were.

02:49:04.937 --> 02:49:07.280
AUDIENCE: Some were.

02:49:07.280 --> 02:49:09.890
AUDIENCE: Well, it's
not just the teachers,

02:49:09.890 --> 02:49:11.787
but they had to perform
within a system.

02:49:11.787 --> 02:49:13.370
NELSON: You mean
designated teachers--

02:49:13.370 --> 02:49:14.610
a very important point.

02:49:14.610 --> 02:49:15.830
AUDIENCE: Well, perhaps.

02:49:15.830 --> 02:49:20.450
I'm going to respond
to Lee Sproulls notion

02:49:20.450 --> 02:49:23.390
that science or
scientific investigation

02:49:23.390 --> 02:49:29.180
is this relational and
interactive thing that actually

02:49:29.180 --> 02:49:31.850
could happen in the
schools much more.

02:49:31.850 --> 02:49:34.730
And I'm going to do that in
a way as a sort of summation,

02:49:34.730 --> 02:49:38.300
perhaps, as well, because we're
at the end of the conference.

02:49:38.300 --> 02:49:41.600
And what I'd like to
know is what each of you

02:49:41.600 --> 02:49:44.870
learned from each other that
you didn't know before you

02:49:44.870 --> 02:49:49.550
got here, and in some sense
what you think your biggest

02:49:49.550 --> 02:49:51.570
similarities are.

02:49:51.570 --> 02:49:55.160
But also, what do you think
your biggest differences are?

02:49:55.160 --> 02:49:57.740
Because I think within
that, we sort of know

02:49:57.740 --> 02:50:01.310
where some aspects of
the field are moving.

02:50:01.310 --> 02:50:05.864
So I guess that's my question.

02:50:05.864 --> 02:50:08.290
MODERATOR: We'll
start [? with you. ?]

02:50:08.290 --> 02:50:09.200
KAY: Yes.

02:50:09.200 --> 02:50:11.040
[LAUGHTER]

02:50:14.270 --> 02:50:17.030
SPROULL: I learned that
Ted Nelson and I are alike

02:50:17.030 --> 02:50:20.450
because we both
have purple socks.

02:50:20.450 --> 02:50:24.020
I'm not wearing mine,
but he's wearing his.

02:50:24.020 --> 02:50:28.580
And I learned that
Alan Kay actually

02:50:28.580 --> 02:50:33.020
did believe that kids
would be working together

02:50:33.020 --> 02:50:34.790
using their Dynabooks
over the network.

02:50:34.790 --> 02:50:35.990
And I apologize, Alan.

02:50:35.990 --> 02:50:39.800
I should have known that you
would have believed that.

02:50:39.800 --> 02:50:41.480
I don't want to
be only facetious,

02:50:41.480 --> 02:50:45.400
but that's a rather difficult
question to deal with.

02:50:45.400 --> 02:50:50.960
So I think that
most importantly, I

02:50:50.960 --> 02:50:54.860
wouldn't say I learned sort
of bits of information.

02:50:54.860 --> 02:50:59.930
I experienced, I
guess, a reaffirmation

02:50:59.930 --> 02:51:04.550
of the importance of the
ideas and the importance

02:51:04.550 --> 02:51:11.000
of the scientists who
have been pursuing them,

02:51:11.000 --> 02:51:14.150
sometimes with reward and
honor and sometimes without.

02:51:14.150 --> 02:51:15.710
But that didn't
matter because it's

02:51:15.710 --> 02:51:19.230
the ideas that were important
and continue to be important.

02:51:19.230 --> 02:51:23.480
And so what was most important
for me was the reaffirmation

02:51:23.480 --> 02:51:24.440
of that community.

02:51:28.160 --> 02:51:30.800
NELSON: Well, I learned
that everybody here is

02:51:30.800 --> 02:51:33.770
more idealistic than I thought.

02:51:33.770 --> 02:51:38.937
Well, no, some people
couldn't be more idealistic.

02:51:38.937 --> 02:51:41.270
KAY: I'm just glad to see all
these old characters still

02:51:41.270 --> 02:51:42.394
hanging in there.

02:51:42.394 --> 02:51:44.250
[LAUGHTER]

02:51:45.180 --> 02:51:49.220
PRESENTER 2: I guess one
thing, which actually Vint Cerf

02:51:49.220 --> 02:51:53.630
pointed out at a similar
sort of mix of people,

02:51:53.630 --> 02:51:56.600
said that one thing that he
pointed out everybody had

02:51:56.600 --> 02:52:01.850
in common was
persistence, having ideas,

02:52:01.850 --> 02:52:04.340
and even though people
telling you not to do it,

02:52:04.340 --> 02:52:06.718
hanging onto it and
being really stubborn.

02:52:06.718 --> 02:52:08.510
And doing it even though
you're told not to

02:52:08.510 --> 02:52:11.160
and even though nobody's giving
you money to do it and what

02:52:11.160 --> 02:52:11.660
have you.

02:52:11.660 --> 02:52:15.123
That seems to be something which
everybody has had in common.

02:52:15.123 --> 02:52:17.290
I don't know whether it's
something intrinsic to us.

02:52:17.290 --> 02:52:22.400
Or it's just that our ideas,
which once they've got you,

02:52:22.400 --> 02:52:24.200
they don't let go.

02:52:24.200 --> 02:52:29.180
Apart from that, I guess it's
pretty amazingly heterogeneous.

02:52:29.180 --> 02:52:31.760
And the differences
are part of sort of--

02:52:31.760 --> 02:52:34.510
and everything else.

02:52:34.510 --> 02:52:35.150
Which is good.

02:52:37.640 --> 02:52:39.140
ENGELBART: What am
I supposed to do?

02:52:43.140 --> 02:52:47.030
If it's to say goodbye, well,
I enjoyed it thoroughly.

02:52:47.030 --> 02:52:51.530
And it opens my eyes
to something that--

02:52:51.530 --> 02:52:52.920
it was almost embarrassing.

02:52:52.920 --> 02:52:58.220
It's that I sort of lost
track of a lot of things that

02:52:58.220 --> 02:53:00.050
are going on that are
important and that I

02:53:00.050 --> 02:53:01.280
wish I could keep track of.

02:53:01.280 --> 02:53:04.070
So maybe I'll turn over
a new leaf or something,

02:53:04.070 --> 02:53:06.440
or redivide my time differently.

02:53:06.440 --> 02:53:07.850
But anyway, I
heard those remarks

02:53:07.850 --> 02:53:10.290
about idealistic old farts.

02:53:10.290 --> 02:53:13.020
And I thought, okay, that's
why I have to say something.

02:53:13.020 --> 02:53:14.000
[LAUGHTER]

02:53:14.000 --> 02:53:16.450
Anyway, thank you
for the meeting.

02:53:20.932 --> 02:53:22.890
PRESENTER 1: There's
nothing to say after that.

02:53:22.890 --> 02:53:24.900
I guess what I
thought I learned is,

02:53:24.900 --> 02:53:30.960
once again, there's people who
are laying out broad visions,

02:53:30.960 --> 02:53:33.600
and there are engineers
who are building guts.

02:53:33.600 --> 02:53:36.930
And I'm led back to my
preference for the engineers.

02:53:36.930 --> 02:53:38.777
And I'm glad to see
that Bush was one.

02:53:38.777 --> 02:53:40.860
And I realize this is one
of the great engineering

02:53:40.860 --> 02:53:42.960
institutions of the
world, and I thank it

02:53:42.960 --> 02:53:45.185
for running the meeting.

02:53:45.185 --> 02:53:47.610
MODERATOR: Sounds like a
good note to finish up on.

02:53:47.610 --> 02:53:51.530
I'd like to thank our wonderful
speakers and the audience

02:53:51.530 --> 02:53:54.500
for hanging in.

02:53:54.500 --> 02:53:55.940
Sorry about that.

02:53:55.940 --> 02:53:56.990
Sorry, Robert.

02:53:56.990 --> 02:53:59.210
I'll promise to do better.

02:53:59.210 --> 02:54:01.380
Oh, boy, don't like
to cross that guy.

02:54:01.380 --> 02:54:03.220
[LAUGHTER]

02:54:03.220 --> 02:54:06.560
I want to thank our wonderful
speakers and wonderful audience

02:54:06.560 --> 02:54:10.800
for a really amazing
event in my life.

02:54:10.800 --> 02:54:16.010
And I'd like to
invite any of you who

02:54:16.010 --> 02:54:22.070
would like to hear Ted do a
reprise of his wonderful talk

02:54:22.070 --> 02:54:23.600
of yesterday.

02:54:23.600 --> 02:54:26.240
And for the rest of you,
godspeed, and see you

02:54:26.240 --> 02:54:27.410
at the next one.

02:54:27.410 --> 02:54:30.160
[APPLAUSE]

